        <p>Practical philanthropy has an excellent example in the person of Mr. A. Brooke, of Brooke, Bontl,and Co., Limited, one of the new Surrey magistrates, who has, we are told,given an invitation to cclists to uNe a delightful little caravan which has its pitch in his pleasant grounds, and to enjoy a week end, or a few hours at least, amid the Surrey bids. His estate—Leylands—is six miles west of liorking, and about one mile from Leith I-hill. Tile first to respond to this open challenge of hospitality were lady medical students, who walk the Royal Free Hospital for Women. They, indeed, felt they had found "A home from home" when their curious eyes lighted on even a thermometer and a little medicine chest, so complete is the equipment of the little caravan.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Brooke are watching the expeti- rnent with great interest, and so, 1 should say, are the cyclists. They do much for their caravan visitors. Their tennis courts and golf liziks may be used ; their fruit gardens may be " visited " ; and once, when there was a failure in the commissariat, of the carax an occupants, they suggested that their own kitchen should furnish the feast. Mr. Brooke is a staunch Liberal, though, despite the efforts of friends, he is not a Parliamentary aspirant.</p>
        <p>Are we, or are we not, this great British nation, descended from the Ten Lost Tribes ? Is Her Majesty the Queen a descendant of King David ? These are portentous questions, and should not be dismissed lightly. At any rate it is a fact that our gracious Sovereign possesses a family tree in which her Jewish lineage and the Je.ivish lineage of the British people are shown ; which perhaps ahe treasures as a proof of human credulity. At the bottom of the tree is the name of David, at the toP that of Victoria. But if you are inclined to deem t: is document conclusive you will not have the august countenance of Her Majesty—who locks upon the whole affair as a hoax, or what would he a hoax in a less exalted sphere.</p>
        <p>Mark Twain's little daughter Clara is evidently what the Yankees call very" bright," for she hAs given the world another funny story which is worth repeating. Her father is now over in this country with Clamand a friend of theirs has informed the world that Miss Clara doesn't think much of her 6‘ popper" in his professional canacitzt as champion American humorist. Asked the other day Omit one of her father's books this delightful young lady replied, Really, I can't give an opinion. Papa's books bore me terribly, I haven't read half of them. Papa is the nicest thing in the world as papa, but, oh dear, t du wish he was not a famous funny man." This •frank confession has tickled Mark Twain so tremendously that he now goes about tell- ing it to everybody, following up this bonne bouchee with another story against himself: "For years I have derived pleasure from the know- ledge that my works were almost the only thing which Mr. Darwin read during the last years of his life. Now that pleasure has been taken away, for I am told that during the period in question Mr. Darwin suffered from a kind of mental atrophy, and was forbidden to read anything but absolute drivel." •</p>
        <p>That Flemish caterer who served up wild pig at the Antwerp banquet to the Lord Mayor of London and called it a haunch of venison must have hen a man of daring resource. A London Lord Mayor, having eaten his way up through hundreds of city banquets to the highest municipal dignity of the .countvy, ought by this time to know the flavour of venison, and should be above the suspicion of not being able to distinguish it from pork. Perhaps some kind interviewer will induce his lordship to unfold his thoughts as he brooded on the fl tvour And appeararice of Belgian venison. If he recog- nised the character of the wild pig in it, he was of course too well bred a man to say so. Aristides. however, is just. The so-called venison was eaten •out of season, when the sale of game is punishable in Belgium by law. So the Public Prosecutor brought an action against the caterer, who con- fessed that. he had served up wild pig as venison Even this did not allow him to escape, for he was fined 50 francs for decepti,n. But was it. really wild pig after all? Might it not in reality be veni- son, and might not the ingenious caterer have re- Aorted to the ruse of calling it pig to escape the heavier penalty attached to admitting it to be venison?</p>
        <p>From Hungary comes the report of the death of S very interesting woman, Frau Molnar, who foucht in man's dress in the war of Hungarian lndepend. trice. Her maiden name was Anna Szilagyi, and she was quite a girl when the revolution broke out. She joined the army fighting tor indept.,nti.. ence, and her disguise was so complete that 11.- fought in several battles without being discovered 'The secret came out owing to her getting severely wounded, but before her sex was known she had succeeded in attaining the rank of Quartermaster in the army. After the war had ended she married Joseph Molnar, lieutenant of the company in which she had served, and settled down to live quietly at ilaab.</p>
        <p>The contatants for Mr. Gladstone' seat in Mid- lothian at the:_next eletnion are now finally decided Tapon. Sir Jams Carmichael, the Liberal, is to fight Viscount I:o4lrYmplei a son of the Earl of Stair. The Dalqmples have been connected with Midlothian for a-very long time, and Sir James Carmichael is also a popular local man. 'the Viscount will, how- '-ever, have, as Col. Wauchope had, the full weight of the Church on big side, and in Midlothian th, 'Church is very powerful. Although,. there is no Liberal morning paper in Edinburgh now. there -ought to be little difficulty in keeping the seg.</p>
        <p>• . Captain .-laisk Crawford, the "post Scout," *hose .visit to Mr, Genre IL Sinis that: gentleman Po picturesquely described recently in." Mustard and. • Cress." has come over here to try•and prove himself. -entitled to a great part of the •.bist,Wallace estate, mow held by the-State and City, orNew York, arid the property is estimated to be worth four million pounds. The trip. to • Scotland :Was, undertaken thp 3,lvice of. f4tew York lawvers, who believe that he rally there•flud links of evidence which will eubstantiate hill- claim. •</p>
        <p>The Empreis Eugenie recently took Prince Napo- leon with her to Windsor to present him to the Queen as her heir—an impressive fact when tbs. ex‘naistress of the Tuileries' fortune is considered. Most of her wealth is in good gnglish stocks, too. It is bring told of Ettg6nie that on a late visit to Paris, she went "incog" to a fashionable palmist. to have hetfortune read. As part of the necromancer', art is.not to see his fair penitents, she had to put her hand through a slit in a screen. After quite a cursory examination, the fortune-teller said : "Madame, your hand is so extraordinary that one of two things must, be the truth either my ski!) must be at fault for once, and I see impossible owents, or you must. be the Empress Eugenie. for no other band could tell of such strange vicissi- tudes."</p>
        <p>• The latest outcome - of the enterpriP of the most enterprising of Colonial Gov- •ernments, New Zealand, is the despatch of an t-xpert in woods to this country, to 'call the attention of people here to the product of the , colony in this way. I saw the expert in question on Tuesday, and must say that his specimens are 410gle of the finest I have ever seen. They are all in the rough, but the grain and colours are wonder- fully good. and the figuring is splendid.. This is particularly the case with the New Zealand honey- -suckle,wnich shows varied figures, though cut from the same "flitch." Other woods are tqually tirw, -and the prices are very low. AS a matter of fact, the colonists .were quite unaware that they had •such a source of wealth at their doors, and have 'been wasting it in order to make way for oats. The ,distinguishing characteristic is the satinwood tex- ture of all the woods of the colony, and the readi- with which they can be cleaned. One wood, that •of the silver pine, is likely to he extensively used -for wood paper. This is one of the instances where the:lmperial Institute has proved useful, the ex- hibits there having attracted tha at tentiqn,of those intere,ted in the obtaining of handsome woods for -eabin.t work,and induced the Colonial Government to embark,,upon its present enterprise.</p>
        <p>Few men now living can boast of a more varied and rompntic career than Ciptain Jack Crawford.</p>
        <p>'1 tsk: SECRE OF BEAU IV is found m CuTicutta SoAe, the mrst effective skin purifying and heautii3irii soap in the world, as well as the purest and sweetest 'or iniiet and nursery. It is rho only preventive of pimples, blarlacar's. red, rough, and oily skin, red, rough hands with shawless nail, dry, thin, and falling hair, and simple baby blemishes, bec;iuse the only preventive of flammatigm and elrwg,ing of the pores, the cause of nor affections of the =kin, scalp, and hair. Sate ynnator than the ermbin d a'es, of all other skin and gorupleatou soaps.</p>
        <p>When a boy in his teens he worked as a slate- picker in the coal mines of Pennsylvania. After the outbreak of the civil war, while still a boy, he ran away from home in order to enlist in the Union ranks. Soon after his enlistment he was seriously wounded, and while in the hospital he was taught his letters by the Sisters of Charity who attended nim. After the close of the war he went to the Far West and engaged in the United States service. His bravery as a scout caused him to be promoted to the position of chief of scouts,in which capacity he led the Black Hills Rangers at the opening of that country.</p>
        <p>Captain Jack was with General Crook through the Sitting Bull campaign of 1876. He also carried the "New York Herald" despatches from Slim Buttes to Fort Laramie, a distance of three bun- hundred and fifty miles, making the ride through a country peopled with hostile Indians in less than three and a half days. In this ride he killed two horses. From 1870 to 1881 he was chief of scouts under Generals hatch and Buell in the Victoria' campaign, during which over five hundred men, women, and children were slaughtered by the In- dians.</p>
        <p>OVER THREE THOUSAND PER CENT</p>
        <p>"Loans from five shillings to one pound granted im- mediately on own security." That was the announce- ment, emblazoned in gold letters on a first-floor window in a S.E. district, which caught the writer's eye. Being paturally inquisitive, we mounted the stairs, entered the office, and alter a query or two on things in general we a%ked the manager for information as to what class his customers belonged. "Oh, mostly men working on their own account," he replied civilly. "We do about three hundred loans a week, but it is very seldom we lend a sovereign to one person; nine out of ten of our borrowers only want five shilling at a time. What do they want it for? Why, mostly to replenish their stock. A man perhaps buys a quantity of goods and finds that to get rid of his ' bargain 'he must sell at a loss. Next day he has not 6nough to buy a fresh lot, then he comes to us. Another man, perhaps, may like a drop of drink, and when once started never stops until he has spent all his takings, profit and stock money. We enable him to make a fresh start. And there is one thing about our customers." went on our informant. "They make every effort to repay the loans we advance. As a matter of fact, during the last three years our bad customers— those who have not repaid us—number less than a hun- dred. We lend the money for a week, you know, but the large majority of our advances are made on Fridays and repaid either Saturday night—when we keep open till twelve—or Monday morning. Of course, they pay a week's interest just the same. What interest is charged? One penny a week for every shilling; so that, with a penny for the card, a loan of five shillings costs the borrower sixpence." We then left, and making a mental calcallation, discovered that a man who borrows five shillings on Friday and repays on Saturday is made to pay by way of interest at the rate of three thousand six undred per cent. per annum. Truly this was a philan- thropic institution !—" Cassell's Saturday Journal."</p>
