        <p>M ONON'S NURSERY BOON (Unequalled for DE ,TIMICING NITS, and PARASITBS . in the HUMAN HAIR.)</p>
        <p>OF CHEMSTS and the PROPRIETOR, J. L. Al 0 X 0 N, 252, PARK-ROAD. LIVERPOOL. 6d. and Is. per box. Post Free V, and is. .I)l,Are tret thii this pier.</p>
        <p>Ills lordship. •• rintiSrbe off it eight to catch the train. Good-night, Giralda. Good-night and good- by." k He pressed her hand warmly. "I shall be up to take breakfast with you, if I am not ill," said Giralda, shyly. "Good-night, my lord. Good-night—PauL" She moved slowly from him, the housekeeper being ready for departure. The young lord flew to the door and held it open until she had passed out, and then he flung himself on the couch she had quitted, and lived over again the experiences of the evening, recalling her every look and word, her very tones, even, and blamed himself for hav- ingbeen too precipitate in his avowals and won- dering if he had frightened her; and it were possible that she really returned his love. While he was thus torturing himself Giralda followed the motherly housekeeper along the wide hall, and into a narrow corridor from which several doors opened. Mrs. Latten paused at one of these, and said, as she turned the knob: "This is the blue room, Miss. It was my lady's own chamber. As you look so ill, I will sleep in the ante-room adjoining, so as to be within call." She led the way into the chamber, Giralda fol- lowing. It was a long, wide, stately room, with high walls hung with pictures, and a gigantic bay- window, occupying nearly all of one side. It was' furnished in blue of an exquisite shade, the carpet being blue, with a pattern of silver arabesques, the couches and chairs of blue satin, and the low French bedstead having a spread also of blue satin embroidered in the centre with silver wheat sheaves. The effect of this unity of colouring was very fine. The only glimpse of contrasting colour was afforded by the fire on the spotless hearth. Giralda crossed the floor and lifted the blue satin curtains and looked out. The window pro- jected upon the inland side into a lovely terraced garden, where statuary gleamed in the moon- light and the well-kept mounds and crescents promised a wilderness of flowers later in the season. "How beautiful it is here Mrs. Latten !" she</p>
        <p>said, as she returned to the fire. "You should be here in the summer, Miss!" de- clared the worthy housekeeper, her great weak- ness being her pride in her young lord and his possessions, his present residence being her especial delight. "Then the Eyrie is fit for a queen 1 Our flowers are the finest in the kingdom. Our conservatories—you ought to see them Miss! Not but what Trevelyan Park is a grand old place !" added the motherly creature, fearing she might have hurt Giralda's feelings by boasting 9f her own superior grandeurs—for she always said "we " and " our " when speaking of Lord Gros- venor or his property. "But Lord Trevelyan has let it run to decay during the last twenty years or so. Sit down, Miss, in this easy-chair, while I brush out your beautiful hair. It will do your head good I' Giralda sat down and yielded herself to the housekeeper's gentle ministrations, while the worthy soul prattled of her young lord, his good- ness, his generosity, his daring, and his many bright virtues, and certainly, although it was her favourite theme, and one frequently dilated upon, she-had never had before such an interested and unwearied listener.</p>
        <p>"He do have the best temper in the world," continued the good woman, lapsing in her earnest- ness into that utter disregard of grammar peculiar to her class. "Not that he has a mild, milk-and- water disposition, Miss. Heaven forbid! He is all life and energy and fire, and I have seen him angry, Miss, and that stern with some misdoing wretch tint I've trembled in my boots. But he is never cross nor captious. Always bright, always smiling, always gentle and courteous, he is one of the true nobility, Miss, whether they are rich or .or, and have handles to their names or not. " y," she added, triumphantly, "he is polite to every servant in his employ, even to the lowest. He never forgets that even the poorest and hum-</p>
        <p>blest has feelings. Bless his deal; heart!" She brushed vigorously and excitedly, but Gir- Edda was not conscious of her unintentional rough- nese. The young girl experienced a strange pleasure in listening to these praises of the splen- did boy who had told her so recently that she was all the world to him. But the housekeeper's conscience pricked her for talkativeness as she marked the pale and weary looks of her young charge, and she checked her garrulity at last, saying: "The bed is aired, Miss and your night-dress 4 warming by the fire. The sooner you get to sleep the better you will feel in the morning. If you want me, or do not feel well in the night, just touch that bell at the head of your bed. It com- municates with the ante-room, and will arouse me In a moment." She said good night, and withdrew. Left to her- self, Giralda attired herself for bed, and then knelt and said her pure prayers for her loved ones, not emitting the name of Lord Trevelyan. A new name following his lordship's—tile name of Paul Grosve- nor ; but not even In her prayers could she speak that name aloud, nor witheut an innocent and ten- der blush. Then she crept into her soft and yielding bed, and sank almost immediately into the deep slum- bers consequent upon physical'ethaustion. She did not awake until morning. She lay a few moments on her pillow in a delicious dreaminess, not realizing her whereabouts, but as her eyelids softly unclosed, and she saw the morning's light stealing in through the window curtains, she started up, her memory asserting itself. A fire, freshly made, was burning brightly in the grate, and diffusing a pleasant, genial heat, but .Mrs. Latten had gone out as quietly as she had performed the task. "It must be very late!" murmured the young girl, in sudden alarm, "Paul has gone." She reached out for her watch, which was on the etand at the head of her bed, and drew it to her. ,The black enamelled hands pointed out the fact that it was but ten minutes of seven. She sprang out of bed and hurriedly began her toilet. Save for a sense of weariness that still clung to her, she had quite recovered from her threatened illness. She had completed her toilet, and was looking very lovely, although still pale, when the house- keeper entered from the ante-chamber, stopping softly, fearing to disturb her. Giralda greeted her with a pleasant smile. "Has Lord Grosvenor breakfasted yet?" she asked. "No Miss. His lordsdip is in the oriel parlor, ready for his journey. He sent me in to see how you were. His lordship is anxious lest you should be ill after your adventure." s, "I will breakfast with him," said Giralda, "if you will lead the way. My appetite is beginning to return to me, Mrs. Latten, and I dare say I shall do better justice this morning to your culi- nary skill." Mrs. Latten expressed herself as pleased and flattered, and, beaming with smiles, conducted the maiden to the oriel parlour, leaving her at the door. Giralda passed in alone. hle assuredy willo u nggo n t The young lord was pacing to and fro with an anxious step. He heard the door open and close, and exclaimed, Without looking around : • "How is she, Mrs. Latten? Not ill? Not—" P He looked around now, having arrived at the end of his beat. The next instant he came hurry- ing towards the maiden with outstretched hands. "This is an unexpected pleasure!" he exclaimed, all eagerness and animation. "It was good of you to get up to see me off, and so relieve my anxieties about you. I can go away with alight heart now. Are you feeling well ?" "Quite well, except that I am still a little tired, my lord," was the response. "In a day or two I shall be as well as ever. Say nothing of my adven- ture to," she stheepaapdadeadu,dtomaemxplaainPamuyl—coynefit d3'7eunucewiltil hyaovue . anT:d, wishes, expressed or unexpressed. her rd tchoantduhcetelhuledr remember maenmeheasry easy-chair, shier; leave the Eyrie," said Giralda. "Uncle Trevelyan v*eill be anxious about me, and the Park is on your way to the station." he carriage with you when you "I will see you in Lord Trevelyan's charge before I leave you," said Lord Grosvenor. "I cannot go until I know you are safe." The housekeeper and maid came in with the breakfast at this juncture, and also with the news that Lord Trevelyan's green chariot had just entered the lower grounds, and was ascending to the Eyrie. ," My uncle has sent tor me," said Giralda. - "He is anxious for me to come . back. But I will pour your coffee, my lord, before I go." They sat down at the table, and she proceeded, _ _ .</p>
