      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>MOB LAW 1N AMERICA.</head>
        <p>MOB LAW 1N AMERICA.</p>
        <p>SIX NEGROES SHOT.</p>
        <p>A revolting case of mob law, evidently the resillt of a nal-chilly planned plot, is reported from Shelby County, Tennessee. On Friday evening an officer in the district arrested six negroes who had been caught setting fire to number of barns and dwelling-houses. After the Prisoners had been handcuffed and chained together they were placed in the waggon, which started for the gaol at Nlillineton, accompanied bV the officer and one driver. Hy nightfall it reached a creek two miles iron: Milling- ton. and the officer was atantit to lead the horse across the bridge when a voice rang out from the woods by the roadside. " Don't try to cross thcre. The bridge is down. Come this way." The officer unsuspectingly turned the horse's head in the direction from which the voice Came. Hardly had he done so when two guns wire pr.( sented point blank at him and a voice exclaimed, "Throw up your hands." He had no choice but to obey, and a watch was placed over him. A large crowd of armed men then marched from the woods and rapidly ap- pr ached the waggon. Their anery threats soon reached the ears of the wretched negroes.and the latter, divining their purpose, attempted to leap from the cart and make their escace under cover of night. A volley trio forty to fifty guns were poured into them, and t'lev all fell back into the waggon wounded and dying. A dczen mi n then climbed on to the wheels and ;hafts and threw the negroes out upon the ground. Volley after volley was aimed at them, and the mob did not cease firing even when all the six had been killed: The Ilead of one of the negroes was almost shet from his bo.dy, and some of the others were terribly mangled. '1 he mob afterwards rode away. They were all un- masked, but none were known to the offic,r.hy sight. '1 he autl orities have organised an active search for the lynchers. Two detectives have been arrested on suspicion of complicity in the outrages Warrants have also been issued for the arrest of forty farmers of the char.,: of te-'g inThcated in the iThelling. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAMINER. SATURPAY, SEPTEMBER 8, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER. SATURPAY, SEPTEMBER 8, 1894. LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Silk-plated garments are becoming the rage in Lon- don for underwear. The surface is of silk and the back of wool, the combination embodying all the appearance of pure silk with the comfort and hygienic advantages of pure wool. Signorina Teresina Labriola, a young lady, aged 18, who took as the subject of her thesis, Honour from the Legal Point of View," has had conferred on her by the Univerz-ity of Rome the degree of Doter of Laws. This is the first time it has been given to a woman. Signorina Labriola is the daughter of a professor well known for his socialistic opinions. Abbas Pasha, the young Khedive of Egypt, always has his mother with him, and she outranks all his advisers. She is said to be one of the most beautiful women in Egypt, in addition to being the brainiest. The Khedive lives the life of an English or American well-to- do farmer. On his large model farm he has established a model village, with school, club, and mosque, and a fire-enine of modern manufacture. He rises at five o'clock and works hard, fora sovereign, all day. He is fond of riding, driving, and outdoor sports, and is an excellent shot. • Miss Ida Platt is the first coloured woman to be admitted to the bar in America. She was recently graduated from the Chicago Law School, where she had made an exceptionally bright record. She is a young woman of excellent ability and high proficiency in the languages, German and French, and is, besides, an accomplished musician. Her appearance is made dis- tinctive by a mass of grey hair above a regular and finely-featured, expressive face.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE,</p>
        <p>You are doubtless familiar with the saying that "one swallow does not make a summer," but in view of the weather we have been recently experi- encing it seems to me that it might be altered to read "One fine day does not make a summer." The Yankee's definition, after all, is very near the truth. I regret to say that I am one of those unfortunate beings who have not yet taken a holi- day. There are many in the same position, and far from considering the summer at its close, they are only now considering what garments to take with them on their annual holiday. As the uni- versal blouse continues, and as its claims are unusually strong upon all those about to travel, I am adding a pretty model of this particular gar- ment, which in shape scarcely differs from every other blouse, yet shows a marked individuality in its trimming. The material is a soft, peach-like surah trimmed most effectively in stripes of yellow guipure Insertion, this being run through at either edge with a dark green satin ribbon of infinitesimal proportions, and giving an unusually smart finish to this pretty bodice.</p>
        <p>As soon as a maiden is betrothed in Germany she is called bride by her sweetheart, who addresses her thus until it becomes time to call her wife. Immediately upon betrothal, the lovers exchange rings, which, if the course of true love runs smooth, are to be worn ever afterward till death parts them. The woman wears her betrothal ring on the third finger of her left hand until she is married, and then it is transferred to the third finger of the right hand. The husband continues to wear the ring just as the wife wore hers when she was bride, so that one can tell easily at a glance if a man be or be not mortgaged as to his affections.</p>
        <p>For ig wear, town and the country, the tailor-made garment is the most suitable and in the best taste. Serge, tweed, the &amp;emetic sack- cloth, and, weather permitting, holland, drill, and alpaca, are the most useful coat and skirt materials, but upon the waistcoat depends the smartness of such dresses. Tiro black moiriS vest is that to which I give 'Reference, but it must be most care- fully made, and of gGod material. The holland shirt, with white collar and cab, comes next, and looks well with navy blue, black, or green serge. The head-gear worn with such costume should be strictly in keeping with this style of dress, a wide- brimmed black or burnt straw hat, plainly trimmed with an upright bow, through which are passed a couple of quills, a lignt felt or sailor hat being the only admissible accompaniment.</p>
        <p>•Of head-gear there is a bewildering assortment, as usual. I have chosen a bonnet composed of finely out jet. Small green myrtle leaves form a becoming frame 'to the face, while at each side nestle two faded roses. A smart little upstanding bow of black velvet in front, and a wider one at the back, complete this stylish little bonnet. Velvets and velveteens are once more making their appearance, and I um oompelled, with rein°. tance to own that these rich and warm material. emphasise the waning of the summer season. Delightful trimmings in narrow ribbon velvets are seen, many being used in the way we have hitherto employed moire ribbons. In regard to colours, brownie a shade that promisee to be un- usually.. well patrouhed during the coming months.</p>
        <p>I should like to tell you *I ihartning dress effect which I recently noticed. A white silk dress was pleasingly trimmed with blue chiffon, the ' sleeves bearing very large puffs caught with bands of insertion, while the bo3lco had draped braces of chiffon tying up on the shoulders, and fastening at one side of the waist with a short bow and tasselled ends.</p>
        <p>White or light-colcured feathers can be washed in benzine without losing their curl or colour. They should be swung in the air until dry. Another plan for white feathers is to wash them in warm water and °twill° soap; rinse three times to remove fully all the 814 p ; pass through a warm solution of oxllie acid and then lightly starch; dry In a 'warm room by lightly beating each feather agiiust the hand or near the fire. 'Hats trimmed with feathers should be kept in a bandbox when not in use, to preserve them from dust and damp. In the summer rip feathers out of winter hats and put them in a tightly-sealed box with gum cam- phor. To curt ostrich feathecs, have a dull knife, with the top hollowed out nesr the point, if you are going to make a business of it. Hold your feather over the fire, but not sutfi liently near to scorch it, shaking it gently until warm; then hold- ing the feather in the left hand, place the Libre of the feather te-meen the thumb and knife edge, and draw it along quickly, curling the end only. Yours lovingly,</p>
        <p>Jimmut.</p>
