      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Bobbies.</head>
        <p>Bobbies.</p>
        <p>Hobbies have a decided tendency for good on the well- being not only of the young but of the old. . . . Many and many a youth bas, says " Cassell's Book of the Household," been started on his upward path through life by the adoption of a hobby. It has filled up his spare moments; it has kept him from temptations out of doors and given him sotnething to think about at home in- doors. It has kept his mind from preying upon itf,elf, so to speak, and killed that deadly weed of 'self con- sciousness "which at a c( rtain period of every youth's existence takes root in his being, and may, if not de- stroyed, grow up into a very obnoxious and troublesome plant indeed. Many a hobby adopted in youth may be found of benefit in after-life Suppose a lad has learnt to use tools : the struggle of existence is becoming greater every day, and no one can ever be the worse for having learned an honest handicraft—say, that of car- pentry or joinery. On the contrary, if a young man has to go abroad to a new land to make his living—perhaps even pioneering—he will find the hobby of his youth be- come one of very great utility indeed. Almost any hobby teaches business babas, and instils into the mind that scrt of ambition to excel. But still more, in later life, when cares press and yet actual occupation may diminish ; when children leave the home, and quieter times settle down around one; then a genuine hobby—a pursuit in which one can for a time find an absorbing interest, and which may furnish stimulating intercourse with others who have the same tastes—may be of incal- culable benefit. It finds one occupation ; it finds one pleasant intercourse and communication; it finds one rfading with a purpose in view; and these things may be as the wine of life to people in later middle age. Hobbies have helped many an octogenarian to enjoy life and be a blessing to his fellows to the very end. Then, again, how a hobby may alleviate disease and pain, no one but a sufferer knows. . . . Still again, such pur- suits broaden a person's character and nature. lt is piti- able to see how some men seem to get totally absorbed in the mere business of making money. Gradually their business, though it may not claim in reality their time aid effort, usurps more and more of their thoughts, till at last they seem able to care about nothing else, save occasional excitements of a more or less questionable kind. Try and have something to care about and follow up besides that, else the time will surely come when the want of such an unselfish interest will be sorely felt. 411. - PAVEMENT A Wl' UP-TO-DATE.</p>
        <p>The pavethent arist now rises beyond the mackere and I roken-plate school. Be goes in for landscapes and up-to-Gate portraits copied from the illustrated papers. He has even given up the paving stones for cardboard, and, if he is lucky in having a good " p'tch," lights up his collection at night with three or four paraffin lamps. "Well, you see." says an itinerant artist the other day to a writer in " Cassell's Saturday Journey," "ii you takes up room on the pavement, the police don't like it ; and if a shower of rain comes on,where's your drawings? Besides when you stick up your picters against the the public can see 'ern better. How long have I been ai. this kind of work? 'Bout nine or ten years. I never had no training. but I was always fond of looking at pictera. When I came gut of the army I hadn't got a penny, and how to support the wife and children I didn't know. One day, when it looked as if there waen't going to be any Sunday's dinner, I thought I'd have a try at the game. So I did, and to my surprise took 16s. 4d. the first day. I've stuck to it ever since,though it ain't so good as it used to be. Pitches are hard to get and there's ever so many more in it. Why. I know a man and his wife and their three sons and a daughter all at the work, and there's lots more. The best pitch as ever I had was at Knightsbridge when a foreign kine was over here. I spotted it at the time, and, thinks I, there'll 1e a lot after this. So down I goes at midnight to start work, and at daylight sure enough half a dozen others come along, and wild enough they were to find me there. I was on that pitch a couple of days, and the leather bag as we carried the tools in in them days—it was pavement work then, you understand—wouldn't hold the half-pence. Another good pitch is Chatham. does a bit of soldier life as catches em. There's the • Wounded Friend' as always takes. Lots of men can draw a sight better than me, but they all say they can't touch my 'Wounded Friend.' Sometimes I does tne work and leaves the boy in charge while I go to another pitch. Shall I bring my boy up to the business ? No, I shan't. 'Taint good enough now."</p>
