      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>COLLAPSE OF THE LANCASHIRE COAL</head>
        <p>COLLAPSE OF THE LANCASHIRE COAL</p>
        <p>A GLIMPSE " BEFIIND THE SCENES." The shadows of Westminster Abbey fall on a scattered colony of men, women, and children who pick up a precarious living by sweet music, principally organ - grinding. Whatever their origin was, the Cockney de- 1.-Tient now prevails ' • and their pride of descent soems concentrated in the desire to transmit their trade from father to son. They infinitely prefer the excitement of a sliding scale of existence which may mean three pounds one week or three shillings, to the dull monotony of even ; work and even money. Miss Searraw, a clever lady journalist, determined to become one of this strange community, and he tells us all about it in the" Newnery lieuse Magazine." ENGLISH PROFESSIONALS. , My first discovery was, she says, the fact that, at least, Westminster organ-grinders living in Westminster, did not bind themselves to be Italians—though many ' dress as such, and mostly owned their organs, or hiret them Irom each other. . . . A week later, and I was domiciled with an organ-grinder and his wife, having made a bargain with them that I should be treated aa ore of the family, eating what they ate, and drinking what they drank. The man was to take me on his; remd with the organ the first day; for the remainder of the week I was to manipulate the machine unaided. Organ grinders cultivate a distinct code of manners. Anybody can take a machine and "grind," but it is not everyone who has faculties for the comprehension of all the delicate windings of this branch of art. The initia- tion is a matter of favour, and the favour is not easily won. They study mankind with the same diligence as a metaphysician, and with as much skill ; their ready wit is rarely at fault, and their scorn is scathing when their ranks are invaded by the incompetent or the amateur. In the morning their rule is to do the better class streets, their demeansur modest, their attire neat. Fracas with policemen are avoided, associates are ac- knowledged but by eye-salutation ; anything like beg- ging is studiously avoided, and a stolid amiability is the ' marl of the whole tribe. In the evening this is thrown off; lower localities are the most 'lucrative ; outside a publia house they sometimes do a roaring business, and are sure of a drink or two being given. There they in- dulge in friendly bandinage with those who surroiund them, are not afraid to " cha if" a passing" peeler" or descend to abusive language. THE LADY AMATEUR.</p>
        <p>I had much to learn, but I enjoyed learning it : 1 pasmers-by tossed half-pence generously; servant-maidg had their favourite tunes and expressed their gratitude by gifts of scraps. . . . For the initiated there are places where they can retire in the middle of the day for a rest, a wash, or a sleep. A penny an hour is charged ft r their stay, and their maehine is housed on the same Let ms. . . . The blinds are closed all day and there are secret passwords by which the manager can make the inmates aware that the police are on th,-/ir track. Then a general exodus takes place by divers ways known only to themselves, and a few b)na-fide cress-sellers. e..rnet-plavers, or apple-women are left, who are never known to"' rat " on their companions. On a wet day ta- b . able to dry one's drenched clothes if far from home is a decided gain,but to have to consort with such a low cl4ss of criminals rather militates against the advan- tage.</p>
        <p>A HARD LIFE. To call an organ-grinder's life an idle life or a lazy one is to show you know nothing at all about it. It is a pretty good test of endurance to stand for ten or twelve hours a day moving your arms all the time. You have to use your eyes, to scan the passers-by, note the people A t the windows, and watch the surrounding crowd. You have to stand exposure to weather, and it is no joke to play unconcernedly while an east wind is turning your face blue and making your teeth chatter. Machines are a constant cause of care : the trained car ha Rto listen to the first indications of " an out of order "constitu- tion. A tiny change in the barometer may render them , wheezy raspy, or wiry in tone, and it takes away from the charm of one's earnings when half has tog° in re- pairs. Another trouble is the fickleness of the public taste; they soon get tired of tunes. A new one costs seven shillings and sixpence. Tunes make or mar you. One man may earn thirty chitlings a week, his comrade butt' six shillings, because one plays popular airs, the other does nGt.</p>
        <p>THE MAKE-UP. Main, clothes are an anxiety, the garments of an rgan-grinder giving him as much thought as the toilet of a West-end dame. He never forgets there is a certain de: ree of shabbiness beyond which the police do not let hue transgress, and it takes many an anxious hour of Sorry. to present that degree of attractiveness which be considers commensurate with his position. A dealer in " old " told me that organ-grinder• . e-e more trouble than anyone else, and that one bad - ii to him after rejecting three waistcoats, five coats. Rix iia,PR, ...you know we're bound to be pertickler; t.'l3 eye of the world is upon us."</p>
        <p>841, with all its dtaw-ostits, life in the streets has its 0 arms, and I will own that after a week of it the yap - b ndism of such an existence fell on me like a spell : ,Aori I urid,rstood, as no words could have made me ,1-1-7 :too how liberty-loving man, with his animat instinct of lawlessness, revels in its independence and f ,0111,1 restraint, and would rather run the chance necasiorral doss-house bed or a night on the Ern - b nkment than submit to be shackled with the fetters of he sweated slave: _</p>
        <p>Miss Sparrow's personai emolument at the end of the wi,k she found to be one pound ; one shilling and Si* e we she paid for her rooms five shillings for her board. :b• had only two wet days to mar her pleamantness of her task, and among her experiences she had increased tier vocabulary of abuse, and learned on how little one can live.</p>
