      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SALES ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>SALES ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The attempt which was made, after the close of the pretracted coal strike of last year, to establish a combi- n ..tion of coalowners truoughont Lancashire and chivy' hire, with a view of preventing undue competition la -eie in irket, haa,after a comparatively few month's trials r,Lilen through. The association, which was divided into disrriets, each of which regulated prices in its respective iiisrket, with a Council to which any alteration in liSt yates had to be submitted for sanction, exercised for a tune effective control over prices, and gave a check re, :tie reckless competition of previous years with regard to - the as and locomotive fuel contracts,upon which a fair!? .. 441trsta ntial advance was this season generally maintained- tsc:cently, however, there have been considerable coin' , piairits on the part of some of the leading coslowners and c.,lliery companies connected with the Association that not a few of the members had been selling at under the official rates agreed upon-at the meetings. A month or two back there were one or two important secessions. and at ft meeting in Wigan on Monday matters were brought to a climax by the representatives of several of the largest colliery concerns in L tacaehire intimatißg that, in consequence of the persistent underselling of some of the members, they sh.aild discontinue their con- nection with • the Association. As the other members present did not, under these conditions, care to carry 01' the Association, it wan decided that no further meetings should be called, the chairman suggesting that perhaps the best thing -to do under the circumstances was to make the earliest possible arrangements for the decent interment of the Association.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH COUNCILS ACT.—An organisation called the Parish Councils Association has been formed on the same basis as the County Councils and Local Boards Association. This Association is deservedly making' great headway, and it must prove a great boon to all persons interested in parochial govern - meat, as it supplies all its members legal and general information, all official circulars, &amp;c., free of charge. Moreover by publishing a monthly magazine all members will be kept up to date in all matters relative to the new form of local goveTO- ment. To parish officials such as overseers and assistant overseers as well as clergy, churchwardens and council- lors,this organisation will be invaluable as many of the ari comparatively inexperienced in the new law and its proeedure ; besides, few have the time to bestow in MO" tering the intricacies of the Act itself, and as there are many vital and important functions to be carried out there is no doubt many will avail themselves of the Or portunity and become members which can be done no . payment of a nominal subscription. As will be see" from an advertisement all particulars may be had 011. applieation to Mr. J. Wallis Davies, the solicitor aria sect , either at 22 Great Gaorge-street, Westminster. London, or Town Ball, Abergele.</p>
        <p>The largest comets are so ra.rified that they never harm pl4nets and satellites hy colliding with them. ScientiSts say that four million wrqls spun by you'll!, spiders would not form a strand as great iu diameter a, a hair from the human head.</p>
