      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>"BIRMINGHAM SATURDAY NIGHT."</head>
        <p>"BIRMINGHAM SATURDAY NIGHT."</p>
        <p>"Being naturally a little conservative and sceptical and not too ready to accept as facts everything that comes before our notice, and having our attention so frequently calkd t the testimonials with reference to the value of Warner's Safe Cure as a safe and effective urative agent, we determined to satisfy our curiosity Ifl a practical manner. A representative of " Saturday Night " was accordingly despatched round the district to make dircct enquiries among two or three prominent Birmingham people and ascertain how much truth they contained.</p>
        <p>Our reporter visited two Birmingham gentlemen Nyl.iose testimonials had been printed, and found the naid statements genuine. In fact, Warner's Safe Cure 13.tid effected cures of long standing torpidity of the liyer,characterised by indigEstion, pain in the shoulders, giddiness, nausea, headaches, and general debility after all other efforts to obtain relief had proved ineffectual. IVe are satisfied now that the testimonials for Warner's Safe Cure are true and a fair tribute to the merit this medicine is known to possess. This calls to mind that pea,rs ago a lad in our employ W 35 stricken with Bri gilts Disease of the Kidneys,and his case pronounced hopeless by eminent Birmingham physicians. He was afterwards discharged from one of our leading hospitals as incur- yet was restored to complete health solely through the use of Warner's Safe Cure. "A living monument' o the virtue of this medicine. " Honour to whom ti°l3oUr is due," and all honour to such a life-saving agency."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Vouncil was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday evening. There were present Aldermenß.W. Fran- Comb, Hutchinson, Sutton, Bolton, Councillors R.T. Pairclough, Baird, Shillcock, J. Muir, G. Plumpton, Cannell, R. Heaton, Shaw-Green, J. Evans, W. P. Bkelton, J. Smethurst, J. Hepherd, A. Bennett, H. Roberts, H. J. Lougton, Wilkinson, J. Hill,A.Woods, T. Flood, Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), J. rairhurst (borough treasurer). On the motion of Alderman HUTCHINSON, seconded by Councillor Slow GREEN, Alderman Francomb, the De- PUty Mayor, was voted to the chair.</p>
        <p>THE LATE SIR GILBERT GREENALL. , The following letter was read :—" Walton Hall, War- ngton, August 13th, 1894. My Dear Sir,—Will you Einctly convey to the Town Council my deep sense and appreciation of their kindness and very sincere and Crateful thanks, in which my children join, for their BYrnpathy in our bereavement ?—Believe me, yours Ina ost ,faithfully, SUSIE GREENALL. J. Lyon Whittle, 7</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>LATE ALDERMAN ASHTON.</head>
        <p>THE LATE ALDERMAN ASHTON.</p>
        <p>A. letter wajit7e-ad-1:r;m--3-1;:-.137 Ashton thanking the vouncil for the vote of condolence passed with himself and family on the death of his father, the late Alderman Atht,on.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN HOLMES AND THE FREEDOM OF THE BOROUGH. Alderman Holmes wrote :—" Dear Mr. Mayor,—l de- SIM to express through you to the members of the Cor- teration my sincere thanks for the unsolicited distinc- ,lol2 which they have just conferred upon me by present- s me with the freedom of the borough, wl:ich I shall ~ver value as a mark of your appreciation for the services have been able to rend( r for the good of the town as as lay ia my power. I look back to my long connec- blen with the Warrington Corporation with great plea- eure•—Yours faithfully, CHAS., J. HotmEs. The Mayor O f Warrington." (A pplau,e.). The CHAIRMAN said this letter would be recorded with 'ne minutes of the special meeting of the Council for the esentati on of the casket to Major Pickmere. He .uought that Alderman Holmes was so incapacitated by Illness and taken by surprise that at the time he could 11 (it express his thanks to the Corporation. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE ELECTION IN HOWLEY WARD. The CHAIRMAN declared that Mr. John Evans, of 33, ..,Church-st., licensed victualler and brewer, had been ‘4Lily elected a councillor for the Elowity Ward.</p>
        <p>FINANCE. • , The minutes of the Finance Committee which appeared in last week's " Examiner" were confirmed on the za,otion of Councillor Shaw Green,seconded by Councillor +Dlopherd.</p>
        <p>SANKEY BROOK AGAIN! Councillor PLIMPTON said that before the minutes of the ~._4,llitary Works Committee were confirmed he wished to nail a question respecting Sankey Brook. The state it 'Was in at the present time was such as to make it an tkoorninablo nuisance. He was informed that the Sani- 'all' Works Committee were responsible for a good deal .1.?1 the nuisance by turning refuse into the brook from 140rigford Dept. Councillor Sittu.cook said Councillor Plumpton was !ntirely mistaken in saying that ref use wc s turned into rlle brook from Lonuford Depot. There had been no refuse turned into the brook for some months. The refuse came more from St. Helens way. (Hear, hear.) Councillor BENNETT thought that if the nuisance was calmed at St. Helens the Corporation should take steps to, prevent its continuance. The Corporation should some remedy. He understood, however, that the ‘'urporation were not wholly free from blame for a f•onsidera,ble amount of local sewage found its way into e brook. The CHAIRMAN said it would be wise not to continue _zke discussion in the absence of the Town Clerk. He Understood that no Warrington sewage was turned into tha brook. the subject then dropped, and the minutes of the wrotnittee were passed.</p>
        <p>"WAS IT MONEY %ELL-SPENT ?" „CeUßCillOr LONGTON wished to know if the expenses of ‘ue deputation to London was included in the sum ap- tearing in the minutes of the Sanitary Works Commit-I ; if so, what was the total cost, and could the Chair-1 'Van give them some evidence as to whether the money "5 been well spent or not? When the question of ap-1 )20anting a deputation was raised he asked them if it ould be money well spent, and he was assured that it ZTuld be. If it had been money well spent would the aairrnan of t he committee let them knowlrankly ? CHAIRMAN said the expenses of the deputatioP were included in the sum. }3 Councillor SuiLt.coat said the expenses of the deputa- "Lau were £l2 12s each. net Call 'lt n or LONGTON said he would not have put this 4estion if he had observed that the Chairman an.l It PutY-Chairman of the committee were not present. :le Wished to know if the deputation were going to pre- "cot a report. ,Aiderrnan SUTTON replied in the affirmative, and tz?cled that he did not see what it would avail Mr. Long- thla if the information were supplied him in reference to at Particular case. C°. tMeillOr LONGTON : For future guidance. I,lclerman SUTTON said when the report had been fur- 414:;_ed Councillor Longton would have plenty of time to for" inquiries and act accordingly when the time came I,!PPointing a deputation to go to London. L'io Minutes were then confirmed.</p>
        <p>1,, LATCHFORD AND WILDERSPOOL CAUSEWAYS. r *Zia Minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee tre of a routine character, except that which recom- e:lded that the sum of £8,190 be accepted from the tenutitY Council of Lancashire in commutation of the ,'"4al cost of repairing the Latchford and Wilderspool ,115evrays. 13‘ucillor HEATON proposed the confirmation of the granr,Les, and expressed his opinion that in regard to the t from the County Council they had made a good Alderman HUTCHINSON seconded. .erw?euncillor HEPHERD hoped that the committee would thibrld s.orne of the money, and put the roads in some- hod g like decent order. They were a disgrace to any- il their present order. ,4ik'euncillor ROBERTS asked whether in this sum the 4:felllng of the roads was taken into consideration. If were not so, he thought it was a very poor bargain. th a very few e Cor • oi years buth roads must be widened, and f Por the ation had got to widen them at the expense f Pau'atePayers a Warrington, the fact that they had got :11ent for thirty years' maintenance did not make it a ;sY nattering bargain. (Hear, hear.) th'eciuncillor Woos asked if the question of widening ktbridge had been considered. dderinan Surrow said the widening of Warrington aphip did not effect this question at all, and a separate Nyjcil"geirient would be made with regard to that and the es-fling of the roads. 44:U40110r HEATON said there was no doubt that the ilideetaA would have to be widened, but under no con- Would the expense be paid by the County 0h..'.r1e,!. He thought they had made a proper bargain Itieinirig thirty years' purchase money for these roads. te,..3:Were not in possession of any final information con- the widening of the bridge, but he believed they I,Tu get everything they wanted. ce'llue CHAIRMAN said he happened to be at the County Nvoelirl Meeting when this matter was settled. They , cc aware that the county of Chester up to now gilic:!ad to repair the roads from Warrington Bridge to biz elBear Bridge and Wilderspool. That had been a 'kith karge on them, so they had entered into a contract a ye t ue Warrington Corporation for doing it for so much tioe ar.• For some years past, however, the Corpora- owect': Warrington had been anxious to get it into their 1e of When the County Councils were formed the Of L o' Warrington was put under the County Council ih takncashire, whereas they knew Latchford used to be The county of Chester for administrative purposes. unt37 Council of Cheshire handed over to the 7 Council of Lancashire £8,190 to take the respon- ,_"l,7 instead of themselves. The Warrington Corpora-</p>
        <p>... Tbt„ f?,,T , , Utleciiia Laundresses say °Mohler@ " STAB G o iz.,611. ter Ilau, kt Leci for imparting a beautifnl finish toalistarth''s cold or boiling water starch. Mr. Cog 11 si tareg Gloss ply wife will not finish our linen without your rit_ro Pleased ,1 , _From Mrs. —, Bradford : "Dear sit.— ic[l,4o:lltid be used lila o e% Giazes..a lidifiyrn you that I have tried many so-called (11,:shes, but find none equal to yours." It 104._ stAreti seller try_ house. Sold at id., 3d., and 6d.„by procure it. T. L LI.I. lac/. Bvackburnt. ;.?gtpritz:tOirt:2kAa7till L. .</p>
        <p>tion had been anxious to get hold of that sum, and, act- ing on the advice of the Borough Surveyor and the Town Clerk, they entered into negotiations for having it handed over to them. He had brought up the matter of the widening of the bridge, and he was told that there were difficulties in the way, as the Midge was originally built by the hundred of the Wes; Derby and the county of Chester. They would have to be congulted,and would have to pay a proportionate share of the expense when- ever the bridge should be altered. It was, therefore, postponed to a future meeting. The minutes were then confirmed. THE SCHOOL BOARD QUESTION.</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN, moved the adoption of the minutes of the School Attendance Committee and drew attention to the increase in the number of children attending school. Councillor SnfEnfurtsT, in seconding, said he expected that Councillor Shaw Green would have said something about their having staved off a School Board ; it must have slipped his memory. He was sure that the inhabi- tants of Warrington would be pleased to know that through the energy of the Chairman and the rest of the committee, and the kindness of the Rect .r (the Rev. F. W. Willis) and his co-trustees, they had been able to stave off sa School Board for some length of time. He thought it would give everybody in Warrington pleasure to learn that there was every probability cf their being able to entirely dispense with a school Board. (Hear, hear.) Although an inspector might come down and make an enquiry, it would be shown that there was sufficient number of places for children. The Rev. F. W. Willis had turned a place of worship into a school. Councillor SHAW GREEN remarked that during the month there had been a communication from the Educa- tion Department threatening them that if they did n.t conform to the regulations it would be necessary to hold an inquiry and issue a final notice. In the meantime they had been in negotiation with the Rector, and now they had secured St. George's Mission foe an infant school, capable of accommodating 300. It was up to present late requirements, being renlete with modern improvements in every way. Me Elucation Depart- ment had been written to, asking them to send an inspector down to examine the building, and they stated that they felt sure there would be no necessity for hold- ing an inquiry. The committee wanted to avoid expense and to let the Department see that they were in earnest in endeavouring to find accommodation. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were carried. MUSEUM, ETC. COMMITTEE—FREE STUDENTSHIP FOR THE CONTINUATION CLASSES.</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS, in ' moving the adoption of the minutes of the Museum etc. Committee, said • that he wished to draw the attention of the Council to the fact that they had just issued the syllabus for the classes in connection with the Technical Institute, and School of Science, for the session of 1894-5. He would be very glad if thes Council would un every occasion which was convenient and agreeable to themselves, let the facts and conditions of the syllabus be as generally known as possible. The institution was of immense value to the community, and the younger members of the commu- nity would find them of great importance to them in preparing for the battle of life which they expected to enter into. Councillor Muni seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST asked what system the Com- mittee had adopted in granting the free studentships to the various schools. It depended upon the answer to his question whether he moved an amendment or not.</p>
        <p>Councillor &amp;Law GREEN said that he did not think the Deputy-Chairman (Councillor Roberts) was present when the arrangements for the scholarships was gone into. It was agreed that the scholarships should be given to various schools in proportion to the number of scholars in the higher standards. Councillor SMETHURST said if that was the answer he would move an amendment that the minutes be referred back to the Committee. He found that People's College with 1,375 scholars had seven scholarships allowed, while there were other places with 900 who had only three. He gave a number of statistics for the purpose of showing that the Committee had not dealt Justly in the giving of the scholarships to thv various schools. He complained that too many scholarships had been given to a school where the people could better afford to pay, and that the poorer schools had not enough.</p>
        <p>Councillor SHAW GREEN : It is not the number of scholars in attendance at school taken as a whole, but the number in the higher standards, that the scholar ships have been appertioned to. Councillor SMETHURST still dissented from the proposal of the Committee. Councillor ROBERTS said that Councillor Smethurst missed the explanation of Councillor Shaw Green. The scholarships or studentships were not awarded in pro- portion to the total numbers in the various schools. They were restricted in the first instance to the students already in the higher standards. The object of the con- tinuation schools was entirely missed by Councillor Smethurst. Their object was to prepare the scholars for the science and art classes afterwards. T:4EVO was an interregnum if he might so term it, between the passage from the ordinary day Schools, and the time when they joined the classes of science and art. The continuation schools were to restore the knowledge which had been lost after leaving school, and thus to prepare them again for the science and art classes. They had therefore to restrict the scholarships to the higher standards, and he therefore thought that the plan proposed was an equit- able and fair one. It was the first time that they had given the scholarships, and the Committee could not divise a more equitable plan than this at the present. He hoped that Councillor Smethurst would withdraw his resolution.</p>
        <p>Alderman fluTounisoN : I think the explanation is a good one. , The CHAIRMAN said that People's College ranked as a national school, but they all knew that the people who sent their children thvre were much better off than the parents of the children who went to other national schools of the town. If the plan proposed were adopted for good, there was no doubt that People's College would always have a very great majority of the scholarships, simply because the parents could keep their children at school up to the seventh standard. He hoped, however. that Councillor Smethurst would withdraw his resolution, as the committee had only one object, to serve best for the majority. He thought that the managers of the other schools could be trusted to look after themselves.</p>
        <p>Councillor SMETHURST said that he would withdraw his amendment on account of what Councillor Roberts bad said, although he did not feel quite satisfied. Councillor R. T. FaincLotrou congratulated the com- mittee upon the excellent syllabus they had issued. He thought it only fair that in a matter of that sort, they should congratulate them, because they must have taken a great deal of trouble. He would at the same time like to make a suggestion in resptct to music. With respect to the higher class, he thought that they ought to give a concert at the end of the season and he did not see why it should not pay or even make money out of it. The money might be used for defraying the cost of the dis- tribution of prizes, or might be given to the Infirmary. It would be an encouragement to the pupils and teachers. Perhaps the Town Council would attend the concert. (Laughter.) It rarely happened that they saw a mem- ber of the Town Council at a good concert. (Renewed laughter.) Their attendance would be an improvement to them. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were then agreed to. THE CEMETERY BIERS.</p>
        <p>Councillor HILL in moving the confirmation of this committee's minutes said there was a decrease in profits and receipts from funerals, as compared with last year. Of course they did not wish to increase their receipts by an increase of funerals. (Laughter.) Councillor SMETHURST seconded the motion.</p>
        <p>Councillor WOODS congratulated the committee upon their excellent syllabus. (Laughter.) He was glad to see that they were reducing deaths and burials. (Re- newed laughter.) He wished to call the attention of the committee to the biers of the Cemetery. Alderman BOLTON: Beers? (Laughter.) Councillor Woonsi I mean biers. The CuainmAN : Would it not be well to spell the word ? It is tickling the ears of a brewer here. (Laugh- ter.) _</p>
        <p>C'ouncillor WOODS said he had already done so to those sitting near him. (Laughter.) It was time they had biers on wheels, and not such delicate and consumptive things they had now. (Renewed laughter.) Councillor HILL said the committee had done what they could to remedy certain defects at the Cemetery, but it was suggested that a deputation should see some of the most advanced burial grounds. (Loud laughter.) The minutes were then agreed to. TOWN HALL, Sr.C., COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bouros moved, and Councillor Dr.CANNRLL seconded, that the minutes of this committee be con- firmed.</p>
        <p>Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOUGII asked when the question of the enlargement of the Town Hall would be taken into consideration? Councillor BENNETT : And how are we to deal with the lawn in front of the Town Hall?</p>
        <p>Councillor ROBERTS : Do you propose to have any public recognition of the gift of the gates to the town? If so when and of what character? I don't think the gift should be allowed to pass without notice. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Alderman BOLTON said he should have thought Coun- cillor Fairclough had had enough of the question of the proposed enlargement of the Town Hall. It wa not intended to touch the lawn—(hear, hear)—while with regard to the proposed celebration, it would be better for the work connected with the gates to be done</p>
        <p>EPPS'S COCOA.—GBITIPUL AND COMFORTING.—"By ft thorough knowledge of the natural,laws which govern the operations of digestion and nutrition and by a aireful applica- tion of the fine properties of well-selected Cocos, Mr. Epps has provided for our breakfast and supper a delicately flavoured beverage which may save us many heavy doctors' bills. It is by the judicious use of such articles of diet that a constitution may be gradually built up until strong enough to resist every tendency to disease. We may escape many a fatal shaft, by keeping ourselves well fortified with pure blood and a properly nourished frame."—" Civil Service Gazette."—Made simplywith boiling water or milk.—Sold only in packets, by Grocers, labslled—" JAMES EPPS SG CO., Ltd., Hornceopathic Chemists London."—Also makers of Epps 's Cocoaine or Cocoa Nib-Extract: A thin beverage of full flavour, now with many beneficially taking the place of tea. Sold in Warrington by Bowden, Bamford, and Pezcival —Advt.</p>
