      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>riflE EXAMINFB, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 8 1894.</head>
        <p>riflE EXAMINFB, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 8 1894.</p>
        <p>before it was taken into consideration. The minutes were then agreed to. GAS COMMITTEE. 'A WORN OUT" LOCOMOTIVE:</p>
        <p>COW:16110r HEPHERD, in moving the confirmation of the minutes of the above committee, observed with reference to the locomotive they had purchased for £250 from the Ship Canal Company, and which required £l.OO for repairing and painting, that under the old system a man was injured, while twelve months ago one was killed. Tile Committee, therefore, thought it high time to get a locomotive. C ,uncillor R. T. FarficLouGni seconded the adoption of the minutes. Councillor WOODS would like to have the details of the gas tests. Ths gas seemed to be going altogether. (Laughter.) Councillor SMETHURST called attention to the increased expenditure which the purchase of the locomotive would bring about. Councillor Floor. remarked that £lOO was a very large expenditure to make so soon alter the purchase of the locomotive. Be also complained that the locomotive was worn out. There was more liability ;to accident in the case of a locomotive than in the case of horse hire. Councillor ROBERTS thought that if the Gas Committee were not so be trusted to manage such a detail, they were not competent to manage the affairs of the gas works at Longford. (Cries of " Oh, oh," and " No, no.") Usk contended that a great number of horses were ruined' in waggon shunting. Councillor HEPHERD, in replying to the various ques- tions which had been raised, said that the Gas Commit- tee were anxious that someone should examine the gas and that the engineer's reports, which they got every month, should be found to be correct. With regard to the locomotive, they examined four or five belonging to the Ship Canal Company before the Dislev was pur- chased. The Vulcan Foundry Company reported upon it, and the report was so satisfactory that after spending £lOO in repairs they were told the locomotive would be worth £5OO. The cost of two horses and a man per. annum used to be £lBO. By the purchase of the locomo- tive a considerable sum would be saved. The minutes were then agreed to. GENERAL PURPOSES COMMITTEE.-THE TOWN BELL.</p>
        <p>, This committee's minutes were moved by the CHAIR- MAN, seconded by Alderman SUTTON. Councillor FLOOD, alluding to the resolution on the minutes "That the Town Bell be rung every morning for two minutes commencing at seven minutes to six," said that the ringing should be commenced at a quarter to six. If that were done it would be a great conveni- ence to people who had to go to work early in the morn- ing. More frequently than otherwise the 13,21 l was rung after six rather than before that hour.</p>
        <p>Councillor WooDs suggested that the bell should not be run,'' at all. It was a decided muisance. He had lived underneath the bell for a number of years, and it had proved an enormous nuisance to himself. With regard to it being of use to the working class, he con- tended they could not hear it unless the wind blew in the right direction. Was it intended to do away with night ringing ? Councillor Snaw GREEN: The curfew will still ring. Councillor WooDs : I am sorry to hear that. Councillor R. T. FAIRCLOUGH spoke in favour of the bell being rung earlier, as suggested by Councillor Flood. .</p>
        <p>_ Alderman SUTTON thought it would be a great boon to workingmen to have the bell rung earlier, It had been rung to his knowledge for nearly half a century. (The CHAIRMAN "Dear me.") And no complaint had been made up to the present time. He should be sorry for the ringing to be discontinued. (Hear,hear.) Councill"or WOODS : It is all very well for you country gentlemen to say that. (Laughter.) Alderman SUTTON proposed that the bell should be rung for seven minutes. .</p>
        <p>The amendment was not seconded, and accordingly fell to the ground. The minutes were agreed to, and the Council rose.</p>
        <p>With Or- dinary Postage up to</p>
