      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>RUNCO RN BOARD OF QUA RDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCO RN BOARD OF QUA RDIANS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon the fortnightly meeting of the above Board was held at the Workhouse,Dutton, under the presidency of Mr. John Mclntyre. There were also present the Rev. J. IV. N. Tanner, Messrs. H. E. Heywood, S. Taylor, J.P., A. R. Norman, J. Brandreth, J. M. Williams, S. Woodward, T. Lewis, C. T. Whitley, E. Aaron, C. W. Davenport, John Ashton, clerk; and G. Ash ton, deputy-clerk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>TUE CASE OF LUNATICS.</head>
        <p>TUE CASE OF LUNATICS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported the receipt of a letter from the Local Government Bard enclosing a copy of a report which had been received freim the Commissioners in Lunacy, of a visit made to the Workhouse on July 25th, by Leiut. Col. Irving, and enquiring whether any steps had been taken for the removal of Francis Dixon to an asylum. The enclosure Stated that there were three men and seven women of unsound mind in the Workhouse. Since the last visit of a Commissioner one man had been sent to an aiylum ; one woman had gone to her friends; and four women had died. He saw one woman in bed, all the rest of the imbecile inmates were up and neatly dressed. The visit was made early in the morning. Francis Dixon was in a highly excited state, and accord- ing to the matron had been troublesome for some days. This patient was more fitted for an asylum than for a Workhouse. All the other inmates of this class seemed proper Workhouse cases. The rooms in which they slept were all found to be clean and tidy and the beds comfortable. The inmates bathed weekly. Etis (the Commissioner's) visitation led him to believe that the inmates of unsound mind were carefully and well looked after by the officials of the institution.</p>
        <p>INSPECTION OF CHESTEIL ASYLUM. In accordance with notice of motion the Rev. J.W.N. Tanner moved the appointment of a committee to visit the pauper lunatics in the Chester Asylum, chargeable to the Runcorn Union. The gentlemen named for the com- mittee were the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner,Mr. Yarwood,and Mr. Lewis.—Mr. Heywood seconded and the proposition was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>COUNTY RATE BASIS.</head>
        <p>THE COUNTY RATE BASIS.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the Clerk of the Cheshire County Council referring to the County Rate Basis and giving notice that the committee appointed to prepare the new basis would meet at Crewe on October sth for the purpose of considering objections to the said basis and for hearing parties making such ,objections. The letter further stated that as the County Council had. already approved of the principle adopted lsy the com- mittee in arriving at the. new basis and the reductions from the gross value of the several classes of property, objections to the new basis on the ground that it was in excess of the Poor Rate Assessment, would be enter- tained, unless it could be shown to the satisfaction of the committee that such Poor Rate Assessment repre- sented the true annual value.—After some discussion, in thecourse of which the general opinion of the members appeared to be that Runcorn,Weston, and other districts had been assessed as fully as possible, the letter was referred to the existing committee which have this question in hand. THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT ACT 1894.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Cheshire County Council wrote en- closing a draft of the order which the Council purposed issuing,dealing with the number of guardians 81c.,for the Unionof Runcorn. He would be pleased to know whether there were any observations make upon it before it was sealad. Enclosed was also a print of the order made by the Joint Committee appointed to deal with the War- rington Union inviting the townships of Latchford with- out, Grappenhall, and Thelwall to the Runcorn Union. The number of guardians will be 45, and districts coun- cillors 38, and the number of parish councillors 222. The boundaries of the Weaver and Weston wards are also defined in accordance with those given at the enquiry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FORTHCOMING POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>THE FORTHCOMING POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. E. Heywood moved the appointment of a com- mittee to attend the Conference of the North Western Poor Law District, to be held in the Town Hall, South- port, on the 28th and 29th inst. The gentlemen he named as the committee were Messrs. John Mclntyre, J. M. Williams, and J. Ashton, clerk ; the expenses incurred to be defrayed by the Board.—The Rev. J. W. N. Tanner seconded and the proposition was accepted. TOWNSHIPS BACKWARD IN THEIR PAYMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brandreth in presenting the report of the Finance Committee stated that there was a balance of £1,500 13s. 7d. in the treasurer's hands. The amount which they had to pay out was £143, of which £9O was for Runcorn relief; £3O for Frodsham, and £l9 for Bud- worth. The following townships had not paid their calls, all of which ware duo on the 17th August :—Acton Grange, Alvanley, Antrobus, Bartingttn, Budworth, Clifton, Daresbury, Frodsham, Halton, Hatton, Keck wick, Kingsley, Kingswood, Moore, Norley, Preston-o'th- Hill, and • Whitley Superior.—The report was adopted- and the Clerk instructed to write to the overseers requesting the payment of their calls. Mr. Brandreth submitted the report and estimate of the probable balance in favour of or against each town- ship in the Union at the end of the current half-year ending Michaelmas, 1894; and the Amount of contribu- tion required from the overseers of the respective town- ships to meet the expenditure of the ensuring half-year, ending Lady Day, 1895. The basis of contributions required was calculated upon the rateable as per valua- tion lists in force. The total capital for the current half-year, ending Michaelmas, 1894, amounted to £9,981, and the nett expenditure estimated at £2,246. The probable expenditure during the ensuing half-year, end- ing Lady Day, 1895, was estimated: Common charges, £2,200; county rate, £3,403 ; and balance of sontingency fund, £3,853; total, £9,456. After deducting the prob- able balance, the amount required was £7,210. The rateable value of the Union as per valuation lists in force was £231,235, being an increase of £7,291, as compared with the corresponding period of last year. A rate of 74d. in the £ would produce £7,226, and it was recom- mended that a call for £7,210 be made upon the overseers, which was the same as that in the previous year. The calculations were subject to alterations which might be Made in valuation lists previous to the contribution orders being signed as there would probably be several changes with respect to the assessment of the Manchester Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>" TOBLCCOIPISTS commencing" 111 d. guide (2`.9 pages)3d, TonAccomeTs' Co.-186, Buston-load, London. manager. H. MYERS, Bet. 1866.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SWIMMING GALA AT WARRINGTON BATHS.</head>
        <p>SWIMMING GALA AT WARRINGTON BATHS.</p>
        <p>Another evidence of the popularity of Warring- ton Baths in swimming circles generally, was forth- coming when they were selected for the water polo match between the North and South of Eng- land, which took place last week at the Baths, Legh-st. Although this event was the mest important in the night's programme, a considerable amount of interest was centred in the water polo match between Warring- ton Swimming Club and Mark Addy Swimming Club. The programme also included a number of swimming handicaps for ladies and gentlemen, fa• which valuable prizes were given. There was a very large attendance, in fact, all the tickets which were issued were sold out by yesterday afternoon. Alderman Bolton,chairman of the Baths Committee was chairman of the proceedings. The following were the officials :—Judges, Messrs. J. Hunt, J. Grace, J.Beswick, and 11,,801t0n ; handicapper and starter, Mr. J. Derbyshire (Manchester) ; check starters, Messrs. F. Baxter (Manchester) and H. Tuft ; hon. secretary W.S.C., Mr. T. G. Grounds; hon. sec. to Gala Committee, Mr. Stanley Wilson. The first event was a two lengths race, confined to members of the Warrington Swimming Club. For this there were 34 entries, and they were swum off in seven heats. The following were successful in their heads :- Ist heat, A. Booth ; 2nd heat, A. Monks ; 3rd heat, E. A.Jones ; 4th heat, E. Whittaker ;sth heat, F.Turner ; 6th heat, F. Garside ; 7th heat, W. Creighton. Each heat was well contested, and showed careful handicap- ping. Final : Ist A. Booth, 2nd E. Whittaker, 3rd W. Creighton. The two lengths open handicap (50 Yds.) was next taken. For this there were no fewer than 87 entries, including several noted swimmers. There were seven- teen heats, the first of which was noticeable for the disqualification of a competitor named Parker, although he came in first, because he started before the word "go " was given. F. Jarside who was second was accordingly declared the winner of the heat. One of the spectators at this point had the misfortune to fall into the water, much to the merriment of the audience. Heat two was won by E. A. Jones. Warrington: 3rd heat, P. Ripley, St. Helens ; 4th, W. Hitchin, V.S.C., sth, W. Creigh- ton, Warrington, S.C. ; 6th, W.''H. Lester, 0.5.0 ; 7th F. Bailey, V.S.C., Bth, J: W. Shaw, 0 S.C., 9th, T. Rourke, S.S.C., 10th, A. Magnall, 8.5. C., 11th, T. A. Sutton, Warrington. There was quite an outburst of ap- plause when Tyers entered the baths to take part in the 11th heat. After a very exciting rice Sutton just man- aged to beat him. Tyers' time was 29 4 ssecs., or equal to his performance in the Scotch championship race on Wednesday. Heat 12 was won by J.-Molloy, L.S.C. The 13th was a "dead heat" between J. H. Derbyshire, 0.5. C., and J. Clare, W.S.C., 14th W. Burrows, St. Helens; 15, J. Coyle, S.S.C., 16th H. Greenhalgh, O.W.S.C. and 17th F. Cliff, N A.S.C. Semi-final results: Ist heat, P. Ripley, 2ad heat, F. Bailey, 3rd heat, A. Magnall, 4th heat, Derbyshire. Final : Ist, P. Ripley, St. Helens; 2nd, J. H. Darbyshire, Manchester ; 3rd, F. Bailey, V.S.C. ; 4th, J. Mangnall, B.S.C. The ladies' race (50 yards) which followed was of an interesting character. There were only twelve entries, eight of which were credited to St. Helens.. The winners of the three heats were Ist, Miss M. Baker, Warrington, 2nd, Miss E. Cash, St. Helens, and 3rd, Miss J. Pickles, Openshaw. Final : Ist, Miss Cash, 2nd, Miss M. Baker, 3rd, Miss Pickles. The event of the evening was, of course, the water polo match between North and South of England. The teams were as follows :—North Goal, E. Moss (Manchester Leaf-street S.C.) ; left back, R. A. Crawshaw (Manchester Mayfield S.C.) ; right back, T. Harris (Manchester Osborne -street S.C.) ; half- back, J. H. Tyers (Manchester 0.5. C.) ; left forward, R. Sharrock, (St. Helens S.C.) centre, J. Hunt (Manchester Mayfield, S.C.) right forward, J. H. Hicks (Manchester Osborne-street S.C.). South : Goal, G. W. Haarnack (Amateur S.C.), left back, J. H. Kipling (Otter S.C.), right back, R. H.Hughes tCricketers S.C.), half back, C. R. Cragg (Ranelagh Harriers S.C.), left forward, H. H. Stevens (Amateur S.C.), centre forward, A. W. Burghard. captain (Nautelus S.C.) ; right forward, E. J. Plumbridge (Tunbridge I Wells S. C.) The officials were : Referee: Mr. H. Thom- sett, Hon sec. M.C.A.S.A.; timekeeper: Mr. G. Pragnell, hon. sec.. A.g A.; goal scorer (North) : Mr. J. H. Fisher, V.P., N.C.A.S.A.; goal scorer (South) Mr. E. J. Fackley, hon. sec., S.C.A.S.A. The North during the firs*.; half defended the deep end of the bath. The game was somewhat even for some time, a few good shots by both teams being noticeable. Tyers, however, by a capital shot notched the first goal of the evening for the North team amidst a vociferous outburst of applause. The Southern men now played up, and suc- cess attended their efforts. for Plumbridge by capital play scored for them. The whistle soon afterwards sounded for half-time with the score—North 1 goal, South 1 goal. On the resumption of hoßtili- ties the Northern men were soon en evidence for soon after the commencement, the Northern men scored three goals in succession. 'Tyers contributing two and Sharrocks one. The remainder of the game was of a more even nature, neither side again scoring. On the sound of the whistle for" no-side " the result stood, North 4, South 1. The last event, was the polo contest between the Warrington and Mark Addy teams. The followinc, were the teams :—Warrington, goal, J. F. Dixon ; left back, W.D. Sutton; right back, S. Beaman ; ,half back, Fair Turner, captain; forwards, G.A.Scotson,T.Locker,P.Fur- ness. Mark Addy—Goal,G.W.Bannister; left back,John T. Rowan ; right back, F. Tilton; half-back. J. Baxter (captain) ; forwards, Jos. Rowan, A. PendleburY, J. Brown ; referee, Mr. F. Baxter (Manchester) ; time keeper. Mr. J. Hunt; goal scorer, Mr. Stanley Wilson. The Warrington team defended the three ft. and of the bath during the first half. The differences in the play of the two teams from the match previously witnessed was soon apparent. "Fouls" were a frequent quantity. Warrington were the first to score, Locker by a good shot doing the needful. The Mark Addy men were soon afterwards equalised the game by Baxter scoring. This was followed by another goal for the Mark Addy team. At half-time the score stood: Mark Addy 2, Warrington 1. The second half of the game was of a more exciting character, each side scoring two goals. Warrington were thus defeated by four goals to three. The Chairman presented the prizes' to the successful competitors in the swimming handicaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>DEATH OF THE REV. CANON ' HERON. THE FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF THE REV. CANON ' HERON. THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The remains of the deceased Canon- were laid to rest on Friday afternoon in the chancel of Daresbury church The time fixed for the funeral service was 12.30, but it was an hour later,before the hearse containing the coffin arrived from the deceased's house at Moore. The pos- session was hcaded by a body of the deceased's tenantry, consisting of the followisg .--Messrs. Philip Darbyshire, S. Basnett, J. S. Jones, J. Bradbury, Charles Roylance, John Highfield, William Oulton, John Friar. Frank de Prez, Charles de Prez, N. Bilshaw, G. Hicks. The cortege containing the coffin followed. The coffin was of panel- led fumed oak with brass mountings. This enclosed a lead and also a shell coffin. On an illurnina.ted'plate there was the following inscription "Rev George Heron, M.A., died August 27, 1894, aged 89 years." The following were the bearers: Messrs. H. Lawton, Isaac Lawton, Walter Darbyshire, Philip Darbyshire, jun., A. de Prez, and Thomas Ryder. The chief mourners occupied the first carriage, and were Mr. E. R. G. Hopwood, Major Hopwood and Mr. Price. Dr. White and Mr. J. D. Buckton occupied the second carriage. The third car- riage contained the executors, Messrs. Widdrington, Tatton, and J. R. Buckton. The remaining mourning coaches were occupied by the following friends . Mr. Neville, Mrs. Neville, Mr. C. E. Linaker, Rev. H. B. Blogg, Mr. Brooke, Canon Blackburne, Rev. W. Jones, Canon Penrhyn, Provost Meynell, Archdeacon Gore,and Mr. Wynne Jones. A halt was made at the top of the road leading to the church, when the coffin was lifted on to a bier and carried to the church by a number of the tenantry. They were met at the gates by the sur- pliced choir who headed the proce,sion into the church. There was a large attendance of clergy and local gentle- men. Amongst those present in addition to those already mentioned were: Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, Rev. F. Slater, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Rev. W. Smith, Rev. G. W. Gilbertson, Rev. E. D. Garvin, Rev. Morley Stevenson Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., Messrs. T. H. Lyon, J.P., W. C. Jones, J.P., Robert Garnett, J.P. H. Greenall, J. White, R. Johnson, G. Warburton, H. N. Houghton, T. S. Burgess, J. Swinton and A. Wilson. The burial service was con- ducted by the Dean of Chester and the Rev. T. Whitley. During the lowering of the coffin below the chancel there was impressive silence. The service was of short dura- tion. Only one hymn was sung in accordance with the deceased's expressed wish. He had also requested that no wreaths should be sent. The hymn sung was "0 God our help in ages past." A wreath of ivy was the only floral decoration on the coffin, and this was contri- buted by the gardener to the deceased. At the conclu- sion of the funeral a muffled peal was rung as a mark of respect.</p>
