      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. '</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS. '</p>
        <p>A MOTIONLESS FLIGHT—A flight of stairs. IT is never too late to learn ; but sometimes we finil nothing left to learn, except that it is too late. Gnocam : "What kind of tat did you ask for, madam?" Customer : "Five o'clock tea, please. I hear that is the most stylish now." A DANGEROUS MAN.—Visitor (in insane asylum) : " How long have you been inei 'e?" Inmate: " Ever since the people on the outside futt,.d out that I knew they were ciazy." "Oh," she cried, " if could only see myself as others see me !" "It wouldn't do," said he. " It. would make you too conceited." And then she smiled upon him all the rest of the evening. WHEN peeple are quarrelling out-of-doors, what almond they do ?—Coon-cide. Pura GOLD.—An enthusiastic admirer, speaking of a young beauty s fashiona le yellow hair, called it pure gold. "It ought to be," quail a wag ; "it looks like twenty-four carrots." MAMMA " When that boy threw stones at you why didn't you come and tell we. iusi cad of throw- ing them back ?" Little Son : " Tell you! Why, you couldu t hit -a barn door.' MRS. KLUBMAN : "Going away? You do not know how louesome it is here of an evening." Mr. Klulmeam : " Yes, do; that's the reason I'm going out. ' "I MEANT to have told you of that bole," said Mike to a fr:end, who had fallen into a pit in his garden. " matter," said Pat ; "I've hound iL' JONt.S.-110w are you succeeding. Mr. Horn: blowei', in your work? Rev. Mr. Hornblower.—. Magnificently. There's a great awakening at the close of every sermon ! AGNEs, "Really, Helen has improved in heri music wonderfully since she went abroad." Edith "In what way ? ' Agnes : " Why, she never plays anything now that sounds the least bit like a tune." MASTER Bona's father is the happy owner of a„ hatching in.chine. The other day, as little Bob, was watching a chicken energetically breaking its.. way through its shell, he remarked, "I see how he gets out ; but how did he get in ?" MASTER : " What's up with the horse, Billy ?". Billy: "Broke his hind leg, ina.ssa." Master: "How did he come to do that ?" Billy (rubbing, his head) : "lie done kick me on de head, massa."- AN ALlBL—Prisoner : "01 can prove an aliby,) yer honour." Magistrate " Where were you at the time the offence was committed?' Prisoner ? "Oi was calling on my friend, Mike Murphy, yer hoaour." Magistrate: "Very good. What was your business with Mr. Murphy?" Prisoner: "Sure Oi was after axin' him where Oi cud find a lawyer that cud clear me, yer honour." JCV AND SORRoW. —" What a contrast in those' two girls' faces—one 30 happy and one so sad !" "No wonder. One of them has been married three months; the other lost her Aland a week' before the day fixed for the wedding." "Indeed I And which is which ?" THE STUmPER STUMPED. Mrs. Sponter "Where are yoa going to-night, John ? Mr. Spouter : "I am due, my dear, to address thei Consolidated Cohorts of the Home Industries of Hohokus." Mrs. Spouter : "If you would stop your talking and do more working, several home industries fknow of would prosper better. Run; down, now, and la ing up the coal !" A LOVER, young and euthu iastic, who sang and played nearly two hours befor the house of his- lady-love one evening, was electrified—that shocked—after a short pause, by a cord al "Than you !" from the "other fel ow," who appeared at; the window. YOUNG- BRIDE. (pouting) : "We've only been married two days, Charlie, and you're scolding. me already !" Husband: "I know, my dear. But just think how long I've been waiting for the' chance A STRIKING PrrooF.—Professor (lecturing) : "In conclusion, I would instance mental aberration—a, a mania to which the learned are. freq.,ently sub- ject, and occasionally make themselves ridiculous without knowing it.' After which the professor., took, instead of his hat, the lamp-shade from off--' the bracket, put it on hii head and walked off. GENTLEMAN (on departing, to .hotel-keeper) : "I've been robbed while I've been Staying here." • Hotel-keeper : "Good -gracious, sir ! didn't you keep your door locked? Have you any idea who's-- been robbing you?" -" Rather ! You have ! Look at this bill !" - DbTRESSINC.—" My dearest, you must discharge that old servant who opens the door for Inc. He is growing more impudent every day,'' said a rich • and well-received admirer. " Indeed! I have - often thought of doing so already," was the reply. 17, " That's my father." THE 'HEIGHT OF INGRATITUDE. Seared . Individual (lodging infuriated bull behind a tree): "You ungrateful brute, you! You wouldn't toss a, consistent vegetarian who never ate beef in his life, would you? Is that the return you make ?" ONLY A STICK. —A man once boasted to Sydney. Smith that the stick he carried had been twice round the world. Smith took the stick, andri' after carefully examining it, said, "And yet—and- yet it is only a stick, after all." STOCKTAKING. —" nit not going to open shop at.* all to-morrow, because it's my annual stocktaking day," said a jeweller to his next-door neighbour ae few mornings ago. When, however, the morrow.,; came round, he discovered, to his intense sorrow, I that be had been anticipated over the stocktaking- by burglars during the night. ALACK-A-DAY !—'Twas in a breach of promise suit. The letters all wereread, And here is what the .opening words of each epistle said : "Dear - Mr. Smith, "Dear Friend,' " Dear !John," "eMaa.- darling four-leaf clover," "My 'ownest Jack," "Dear Sir," then "Sir,"—and all was over. POETRY, a waxed moustache, mystery, long hair and a sweet tenor voice, will often make a woman • feel as if there was only a sheet of tissue paper . between heaven and herself; but it is the man with the wart on his notc and six figures in his bank balance who scoops her in and makes her happy ever afterwards. A LITTLE innocent misunderstanding is some- times very needful in helping one over a hard 's place. "Mabel,' said the teacher, "you may spell kitten.' "K double i-t-e-n," said Mabel. "Kitten has two i-s, then?" • "Yes, ma'am. Our• kitten has." JORKINS "That dog of yours lies worried three of My lambs, and I want to know what you pro- pose to do about it." Pa.wkins : "Arc you sure it was my dog? Jorkins : "Positive. ' Paw-. - kins : " Well, l'd bettee sell him. You don't want to buy a dog, do you ?" Baows : " What a nuisance those beggarly tax- collectors are! If yeu can't pay them just when they want you to, they are always pestering you for it. Jones : " I know a man who is never pes- tered with the tax- ()Hector calling on him." Brown : "Oh, who is that ?" Jones : " Why, the the tax-collector himself, of course." - " WOK here, Jones,'' mid a rich fellow to his former schoolmate, " this is the second time you have applied to me this month. I am afraid you do not jive within your means." " Aim," said Jones, "I am trying to find the means to live within." A raw Scotch and English travellers having met together, an Englislonan took it upon him to ridicule the thistle, and exclaimed against the empty boast of its motto—" Nemo me impune lacessit '--when a Seotchman present observed, " The thistle, sir, is the pride of the Scottish nation, but it is nothing in the mouth of an ass !" VICTORIA! Brown, to Jones, who is going to do something his wife told him to (contemptuously): "I should like to be ruled by a woman "So you are, answers Jones. Brown, who prides himself on the fact that his wife never attempts to rule him (indiguantly): " Pin sure I'm not. "Yes, you are," asserts J- nes ; " and so is everyone else_ in this country. ' Brown sees the joke, and Jones goes off rejoicing. A NORTH of Ireland weekly paper advertises Itself thus : " During a late thunderstorm •a man crept for shelter into a hollow leg. The rain fell in torrents, causing the log to swell so that the malt was caught and firmly held within. Death stared him in the face, and the sins of hie past life rose imp before him in all their vividness. Suddenly he• remembered that he wasn't a subscriber to our paper, and he immediately felt so small that he was - able to back right out." A NERVOUS person said that he hated being called upon to make a speech at a public dinner, because 'getting on his legs always sent him off his head! The largest mammoth found in Siberia measured. seventeen feet long and ten feet in height. The tusks weighed five hundred and sixty pounds. The head without the tusks weighed four hundred and fourteen pounds.</p>
