      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>Ws° is the most popular man in Fame the world, or, rather, who is</p>
        <p>the man most written about? According to Mr. HENRY ROMEIKE, the head of an important press cutting agency, this particular honour belongs to the EMPEROR of GERMANY. It seems that people are never tired of reading about the energetic young Kaiser. He not only stands first but he is first by a long way—no com- petitor comes near him. At the present time Queen VICTORIA is probably second on the list,and' there is supposed to ho something like a tie for third place between the PRINCE of WALES and the CZAR of Russia. It will been seen therefore that at the present time the premier places are assigned to royal personages. But it has not always been so. Just before his resignation and when he held power as the head of the government of this coun- try, Mr. GLADSTONE probably held the second place in tkie list of most-written-about men. Go ing furthei back still to the time when PRINCE BISMARCK was in the zenith of his power the name of the redoubtable German CHANCELLOR was even more frequently mentioned in the world's press than that ot Mr. GLADSTONE. The press- cutting agent seems to have his finger on the world's pulse. He can quickly detect whether a public personage is rising or falling in general esteem. The reputations of some men—of politi- cians in particular—are very evanescent. This, it Iseems, applies more especially to, the United States. "In America a public matt may blaze a perfect sun in the newspaper firmament for a time, and then become completely eclipsed," and he rarely has another chance to shine. A press- cutter is often engaged to gather together the obituaries of notable men, and he then discovers that nothing but good is not always spoken of the dead. Mr. ROMEIKE was instructed to collect the obituary notices of the late JAY GOULD but after he had sent in 2,000 or 3,000 he was told not to send any more. The reason for this was that in almost every instance the character and career of the unscrupulous financier were severely condemned. On the other hand 3,000 clippings bearing upon the death of Mr. G. W. CHILD, the millionaire philan- thropist of Philadelphia,were compiled, and in not one of these references was there anything but eulogy of the departed. There are many ways of acltieving fame, but if we may judge by obituary notices the most famous of all modern mortals was the great showman, P. T. Barnum. Few men have been more talked about during their life- time than Mr. BARNUM, and at his death he beat all records. In his case no fewer than 15,000 obituary notices we re collected and these having been enshrined in three portly volumes are now in the museum of Bridgeport, Connecticut, as a gift from the widow.</p>
        <p>A Boating THE boating disaster in More- Disaster. cambe Bay has cast a gloom over the holiday season in this part of the country. Here was a party of pleasure seekers, chiefly from the Burnley district of Lancashire, suddenly upset by a single gust of wind in an otherwise calm day, and twenty-five out of a total of thirty-four persons were drowned in broad day-light, within a short distance of land, and with other boats quite near at hand. It is scarcely possible for death to came in a more unexpected or more tragical form, for in this case close relations— parents and children—and in one instance at least a whole family suddenly passed out of existence.. At the inquest an unsatisfactory state of affairs so far as the regulations for boating at Morecambe was revealed. It appears that although boats are registered there, practically no limitations are im- posed as to the number of persons. that may be taken on board. That is to say, as one of the jury- men remarked, while a cabman is precluded front taking beyond a specified number of "fares," a boatman is left to his own discretion with regard to the number of pleasure seekers he admits into his vessel. And, besidesthis, no provision is made in the shape of belts and other life-saving appara- tus by the aid of which persons suddenly thrown intothe water might sustain themselves for a few minutes, which is often all the time that is needed to give the rescuers a chance.</p>
        <p>SCIENCE has come to the aid of the Identifying detective in many ways and not Criminals. least in the work of identifying criminals. An ingenious malefactor of the Peace type can in a very short time make a pretty com- plete change in his outward appearance, but there are some things he can not do. He can not, for instance, alter the dimensions of the bones of his body. The Bertillon system for the identification of criminals, which the Home Secretary has now decided shall be adopted in England, is, as it has been stated, based upon two postulates. The first</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>is, that d ivers bony parts of the adult human frame do no alter with age, accident, or device; and the second is, that these bony parts can be easily measured with absolute exactness. The measurements that are taken are those of the skull, the middle and little finger of the left hand; the length of the left forearm and foot, and the full stretch of the arms, It is said that since this system of classifying criminals was adopted in Paris nearly three quarters of a million persons have been operated upon and no two individuals have been found to be exactly the same in ell particu;ars.</p>
        <p>THE report that tho Lancashire The Coal Trade. col- Coal Sales Association has lapsed is not without interest to the public. This Association was formed after the coal strike of last year in order to check the unnecessary and ruin- ous competition among coal owners, and therefore its existence has not been a prolonged one. The reason given for its collapse is suggestive. amounts to this, that some of the coalowners hoe acted faithlessly towards the combination they had voluntarily entered into. They have accepted prices below the rates which had been officially fixed, and thus taken a moan advantage of their colleagues of the Association. Under these air' cumstances the dissolutioa of the Association vra° inevitable. It is now much to be feared that the coalowners will again enter upon the reckless and baneful system of competition which was after all has been said the cause of the disastrous strike of last year.</p>
        <p>NEWTON BOARD OF COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>--- The fortnightly meeting of the Newton Board of Improvement Commissioners was held at the Town Hall, Barlestown. on blonday afternoon. There were present Messrs. J. W. Emmett (chair" man), T. Ormand, G. Ratcliffe' H. W. Collingwoodt P.. McMahon, P. Thompson, T. Forster, G. E. wors- ley (law clerk), and R. Brierley (clerk and sur veyor).</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS, &amp;C., CODIMITTRIS. At a meeting of this committee held on Augurt 20, the Surveyor submitted agreements with Mr. R. Alderson and Mr. Wm. Lougton for deposit of nightsoil on their farms for one year, which were approved. A letter dated Ist inst. was read from Messrs. Walton and Co. Resolved, that they be informed the cause of complains in Crow-lane at Chamber's farm has been removed some time ago. It was also resolved that Mr. T. A. Pimblett be paid £2 a year for winding regularly the Tow° Hall clock. Plans for 1.2 cottages in Athot-st., for Afr- J. C. Walton and Mrs. H. Walton, and the alterationS at the Wesleyan schools were submitted and approved... Mr. H. Waterworth's plan for 12 cottages on iffeww' Common was again put back to be considered by the new committee as to common rights.—The minuteS were passed on the motion of Mr. Ratcliffe, seconded bY Mr. Thompson.</p>
        <p>GAS AND WATETI COMMITTEE. At a meeting of this committee held on August Mb, the gas manager's report was read, and it was ordered that quotations for the purchase of gas tar under co°' tract tor the ensuing year be obtained. The inspector report on No. 1 boiler was read, which Mr. Swanl stated had received attention. The carbonizing accoun" for the past month was read, giving fuel used li4.7:tong• gas made 1,483,700,feet, being an average of 10,090164,_ per ton of fuel used. It was ordered that Messrs. Mu; ton and Co. be informed that their claims for interes' cannot be entertained. The committee, however,. rtf" commend that all consumers of gas who are properti owners in the district be refunded their deposits ;. it' further ordered that the price of gas coke at the worigAs be reduced to Ss. 44. per ton. Mr. Brierley reporteu the sub-committee had accepted the tenders of Meter! Slee and Co. at DIS for new tar pump and repair&amp; engines, and the Vulcan Foundry Co. for new Mr cistern 39 10s. It was resolved that a water meter fixed for the supply of water to the gas works- sin water charged for at cost price.—In reply to quest lOj Mr. Brierley said that the amount to be refunded. vrot3 , be £250. The motion was passed on the motion °A Mr. McMahon..</p>
        <p>FINANCE COMMITTEE. At a meeting of the Finance Committee cheques passed for payment amounting to 210571%. Ormand in proposing the adoption of the minutes orr,, committee said that at the last meeting of the conarnitr,, objection was taken to several items in the law elersi; account. The matter had been adjourned, and be discussed discussed later on. MEDICAL OFFICER OP REALM The Clerk announced that a letter had bees from the Local Government Board confirming the rew pointment of Dr. Watkins as medical officer or health. • ALTERATION OP ROAD. A letter was received from Messrs. Greenali andßeeig; ton, on behalf of Messrs. Richard Evans and ConsFal'L for permission to alter a road from Crow-lane to aste' , lane, Haydock.—The necessary permission was given. TRIG WARDS QUESTION AGAIN. Mr. Emmett said that he had received the follostrhi letter from Mr. R. Stone, "Dear Sir, I have received from Messrs. Pearson and Ellis, the bill of thecosts2.°re the preparation of the memorial to the Lanestitc, County Council for an order to divide the township r" wards. The Commissioners have already agreed tot'', a portion of the costs incurred, but my committee that as the Commissioners adopted the ratepayer's geetions, it is not unreasonable that the whole of the V: penses should be included in the townships); scent% Trusting that this opinion would be in bartnotlY the-views of your Board, I am, yours truly, Itew— Stone.!'' Mr. Ratcliffe I think they have a good cheek. trbel would not take time to bring the thing about, as it "ft, bound to do of itself. I don't think it right that '- should pay one penny piece. Mr. Mehfalum : I object to anything being Paid fc)r the agitation of one or two ratepayers. the Mr. Emmett said that a deputation waited upon' Board some time ago, and the question was thoro4l;.. gone into. A bargain was then made that the CO stoners agree to pay £55 ot the expenses which had IN', Incurred. He was quite with Mr. Ratcliffe that if !Phi committee had waited a little, the expense wolsid_ have been incurred. At the meeting, the Coinmissios-0 gave the matter every consideration, and he there thought that they should carry out the bargain entelv- into at the time. t sif• 'Mr. McMahen : We ought not to pay anything We have our own law clerk, and we pay for his el:ei vices. /am altogether opposed to spenaing the of the ratepayers for the folly of a few ratepayers P:ot hag a meeting, I do not know where. I objected W " the first. Emmett : But we must carry out our hsrer Proceeding, Mr. Emmett said that Mr. Stone was Tolt taken. They did not adopt the Committee's schertie!,eo a considerable modification of it. The Conunn..) wanted four wards, while they (the Commissioneiri„, wanted five which was ultimately agreed to. They tlas. , fore agreed to pay part of the expenses, equal to This was agreed to by four votes to two. vt,,srat Mr. McMahon said that there was not a full .e•-•_„,,* and therefore they ought not have agreed to Ply-- thing. meta • Mr. Forster said that he did not see why the should not pay the whole of the expenses, as it vr'—it matter which affected the whole of the townshiP• was therefore only fair that the township should Pel whole of the bill. Idll6 Mr. Emmett said that he considered that it well ar best to adhere to the original resolution, and he posed a resolution to that effect. Mr. Thompson seconded, and the resolution" ej..,g. passed, only Messrs. Ratcliffe and McMahon diStien' RESIGNATION OF COLONEL MCCORQUODALS,„ ._.• ratiCP The Clerk then read a letter from Colonel Mcvv::ioe dale to Mr. Emmett, stating that he regretted do attention to the duties of the Commissioners. Pie 05 • not think ha could longer remain upon the Boarv!.„• therefore placed his resignation in the Chaks hands, and left it to the Board whether it shoulu go effect then or wait until the November election:o tee thanked the Commissioners for their indulgence past. ceileos Mr. Emmett said he was very sorry to have re Ade the letter. Colonel. McCorquodale was - member of the Bean], and ever since ha (Mr. r:,ei,earS) had been a member of the Board (about 26 or 21 We. Colonel McCorquodale had rendered valuanle ser „Atli He moved that he be asked to remain a member November next. Mr. G. Ratcliffe seconded. Several other members spoke in a similar strsish the resolution was passed unanimously. This concluded the business of the meeting.</p>
        <p>FIRE IN A CHIP POTATO SHOP. 1, clir On Friday night, a fire which might have astrous results, but for the promptitude with it was extinguished, occurred in a chip potato 0 vo. occupied by Mr. Pilling, Market-street, Earlest°,oes. Mr. Pining was engaged preparing the Potti,:ted. when be allowed the stove to become overlie.; oP and the fat igniting caused the flames to shond to in an alarmiag manner. Pilling endeavotirehuto extinguish them with water and was sligbrlY:aveet in consequence. P.C. Burns lent some assis"-suo' and with the aid of some damp sacks tlleY telf ceeded in getting the flames under. Fortuos to beyond scorching the ceiling, and destroying machine, no other damage was done.</p>
