        <p>ORifIATION or A LAWN TENNIS CLUB AT RUNCORN. toAt a recent meeting in Trinity School it was decided 4„l°rna a lawn tennis club for Runcorn, Miss A. Bate Dr. Annett being selected as treasurers. Misses ler, Carr, and Bankes. Messrs. Taylor, Rhodes, and tackle tie Y were appointed a committee with power to gotiate for a ground. On the completion of all pre- fitliaary arrangements, the members will prepare them. es for next sewon. A TIM WORK OF Tuu RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE. 4ccording to the September issue of the" Runcorn .Larish Magazine " the district nurse states in her report •'tlat from July 25 to August 25 she has visited ten LA_rolllo and twenty-four medical cases. "Of these one Z4l died, twelve have been taken off the books, and ,ne are fresh cases ; leaving twenty-one cases on the Tweety-thr( e patients have received occasional .five of these have been taken off the books, leaving During the month 340 visits had been paid une nurse. SMART SENTENCE ON WIDNES COAL THIEVES. a 8 o,Monday. at the Widnes Police Court, before Mr. T. Ilfltfls and Councillor W. W. Gossage, two young 741121 en named Catherine Duffy, Sankey-street, and "a Lea, Wellington-street, pleaded guilty to stealing aOtlantity of coal from the' railway siding at the Widnes b_belc, the property of the London and North-Western .r,ilway Company, on Saturday last. They had both nri convicted before the coal stealing, and they were 17Ln committed to gaol for one month with hard RUNCORN CYCLING CLUB. eye 'wash good muster on Wednesday afternoon and tZart was promptly made from Devonshire Square on a iZto Chester via Frodsham and Helsby. The roads 47,in good condition and the spin was enjoyed. On .11", rig at the ancient city, the wheelmen put up their f:.us at the Liver Hotel, and partook laf an excellent .„ Subsequently they stsolled at leisure round the i'LlB and then had a sail on the river Dee. The home- run was very pleasant and completed quite safely '4l in good time. COLLIEVS BREAD is the best money can buy.—Advt. ST. MICHAEL'S SUNDAY SCHOOL, _RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>i„lteferri-n.g-to the girls' departmeut of this school, the 41:1corn Parish Magazine says that a change has taken Ih.te in the superintendency. " Miss Ainsworth, who been connected with our Sunday school work for ;;;12.11e time, and to whom our best thanks are due for the h7l, larity and interest with which those services have rendered, has been succeeded by Mrs. Branch, 1.09, agi'ovilwaV Meadows. Let us pray that her work gs t us may be blessed and made easy, by parents 4471ng their children to school regularly and punctually, ; by teachers and scholars alike giving to Mrs. —4geh a very hearty welcome and every assistance they</p>
        <p>• CHE.SHIRE COUNTY COUNCIL SCHOLARSHIP. 'Co"8 Technical Instruction Committee of the Cheshire araltY Council at their meeting on Friday evening last .7`,l‘ awarded scholarships to the following Runcorn ItZents : Science : Walter Wiswall, engineer's pulpit; Ci°lPh E. Weismuller, chemist ;William Ellis. joiner; Alreld Davies, gas engineer ; B. Crosby, schoolmaster. ,ZL • George Ernest Moss, clerk. These scholarships 2er the fees charged at the institute selected, and rail- fares to and from the classes providing that the of the fees and fares does not exceed £5. The Zrarnittee have considered various applications for L`tats to classes and have decided to give Runcorn £233; ..‘44 FrOdShaM £7O. eatiam's is. BUTTER is the gem in the market .—Advt. PARAFFIN LAMP OUTRAOR AT WIDNES. On Monday morning, at the Widnes Police Court, is'pre Mr. T. 8. Timnais and Councillor W. W. Gossage, tnarried woman named Catherine M`Mullin, who teirled a young child in her arms, was brought up on I,,,tiland charged with unlawfully wounding a single tia'144,13 named Julia Maud M‘Kay, on the 25th ult., by krrawing a naraffin lamp at her. and setting her on fire. 'T. W. Knowles now appeared for the defence, and 414 h • , e did not object to Superintendent Keighley's 7,,rptication for another remand. Mr. Lewin, deputy :;,4gistrates' clerk (Dr. Hutchinson) : Will the injured th,c)4lan be able to appear in a week ?—Dr. Hutchinson: 01 I not for two weeks at the least.—The prisoner was Lti.en, remanded for seven days.—Superintendent Keigh- -7 In answer to Mr. Knowles said he objected to bail, ad the prisoner was remanded in custody. eitUaleil BLACK BIANDE.D TEA.. 2ka. per quarter lb.—Advt. WEDDING AT RUNCORN. e marriage of Miss Maggie Outhwaite, eldest 4:lChter of Mr. T. Outhwaite, Runcorn, to Mr. W. G. to'cason, was solemnised on Monday morning in Halton- Wesleyan Church, Runcorn in the presence of a t,....tnerous congregation. The bride who was very -47efully attired was attended by three bridesmaids, tjz,,wy, Misses Outhwaite (sister), Mellor (cousin of the Nroom) and Hayes. The best man was Mr. A. .0.,"31011. and the groomsmen, Messrs. Win. and Robert '4 th4aWaite, The Rev. J. Pulford Williams conducted ja-oe ceremony, after which the party repaired to the 14;18? of the bride's father and partook of an excellent 11,;4K1Ust. Subsequently, the newly-married pair left 4,fccorn for Barmouth where the honeymoon is to be Numerous costly presents have been received. PAINFI7LLLY SUDDEN DEATH or A WOMAN AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN. 14„re• Williamson, wife of Mr. John Williamson, black- :44l, or Irwell-lane, Runcorn, expired under painfully Jen circumstances on Wednesday night. The de. Q'4 woman, who at the time of her death was about Years of age, had been ailing somewhat slightly for /47,: trionths past, but was to all appearances in corn- tliZtively good health on Wednesday.' She was a wit- the marriage at Bethesda Church that day of ZBPeakrnan and Mr. Chadwick ; and went out again 14,04,2 evening to do some shopping. She returned borne tro-,-,"%e•ven o'clock. Subsequently she fell to the in a fit, and before medical aid , arrived she tik,Z..`ned her last. Her medical adviser attributed the 407 of death to apoplexy ; consequently there was no ,:leeessity for a ccroner's inquiry. Itacen_.-zen's PRasH MAE DAIRT Ma-rim, led. per lb. is "wit.— Advt. 0 MARRIAGE OF A RUNCORNIAN IN MANCHESTER. I:l4.:3_,Tuesday the marriage tpok place at St. James' WZ'in ht Church, Moss Side, Manchester, of Mr. Harry ltt`A, Younger son of Mr. Robert Wright, Runcorn, Lonfieei Emily Hoffman, younger daughter of Mr. John ki 51,01 th Hoffman, Princess-road, Alexandra Park, wpiechester. The bride was attended by Misses Susie D. bridtt (sister of the bridegroom) and Staton (Leeds), as PoDir,stnaids. She was attired in a dress of electric blue Shot with pale gold, and trimmed with electric ifoiftld cream guipure with hat to match. Mr. Arthur givehillan acted as best man. At the altar the bride was Viii,4,4waY by her father. Subsequent to the ceremony conducted by the Rev. S. B. Ainley, the wed- ittleirir,Y were entertained to breakfast at the house of 4eft f e's Parents. In the afternoon the happy pair c'e London where the honeymoon is being spent. 1 tee Y elvedvaluable and useful presents have been , Outz. , ''re. lens sell everything for the smallest possible proit.—</p>
        <p>Tla„ FUNERAL OF MRS. RAYNER, OF RUNCORN'. 404e,;i, tsuneral of the late Mrs. Rayner, wife of Mr. Itho7; itayrier' of "The Elms," Norman-road, Runcorn, lues,,—ed early on Saturday morning took place on loacC,a27 afternoon at the Runcorn Cemetery ,Greenway- Th, 'Am the presence of numerous relatives and friends. takihmeoeased lady was forty-two years of age, and since 430 11_,P her residence in Runcorn some twelve years ulait-,!`l associated herself with the Ellesmere-street Aorf,t! Methodist Free Church, where she was a constant ath,'"lPPer. She had always been a willing helper in tqdßaed cause which the church undertook, and had tete: a large circle of friends who held her in the highest taitt.Tl- The Rev. J. Stafford, the new pastor of the rree Methodist Church, conducted the burial "a a most impressive manner, and delivered a trib„-F address. There was a large number of floral titte;:es among which were one from the Ellesmere- sewing clas, another from Mr. and Miss Rayner 24184• -",,ain,t_d and daughter of the deceased), and one from *10.14-1-o'ssell the best quality goods at lowest possible. ANN,. -.ERSARY OF ST. JOIN'S PRESBYTERIAN SUNDAY It SCHOOL, RUNCORN. thetsvill be seen by referring to our advertising column torn /lithe ahhiversary of the above Sunday School will be of --eneed on Sunday next when the Rev. John Pugh, tiareifc u will preach. ,It is probably known to many 44 eernians that the Rev. J. Pugh is the President of the Retit7rWard movement "in Wales. To this work the rev. '.lhen had devoted perhaps the major portion of his Is, and the success with which it has been attended „a.great measure, owing to his untiring exertions. 4.111Dr-u3ect of the movement is to reach and spiritually ove those persons who would little think of attend- leuet Place of worship. The means by which this is the establishing of small missions, the members latoerl.ch visit personally the people above-mentioned to Mlle 4'llh to them the Word of God. It is thought prob- -2f th, at the Rev.Mr.Pugh will allude to the good results " Work, as well as the methods by which success has leileotieaped. The services will be held in the New Chapel spec, Victoria-road both morning and evening when hYrons and anthems will be rendered. In the tervc°ol3 the scholars and choristers will interpret a tiri,..ce of song entitled " Coals of Fire." The prospective the `.lltiers have been well trained for the occasion, and Doielvent promises to be very successful from a musical of view. The valuable work which the school is tibegg on should induce worshippers to contribute as 'LAY as their purses will allow.</p>
        <p>THE SLEEPING-OUT NUISANCE AT RUNCORN. At the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday morning, before Mr. S. Taylor, George• Holt was sentenced to seven days' imprisonment for sleeping out at 2.30 that morning in an unoccupied house in Pennington' s Row, Runcorn. —Prisoner had previously been convicted of the same offence, and also for neglecting his family and assaulting his wife.</p>
        <p>MORE SUCCESSES OF MR. S. B. CHADWICK, OF RUNCORN. This well-known breeder of horses pulled off five prizes at the Warrington Horse Show on Saturday last. A premium for a mare without foal, stinted to a regis- tered shire horse, a second prize for a mare with foal at foot by any registered shire horse; a third for a foal ; another second fora yearling filly; and another premium for a pair of draught horses shown in gear, but the animal only to be judged.</p>
        <p>HA.YDOCIC LOCAL BOARD. The monthly meeting of the Haydock Local Board on Thursday further considered the clerkship rendered vacant by the resignation of Mr. John Matshews, it being understood that a final selection would be made. Thirty-six candidates had applied, but this number had been reduced to four. The meeting was adjourned until Monday next. It is, however, understood that Mr. C. D. Thompson, sur- veyor, of Warrington, has been appointed, and that his appointment will be ratified at the adjourned meeting.</p>
