        <head>CASE OP RABIES.</head>
        <p>CASE OP RABIES.</p>
        <p>A communication has been received by the War- rington police from the Police Superintendent at Widnes, stating that they seized a dog in Widnes on Saturday evening. The following morning it was shot, as it showed very strong symptoms of rabies. A post-mortem examination • was made on Monday, which showed that the animal suffered from rabies. The dog had been traced as having travelled through Penketh, where it had bitten another dog, and had also bitten several in Widnes, It was a cross-bred bull and retriever dog, black, with white mark on the breast and tips of both hind feet. It weighed about 501bs. and was a very ferocious looking animal. It is thought that the animal belonged to some person in Warrington. LOCAL RESULTS OF THE TRAINING COLLEGES EXAMINATION. The class list of candidates examined in religious knowledge, for admission into Church Training Colleges, held July 3, 1894, has just been issued. The following are the successful local male candi- dates: Class 1, Harold B. Bate,Heath-side Warring- ton. Class ll.—James Armstrong, Blue Coat, War- rington; Edward Smith, Windle, St. Helens; William Smith, Hamilton-st., Warrington. Class II l.—Arthur Helsby, British Schools, St. Helens. The following are the female candidates who have, been suc- cessful Class I.—Edith Alison Barlow, Mount, War- rington; Margaret Ellen Richardson, Hamilton- street, Warrington; Gertrude Foster, Heathside Warrington. Class ll.—Alice Turner, Ladies' School of Industry, Warrington, Class Ill.—Ellen Maude Wilkinson, Church of England, Frodsham ; Alice Morrell, St. Peter's Warrington; Gertrude Martha Andrews, Frodsham ; Catherine M. Gorse, Christ Church, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>SERIOUS ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS ACCIDENT AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An accident of rather a serious nature occurred on Monday afternoon on tip Ship Canal at Runcorn. At about one o'clock the pabsenger steamer Eagle, belonging to the Ship Canal Passenger Company, Limited, laden with nearly 200 passengers for Manchester, was proceed- ing up the waterway under the captaincy of Wm. Gillis. On reaching the sluices, owing to the strong tide that was running; combined with a slight breeze, the vessel was driven to the right side of the canal and struck the butress of the swing bridge broadside. Her starboard paddle-box was smashed to atoms, the wheel injured,and the engines slightly strained. The occurrence threw many of the passengers into a high state of excitement, and some little confusion ensued. Calm was soon restored. however, and the steamer was subsequently moored to the left side of the canal beyond the bridge; the auth- orities at Manchester being communicated with in order that a steamer might be sent to take the passengers on to their destination. Several of the passehgers were forced to go to the Runcorn Railway Station from whence they continued their journey by train. A young man from Leeds had a narrow escape. Flo was standing near to the paddle-box when the collision happened, and for some time he was in a very disagreeable position by the timber falling around him. As luck would have it,how- ever, he was left uninjured and managed to scramble out of the wreckage quite safely. Shortly after five o'clock the "Manx Fairy" arrived and took the patient passen- gers on board and conveyed them to Manchester. The injured steame: after being temporarily righted started for Manchester lit about nine o'clock the same night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>TRADE UNIONS CONGRESS.</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONS CONGRESS.</p>
        <p>The Trade Unions Congress was opened on Monday at Norwich. The delegates, representing trade societies in all parts of the United Kingdom, numbered 380. The Mayor gave them a cordial welcome to the city. Mr. John Burns, M.P., chairman of the Parliamentary Com- mittee, said that although the number of delegates was smaller than at the Belfast Congress last year, its repre- sentative character was well sustained. At least a hundred of those present were town councillors.members of school boards, magistrates or members of Parliament. He hoped their deliberations would be productive of good, and would lead to the development of the labour move- ment in all its branches. Mr. Delves, president of the Norwich and District Trades and Labour Council, was unanimously elected president of the Congress. Messrs. Hsslam (Derbyshire), Threlfall (Southport), W. J.Davies (Birmingham), Mullin (Manchester), and Wilkie (New- castle), were appointed a Standing, Orders Committee. The report of the Parliamentary Committee was read by Mr. Fenwick. M.P. It dwelt on the necessity for reform whereby measures designed for the benefit of the work- ing classes should not be lest through the caprice of an arbitrary and irresponsible Upper Chamber which sought to override the wishes of the people's rapresentatives. The issue, the committee stated, Was one for the con- stituencies, and would ultimately be decided by the ' workers, and not by the House of Lords. • The Congress was resumed at Norwich on Tuesday, when the President (Mr. Delves) delivered his address. ' He was of opinion that the only direction in which an ultimate solution of the industrial problem could be looked for was that of collectivism. Tauching on poli- tical action, he said that election contests for the sake of propaganda might be good, but if in any case the result was to let in a man who would vote against employers' liability and the rest of the labour programme it was bad. Let them preserve their independence, but do not let them retard progress t* gratify a whim, or to register at the at the bottom of the poll a pious opinion which wise alliances might have converted into an Act af Parliament. The delegates afterwards proceeded to ' consider the Parliamentary Committee's report,and there was some exciting debate when the clause was reached which dealt with the Miners' Eight Hours Bill. By a very large majority the Congress reaffirmed the principle of and eight-hour day for miners. At Wednesday's sitting of the Congress a message front the International Peace Congress now sitting at Ant- werp, trusting that a place would be reserved in the programmes of future Congresses for the question of international arbitration, was received with applause. On the motion of Mr. Fenwick. M.P., a resolution was passed calling for a reform of Parliamentary procedure in the sense of putting down obstruction. His view was that the power of the closure should be transferred from the Speaker to the Government of the day. Resolu- tions were afterwards passed on a great variety of topics. The Congress affirmed the principle of payment j of members', of Parliament and also payment of erymen. A motion being made urging the Parliamentary Com- mittee to introduce a bill in Parliament firing the hours of work in bakehouses at eight per day, or 48 tarurs per week, Mr. Ben Tillett moved the addition of words which made the resolution embrace not only bakehouses but" all other trades and occupations." This addition to the resolution being accepted by the mover and seconder, it was put to the meeting and carried by 256 against five votes amidst loud cheers. In the course of the day it was reported that the number of societies represented in the Congress was 179, the number of .delegates was 378, and the number of workers repre- sented 1,100,000. At the Congress on Thursday, on the motion of a Nor- folk delegate, the Parliamentary Committee were in- structed to prepare a bill giving agricultural labourers security of tenure by modifying the Irish Labourers' Cottage Act so as to make it applicable to the whole of Britain. A resolution was adopted calling for an amend- ment of the Factory and Workshop Acts and the appoint- ment of additional male and female inspectors. Mr.J.J. Budge, a Manchester delegate, moved a resolution declaring it essential to the maintenance of British industries to nationalise the land and also mines, minearls and royalty rents. This was seconded by Mr. J. H. Walker, also of Manchester. Mr. Keir Hardie, M.P., moved an amendment upon which there was a lang and interesting discussion—namelv that the words " mines, minerals, and royalty rents," which appeared in the resolution, should be omitted, and the followinc substituted—" and the whole means of production, dis- tribution, and exchanee." The amendment which in plain terms, was a declaration for Socialism—was sup- ported by, amongst others, Mr. Tom Mann, Mr. John Burnes, and Mr. J. H. Wilson, M.P., and, meeting with but feeble opposition,it was carried by 219 votes against 61. A delegate from the American Labour Federation conveyed to the Congress a message of sympathy from 800,000 workers in the United States, and the Congress decided to send a delegate to the Congress to be held at Denver in December next. .4_</p>
        <p>The Legislative Assembly of New South Wales on Thursday passed an Eight Hours Bill for miners. The Independent Labour party at Birkenhead have decided to ask Mr. Tom Mann to contest the seat in their interest.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>Tue FRODSHAM PARISH COUNCIL.—At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority held on Tuesday afternoon, at .the Dutton Workhouse, the Clerk (Mr. Ashton) read the minutos of the previous meeting of the Frodsham Parochial Committee.—The minutes showed that the Clerk had formally reported that the Local Government Board inquiry had been held the day previous, and submitted the scale of water charges which•would be made to consumers in accord- ance with the request of the Board's Inspector. Mr. Charles E. Linaker stated that the five lamp holes had been converted into manholes, RS ordered, at a cost of £36 95., and produced the several accounts, which were passed for payment. Mr. Ashton had intimated that 14 members would be required for the Frodsham Parish Council.amd 12 for Frodsham Lordship. The water sub- committee's report on the condition of the water service pipes was verbally given by Mr. P. Riley.—The minutes were confirmed by the Authority.</p>
        <p>WIDNES Sr. MARY'S Y. R-lINCORN RECS. —St. Mary's jo.irmyed to Runcorn on Saturday to open their season in:an encounter with theßecs. Two or three of the picked team did not turn up, but substitutes were easily found from the numerous members of the club. O'Connor started the game for Widnes. Play was very even for some time until Butterworth obtained the ball, and doubling round scored at the corner. This reverse caused the St. Mary's to brighten up, and they playrd much better, on one occasion appearing to score, but the re- feree did not allow the point. The score remained un- altered at half time. During the second half, the Rees, whose forwards were playing well well together. obtained two more tries, the final score reading: Recs., 3 tries : St. Mary's, nil. Although de- feated, St. Mary's thowed that with a little practice the team may become one to uphold the reputation once I held by that organisation.</p>
