 acting the part of organist. their opinion about it, and adding to the committee if they so chose. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Egan said if he understood Mr. Stuart correctly he wanted Mr. Singleton to define in his resolution the duties of the committee, but he understood Mr. Single- ton wanted the committee to look after the interests of the ward generally. He thought it was quite unneces- sary for any hard words to be used to their opponents. They objected to Earlestown or Newton building a post office at the ratepayers' expense at either end of the township. So far as he underste ,d it, the committee were quite prepared to agree to the building of a central office for both ends of the township, but they were opposed to one end of the township being made up at the expense of the other. (Hear. hear.) The Chairman quite agreed with all that was being said.but seeing that the question had now been deferred, he did not see that it was necessary to go into the details again. The resolution proposed by Mr. Singleton and seconded by Mr. E. Fairclough was then put to the meeting as follows :—" That this meeting thank the committee, consisting of Messrs. Stone, Wood, Clarke, Barton,Syme, Stuart, Liptrott, Sankey, Egan, Simpson, Adams, Pen- nington and Wilkinson for their Services in connection with thewards and postal questions, and re-elect them, with the addition of any other ratepayer, whom they may propose to look after the interests of Newton ward at the election in November." This was unanimously carried, and the following names were added to the Committee, Messrs. Ritchie, E. Fairclough, Crouchley, Hall and Bodmer. The Chairman said the object of that Committee would be to discuss among themselves, and approach gentlemen whoinhey thought would be eligible to stand as ca.ndidatefor the Newton Ward. If they liked to think it over, and suggest a few names, or leave it to the Committee, he hoped they would say so. Alter some discussion Mr. R. B arton proposed, and Mr. Egan seconded, the following resolution ;—" That</p>
        <p>the Ratepayers' Committee be authorised to suggest names for the membership of the ward, which names shall be submitted to a future meeting of ratepayers for final selection."—This was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stuart said they had disbanded one Committee and formed another,and he as a member of the disbanded Committee must ask that meeting to support him in his proposition that they accord to their Chairman a very hearty vote of thanks for what he had done in connec- tion with the Committee up to the present ; because it was a direct reply to what had been suggested by people who chose to call therrs-lves their opponents that they were a self constituted Committee, and he wish to throw tack the slander that they could be a self-constituted Committee when they had Nit.. Robert Stone at their head. He thought it was absolutely essential that that meeting of ratepayers in Newton should agree with him that they had at the head of their affairs, a gentleman. (Applause,) Mr. R. Barton seconded the motion, and said the chair- man's services in regard tv the wards question were simply invaluable, and he firmly believed that it was through his exertions that they were so successful. It was said that the Commissioners did all they could for Newton and looked after Newton interests ; he must say distinctly that was not correct. If they read the account of the deputation which waited upon Mr. Rich on the 9th of June, it was all Earlestown, Newton was nowhere. One of the Commissioners had said that the deputation had got the extension of time from eight to nine o'clock for Newton, as well as Earlestovrn,but lie would say that the extension of tirne at Newton took place on the 7th of May, and the deputation to Mr. Rich was on the 9th of June, so the extension of time was obtained a month be- fore the deputation waited upon him. (Hear, hear.) The vote was heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Stone ha;ing suitably replied, the meeting dis- persed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>RUNCORN IMPROVEMENT COMMISSIONERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN IMPROVEMENT COMMISSIONERS.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above Board was held in the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Thursday morn- ing, Mr. Samuel Taylor, J.P., presiding. There were also present—Messrs. J. W. Lightburn, J. Pritchard, Daniel Bisbrown, Thomas Lea, William Leach, Wm. Hankinson, E. inaison, W. Collier, John Meadow- croft, F. Wiswall, F. A. Lake, law clerk; E. Marshall, secretary; and Mr. Barker, surveyor.</p>
        <p>REPORT OF THE MEDICAL OFFICER. The Socretary read the report of Dr. McDougall for the past month which stated that the number of deaths registered was 2.9, inclusive of two inquest cases. These produced a death-rate of 17.4 per thousand of the esti- mated population ; and of 162 exclusive of the inquest cases. Eleven of 13 deaths which bad resulted from diarrheas were infants under 12 months old ; and the re- maining three were infants between the ages of one and two years.—Mr. Lightburn considered the report to be a very encouraging one.—Mr. Bisbrown thought the num- ber of deaths below the average.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>HIGHWAYS AND BUILDINGS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>_ The following recommendations were made by this committee :—That Mr. James Wilding be appointed assistant surveyor for a further term of one year, at a salary after the rate of £l5O a year; that the year date from the Ist October next ; and that he devote his time principally to the superintendence of the making of private streets and to the work incidental thereto : that the application of Mr. John Sefton to have the time specified in the contract for the completion of the private street work in Bold-street and adjoining streets extended by one month be granted; that the tender of Mr. Thomas Norman for the supply of 6-inch, 9-inch, and 12-inch glazed earthenware socket pipes be accepted ; that the tender of Mr. James Green for the painting of Delph ' bridge Savage's bridge, Waterloo bridge, and the socket of the bridge over Pig-lane,be accepted ; that the tender of John Sefton for the cutting and laying ot pipe sewers in Loch-street, Grauville-street, and Canal-street be accepted. Mr. Wiswall in moving the adoption of these minutes referred to the one having rogard to the re-appointment of Mr. Wilding as assistant surveyor. The committee had just completed ten new streets; had partially com- pleted five others ; and had undertaken an additional nineteen. Mr. Wilding's salary came out of the costs of making these streets and in no way would affect the rates. He thought it was decidedly the best thing to con- tinue this department and to appoint Mr. Wilding to carry on the work. Up to the present the duties of his office had been discharged satisfactorily to all concerned. —Mr. Lightburn seconded.—Mr. Bisbrown remarked that he had purposed opposing the re-appointment of Mr. Wilding until he had heard what Mr. Wiswall had just said. Now, however, he had become possessed of more information and upon second consideration felt convinced, that,in the interests of the ratepayers, it was absolutely necessary to retain the services of Mr. Wild- ing in order that the streets might be completed. Nearly half the number had now been finished, and it was very desirable that Mr. Wilding should supervise the remainder ot the work.—The minutes were approved.</p>
        <p>HEALTH COMMITTEE: COMPLIMENTARY REMARKS.</p>
        <p>The recommendations of this committee showed that the Sanitary Inspector applied for notices to be served upon owners of property in Gas-st., Halton-road, Heath. road, Ellesmere-st., High-st., and Church-place, to abate certain nuisances, and in the event of non-comOiance with such notices that legal proceedings be taken. Also that the tender of Mr. R. W. Harrop for the building of three tipper closets at Lightfoots-court be accepted.— That plans be approved for structural alterations and improvements in Taylor's-row, Stonehills-lane, Peel-st., Cld Pennington's-row and Sutton-st., as submitted by the owners of the property.—That the Sanitary Inspec- tor be empowered under the Public Health Act, 1875, to enter No. 14, Egerton-at., after notice had been given, for the purpose of examining the drains and in the event of their being found in a bad condition that notice be given to the owners to improve them. It was further recommended that if such notice was not complied with the board should execute the necessary works and recover the cost from the owners.—On the proposition of Mr. Imison, seconded by Mr. T. Lea, the minutes were adopted. Mr. Wiswall said it was certainly due to the Committee that they should be complimented for the work which they were doing. They had been instrumental in con- siderably improving the sanitary state of the town, and they had his cordial support.—Mr. Pritchard expressed the opinion that the Committee had ridded the town of many nuisances; and he was very glad to hear that it was not necessary for them to resort to compulsion or legal proceedings in crder to have the requisite improve- ments made by the owners. The Committee were deserving of estimation for having effected such an im- provement in the sanitary state of the town.—Mr.Leach said they rarely had to use force in the attainment of their objects, and this was never used only when all other means had failed.—The • Chairman thought that the Committee devoted a great amount of its time to nuisance cases, and it must surely be evident to every person in the town what a great and good work they were engaged in, and what a large amount of valuable work they had done.</p>
        <p>MARKETS COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>This Consmittee recommended that the following tender of Messrs. T. Booth and Sons for the hire of horses in connection with the fire brigade be accepted: —Fire engine: fwo horses 4s. ed. per mile and 4s. per hour waiting ; three horses six shilling per mile and ss. per hour waiting; four horses Bs. per mile and 7s. iler hour waiting. Horse reel cart :IP ae horse ss. and 25.6 d. per hour waiting. Mr. Hankinson proposed the adoption of the minutes, which was seconded by Mr. Wiswall and carried.</p>
        <p>of the Board a few weeks ago,when they refused to send a delegate to the Sanitary Congress in London. At any rate tilts should have induced them to approach the Board on the subject. Incurring the expense, and then applying for the Board's sanction was very improper.— Mr. Lightburn thought that the money was being well spent and supported the original proposition that the minutes be passed.—Mr. Meadowcroft also objected to the irregular procedure of the committee. No other committee had ever taken it upon themselves to do a thing which they had not the power to do before receiv- ing the sanction of the Board.—The Chairman explained that there had not been sufficient time at the disposal of the committee to bring the Board at an earlier date. The matter had been discussed by the Finance Committee, who had approved of it.—After some further discussion Mr. Wiswall proposed that the minutes be passed under protest axd that the committee be informed that the Board did not look favourably upon their action in incur- ring expenditure in the way described before securing their sanction.—The minutes were then passed.</p>
        <p>TUE RE-ELECTION OF COMMITTEES. The Chairman informed the members that the law clerk had advised him that it was not necessary to re- appoint the various Committees. This would have to be left for the District Council to perform when they came into office.</p>
        <p>THE DIVISION OF THE TOWN INTO WARDS. The Chairman announced that the County Council had forwarded the Order dividing Runcorn into wards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LIBRARY COMMITTEE- : IRREGULAR PROCEEDINGS.</head>
        <p>LIBRARY COMMITTEE- : IRREGULAR PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>Among the recommendations of this committee were: —That two incandescent lamps be fixed on the pendant in the reading room ;—that 135 volumes of new books as per list submitted be purchased ; that the librarian be appointed a delegate to reoresent the library committee at the forthcoming meeting of the Library Association of the United Kingdom at Belfast, and that his expense&amp; (not to exceed £5) be allowed. Mr. Hankinson moved the confirmation of the minutes and in doing so drew attention to the one referring to the appointment of the Librarian as delegate to the Con- ference at Belfast to which probably there might be some objection. If there was, all that he could say was that the object of sending Mr. Jones there was considered by the committee to be a good one, and one from which much benefit would be derived. There would be thirty two papers upon different subjects read at the confer- ence by experts as well as various exhibitions. From these Mr. Jones would get much information and on his return he would be able to use his knowledge for the advantage of the patrons of the library.—Mr. Leach seconded.—Mr. Bisbrown understood that the Librarian had already gone to the Conference although the sanction of the Board was still required. Mr. Hankinson had pointed out the advantages of I attending such a conference—if they were to be con- sidered as advantages—but he (Mr. Bisbrown) did not I think it was proper to first incur the expenditure, and afterwards to apply to the Board for their sanction.—Mr. Imison thought that Mr. Jones (librarian) would not get many " wrinkles "at the conference which would prove of much value. He agreed with Mr. Bisbrown in say- ing that the consent of the Board should have been ob- tained before the expense was incurred. The conference was then in progress, and the librarian had been sent, and he was of the opinion that the Board ought to show their disapprobation of such an action on the part of the Library Committee. He therefore begged to move an amendment to the effect that this minute should be omitted. He did not intend his conduct to be considered as opposition to Mr. Jones. He believed that Mr. Jones would bring them back every information he could —but simply to show that considering the many items of extraneous expenditure which they had had, this was one which it would have been well not to have incurred.—The Chairman pointed out that the money came out of the id. rate. The committee had a certain sum to spend every year from thig rate, and had the £5 not been spent by sending a delegate to the Con- ference it would have been expended in something else. The committee, he presumed, had considered the matter fully before deciding upon such a course.—Mr. Wisvrall expressed regret that Mr. Win. Handley (chairman of the committee), was not present. He held the same opinion as Mr. unison had just expressed, that the committee should have taken as a guide in such a matter the action</p>
        <p>:::,-.:,,,,,. - -.. ---,,c-....4:.,i-.,ii,-;:,:,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WREN THE BOARD W/LL EXPIRE.</head>
        <p>WREN THE BOARD W/LL EXPIRE.</p>
        <p>In reply to Mr. Meadowcroft, Mr. Lake said that no definite date for the expiration of the Board had been fixed by the Local Government Board. He understood, however, that it would be early in December.</p>
        <p>THE GRAVING DOCK. Mr. Bishrown, in referring to the above, asked the Clerk if the Ship Canal Company had replied to thl Board's last letter.—Mr. Lake answered in the affirma- tive. A letter had been received on the Bth August say- ing that the subject would receive consideration.—Mr. Lightburn hoped that before the Commissioners expired the members would try to wipe off their books all mat- ters which would probably tax their successors, in order that they might not be hampered with an unfair amount of work. The agreement with the Ship Canal Company was one of these and he trusted that it would be pressed forward. It was very important that they should ob- tain from the company what was stipulated in the agreement.—The Chairman thought the matter had better go before the Committee who had it in hand.— Mr. Pritchard assured the members that the subject was being duly watched and everything possible was being done. It was his intention to call a meeting of the General Purposes Committee very shortly for the pur- pose of dealing with the matter again.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>HALTON-ROAD BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE HALTON-ROAD BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Walter Collier inquired what the position of this question was. The bridge should have been removed some weeks ace and he was anxious to know what was being done.— Mr. Bisbrown said the plans of the pro- posed bridge had been submitted to the directors of the Bridgewater Undertaking, who were shortly having a meeting.—The Chairman hoped that expedition would be made by the Company in considering the plans in order that Mr. Collier might be relieved. If erected, the bridge would be found very advantageous by the inhab- itants of the neighbourhood. Ile thought that if the matter was left with Mr. Meadowcroft, it would receive due attention. This terminated the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>MARRIAGE OF MISS BESSIE SPEAKMAN OF RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MARRIAGE OF MISS BESSIE SPEAKMAN OF RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A marriage in which considerable interest was manifested, took place on Wednesday morning at the Bethesda Congregational Church ituncorn. The uniting parties were Mr. Harold Chadwick, second son of Mr. W. H. Chadwick, Leinster Gardens, and Miss BOSSiE Speakman eldest daughter of Mr. J. J. Speakman, Stanley Villas, Runcorn. In honour of the occasion a goodly show of bunting had been hung in the neighbourhood of the bride's residence, whilst at the church gates two large flags had been hoisted in the form of an arch. The interior of the sacred building was decked with foliage plants, and gave it an appearance of freshness. Long before the time announced for the cere- mony to take place the church was full of people all anxi- ous to be witnesses of such an interesting event. At about eleven o'clock the carriages arrived at the gates, and the bride, leaning upon the arm of her father, entered the building, and of course became the sole object of atten- tion. She was dressed in an ivory surah silk costume trimmed with lace and wore a tulle veil and a wreath of orange blossoms fastened by a diamond crescent, the gift of the bridegroom. Attending her were two young ladies and two little girls—bridesmaids named respectively, Miss Chadwick, Miss Lettie Chadwick, Misses Eva and Gladys Speakman. The first two were attired in dresses of pink nun's veiling with silk trimmings and brown hats to correspond. The younger damsels were robed very prettily in pink silk with ecru lace hats and pink tips. All four carried bonquets of flowers, tastefully arranged. The castume of the bride's mother was of brown silk, with brocade silk sleeves. The position of best man was filled by Mr. Frank Speakman, the groomsmen being Messrs. Eric and Albert Edward Speakman, brothers of the bride. The marriage ceremony was performed by the Rev. John Simpson, the bride being given away by her father. The union having been .completedahe Rev. J.Sirnpson, inn short address asked the bride and bridegroom to seriously consider the engagement into which they had just entered. It was a sacrament. Marriage was not , meant for happiness alone, but for the development of ' character. He pleaded with them to keep a strict watch over themselves—to exact not too much one from the other—to endure whatever tended to disturb their mutual love and peace. In conclusion he drew their attention to the duties of husbands and wives as set forth in the Holy Scriptures. As the party left the church, Mr. Le Couteur played the " Wedding March." The newly-married pair, as they issued from the doors were met with showers of rice and were driven away amid many congratulations. A sumptuous break fast was afterwards partaken of at the house of Mr.J.J.Speakman. In addition to the ladies and gentlemen already mentioned, the following were guests at the wedding breakfast :—Mr. and Mrs. Chad- wick, Mr. and Mrs. Silcock,Mr. and Mrs. J.B.Chadwick, , Miss Holmes, Mr. Holmes, Mr. and Mrs. Beck, Mrs. and , Miss Evans, Mr. and Mrs. John Speakman, Mr. and Mrs. A. Speakman. Miss Nellie Speakman (Ruthin),Miss Annie Speakman (Colwyn Bay) Mrs. Reed, Mrs. Widders (Stalybridge), Mrs.D.Speakmaa, Mr. and Mrs. Thomson, Mr. and Mrs. Bell, (Bootle), Miss Warner, Miss Wood- ward, (Northwich), Miss Eva Speakman, Miss Gladys Speakman, Mr. and Mrs. Hugh Branch, Mr. H. Hughes, (Brentford), Mrs. Hughes, senior, (Ealing), Mr. Percy Speakman, Mrs. W. Le Couteur, Dr. and Mrs. Robinson, (Hanley), Mr. A. Lloyd, (Ruthin), etc., etc. The happy couple left Runcorn in the afternoon by train for North Wales where the honeymoon is being spent. In the evening Mr. Speakman entertained a few friends in a tent specially erected at the rear of his residence, where a variety of amusements, including dancing, were indulged in with much zest. The following is the list of presents :—Rev. J. Simp- son, case of silver salts and spoons ; Mr. and Mrs. Bell (Bootle), china tea service; Mr. and Mrs. P. Speakman, silver butter dish ; Mr. and Mrs. G. T. Kirkham, case of (silver) fish knives and forks ; Mr. and Mrs. Thomson, "The Three Graces" (marble) ; "Ferrate," (Florence), four pictures, table cloth and serviettes ; Mr. Mellor, suite of brushes ; Mr. and Mrs. Woodward (Northwich), brass mounted oak coal vase ; Mrs. Nixon,antimacassar; Miss Barbara Nixon, pincushion ; Mr. E. Kniveton, case of silver tomatoe fork and jam spoon ; Mr. and Mrs. Chadwick,silver tea and coffee service; Mr. J. J. Speak- man, case of cutlery; Mrs. J. J. Speakman, household linen and sewing machine ; Mr. and Mrs. J. B. Chad- wick, walnut washstand and dressing table ; Mr. and Mrs. Silcock (St. Helens),dinner service and embroidered sideboard cloth ; Miss May Chadwick,silver flower stand and lace mat; Miss Lettie Chadwick,silver flower stand; employesof the Runcorn Gas Works, silver flower stand and cut glass dish ; Mr. and Mrs. John Speakman, silver cruet ; Mr. Frank Speakman, case of silver fish carvers; Mr. Wycliffe Chadwick, silver cake basket; Mr. Eric Speakman, decanters enclosed in silver frames; Mr. A. E. Speakman, silver sugar and cream stand; Miss Eva Speakman, silver jelly dish and spoon ; Miss Gladys Speakman.a pair of pockets; Mr. T. Brooks Handley, travelling cloak; Mr. and Mrs. John Arthur Chadwick (Barnett), brass tray and crumb scoop; Master Leonard and Miss May Speakman, pair of easel mirrors ; Dr. and Mrs. Robinson (London), silver cake and butter knife; Mr. Tom Haynes, case of carvers; Miss Smith (Hooton), pair of salts and spoons ; Miss Williams (Ilkley), silver mounted oak biscuit barrel ;Mrs. Hughes (Ealing), silver fish knives and forks; Mr. and Mrs. Hughes (Brenforcl). silver breakfast cruet ; Miss Hughes (Ealing), painting; Miss Emily Hughes (Ealing), cushion; Miss Wilson, painting on opal; Mr. and Mrs. A. Speakman (Bedford). silver pepperettes and mus- tard pots ; Mr. and Mrs. W. Reed, pair of hand- paintea placques ; Mrs. R,yle, ornaments ; Edwards, antima ceases ; Miss Edwards, glass ink stand; Mr. and Mrs. E. J. Reed (Preston), engraving ; Mr. and Mrs.. Frith, silver jam dish ; Mr. George Frith, case of silver " apostle " spoons ; Miss Woodward (Northwich), drawing-room table ; Mrs. I Branch, cheque; Mr. and Mrs. Tatten, Burn's poems; Mrs. and Miss Evans (Sale), pair of silver serviette rings ; Mr. and Mrs. Thomas, kitchen requisites ; Mr. James Sherratt, case of silver jam spoons and butter knife ; Mrs. Mayer, brass stool ; Miss Bettie Holmes (Stafford), china tea service ; Mrs. David and Miss Annie Speakman, pair of silver serviette rings ; Messrs. Horace and Clement Handley, silver bread fork; Miss Beck and Mr. John Beck (Frodsliam), silver breakfast cruet ; Miss Johnson, hand painted placque ; Mr. A. Grice, bronze clock ; Miss Ashworth, silver sardine box; Mrs. Widden (Stalybridge), pair of steel engravings ; Miss Eva Woodward (Nothwicti), cut glass salts and Spoons: Mrs. Forrester, bamboo flower stand ; Mrs. Kirkham (Blackpool), work basket ; Mr. and Mrs. Edwards (Widnes), salad bowl ; Miss Lizzie and Master Ernest Edwards, card case and cards ; Mrs. Ankers (Northwich), silver sugar sifter and sugar tongs ; Mr. and Mrs. Beck (Frodslaam), pair of fish carvers ; Mr. and Mrs. W. H. Chadwick, pair of hand-</p>
        <p>knitted slippers ; Mrs. Foulk—s (Whitchurch), comb and brush bag ; Mrs. Pritchard. llowPr basket-. ; Miss Lizzie Thomas, ornaments ; Miss Kate Riley (IVbitchurch Ross, Hereford), painting ; Mrs. Cane (9ensingbain, Cumber- land), bandworked table cloth ; Mr. John and Miss Hettie Holmes (Stafford), set of china ; Mrs. Holmes. brass preserving pan ; Miss Bancroft, glass jug ; and Miss Chorley, lamp shade.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of this Authority was held on Tuesday afternoon at Dutton Workhouse, Mr. John Mclntyre in the chair. The other gentle- men present were the Rev. J. W. N. Tanner, Messrs. James Brandreth, H. E. Heywood, Thomas Lewis, C. T. Whitley, J. M. Williams, Samuel Woodward, Edward Aaron, C. W. Davenport, John Ashton, clerk; George Ashton, deputy clerk; J. Farrington, nuisance inspector, and Dr. Adams, medical officer of health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>ALLEGED POLLUTION OF HALTON BROOK.</head>
        <p>THE ALLEGED POLLUTION OF HALTON BROOK.</p>
        <p>A letter was received from the Local G ,vernme-n-t -Board with reference to the action taken hy the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority in regard to the discharge of sewage into Flalton Brook and stating that the Board could not agree with the view that the Authority had now complied with the provisions of the Rivers P,llution Prevention Acts. They requested that the Authority would, with- out further delay, take such steps as might be necessary to prevent unpurified sewage from passing into the brook in question, and that the Board be informed at an early date what progress had been made in that respect, The Clerk stated that a meeting of the Parochial Com- mittee had been called, but only two members had attended. After discussing the matter, they suggested that it be referred to the Medical Officer of Elealth to say what should be done, and that he reply to the Local Government Board to that effect.—Dr. Adams thought that the London authorities would no accept any sewage, even after the settling process. They would have to go in for complete purification.—lnspector Far- rington thought that nothing was necessary to be done, if the Local Gevernment Board would only take a sensible view of the matter. There was a large quantity of water going down the bromic, and there was not a trace of sewage within six yards of the outfall.—lt was eventually decided that the medical officer and clerk should prepare a report and forward it to the Local Government Board.</p>
        <p>OF MR. T. FARRINGTON AS NUISANCE</p>
        <p>INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. E. Heywood, in accordance with notice of mo- tion,proposed that Mr. J. Farrington be re-appointed for another year as inspector of nuisances, and inspector under the Dairies and Cowsheds Order at a salary of £l4O ; also inspector of canal boats at £lO per year ; and that the term " surveyor" should also be applied to him, the duties of which position he would be required to carry out. It was only a matter of form, and would not affect his salary. He thought that if there was any question of increase ot salary it might bc discussed whet the townships of Grappenhall, Latchford, an Thelwall were added to the union. They could give Mr. Farring- ton an extra grant at any time, as they had done in cases of smallpox.—Mr. Brandreth seconded the motion which was carried.—Mr. Farrinc,ton thanked the mem- bers for appointing him for the 18th time, and assured them he should always endeavour to perform his duties to the best of his ability.</p>
        <p>ENGINEER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>A repert from Mr. Wells, engineer, was read. It stated that he had made an inspection of the Ship Canal and had found the Moss-lane syphon in good working order. The doors at the outfall of Keckwick brook had been re-hung. There was, however, an influx of water from a disused temporary sluice to the westward, which had not been efficiently dammed, and the Canal Com- pany's engineer had been approached with reference to having this remedied. With regard to the sea flats out- fall, he thought Mr. Farrington should state what injury the Authority were suffering, from what advantage he expected would be gained by an alteration, and what alteration he suggested would be a benefit.</p>
        <p>TY7'7IOID FEVER AT HALTON.</p>
        <p>The report of the Medical Officer (Dr. Adams) showed that a case of typhoid fever had broken out at Bate's Bridge, Halton. The patient had died. The drainage to the premises was very defective, most of the grids being blocked, and in addition sewage was received from pigstyes and stables, and accumulated in the rear of the dwelling-houses.</p>
        <p>THE INFECTIOUS DISEASES HOSPITAL AT MOORE. Mr. Heywood reported, on behalf of the committee appointed to deal with the above hospital, that it had been decided to apply to the Manchester Ship Canal Company for the terms on which they will either lease the hospital or sell it.</p>
        <p>NON-PAYMENT OF CALLS.</p>
        <p>In submitting the report of the Finance Committee. Mr. Brandreth stated that they had not a sufficient balance in hand to meet all the accounts that had been presented. The townships of Frodshani and Frodsham Lordship had not yet paid their calls, and yet they were borrowing money in large sums. He moved that Mr. Ashton (clerk) be instructed to write to the overseers informing them that if the request for payment was not answered in seven days summonses would be issued.— Mr. Heywood seconded.—The Clerk remarked that the auditor strongly clmplained of the delay in such pay. ments.—The motion was carried. _ NUISANCE INSPECTOR'S REPORTS. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Farrington submitted certificates of analysis of four samples of water from the townships of Appleton and Preston-olh-P ill, all of which had been returned as "satisfactory."—He further reported the existence of a slight defect in a sewer at Helsby, and applied for an order to have the same repaired and improved.—The order Was granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>‘2,000 FOR A PAUPER.</head>
        <p>‘2,000 FOR A PAUPER.</p>
        <p>At the nsual weekly meeting of the Board of Guard- ians ef the Strand Union, the Clerk reported that a man named Thomas Piper, who had been an inmate of the workhouse at Edmonton for sixteen years, had recently received a letter from a firm of solicitors informing him that under the will of a relative who died a abort time ago he had been left a legacy of £2,000, and as a boita- fide guarantee of the genuineness of the bequest, two £5 Bank of England notes were enclosed in the communica- tion for the purpose of providing Piper with a suitable outfit on his leaving the workhouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TERRIBLE DEATH OF A BOATMAN AT</head>
        <p>TERRIBLE DEATH OF A BOATMAN AT</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>"BURNED TO A CINDER."</p>
        <p>Mr. C. W. Tibbitts,deputy coroner to Mr. Chnrton. Chester, conducted an inquiry on Monday night at the Bridge Inn, Frodsham Brilge, into the cirtittn- stances attending the death of a boatman named William Mayo, of South End, Liverpool, whose charred remains were discovered in a burning flat on Sunday morning last. William Hulse, residing at Frodsham Bridge, and em- ployed at the Weston Soap Works, deposed that at about 4 30 a.m. on Sunday the 2nd inst., he was going home from work but on reaching the second berth of the Frodsham Lock he saw that the flat " Minerva" was on fire. He hurried home and informed his father, who got up and accompanied his son to the scene of the fire. Several other men joined them and on arrival at the boat the flames were leaping out of the cabin to the distance of six or seven feet. The cargo, which consisted of cow horns, was also burning. Mr. Bowden, the lockmaster, was knocked up, and they all assisted in extinguishing the flames. Afterwards an inspection of the cabin was made and they found the charred body only of a man lying on the back. The arms, legs, and head were miss- ing. In fact what remained was almost a cinder. P.C. Price was communicated with,and the body was removed to the Bridge Inn. William Hulse, father of the last witness, bore out what his eon had just said. Edward Bowen lock master of the Frodsham Lock, on the Old River Weaver also gave corroborative evidence. The position of the body, he said, would support the supposition that deceased had first been suffocated with the the fumes of smoke; then the bunk in which he slept had been burned and he had fallen with it to the ground. Close to the body was also found a clay pipe, so that evidently the man must have been smoking when lying in the bunk, and by that means probably the fire had originated. After the body had been removed to the Bridge Inn, the flat was taken to Messrs. Heywood and Massey's Works, to where the cargo had been con- signed.</p>
        <p>Captain Goorge Jones, of 44, Gilbert-street, Runcorn, testified to the deceased Mayo being sent to him to act as mate on Saturday morning whilst at Liverpool. Mayo. who was at that time under the influence of drink,would have been about 38 or 40 years of age. He had had no settled residence at the time, but worked at odd jobs at the docks of Liverpool. Witness allowed him to sleep all the way from Liverpool to Weston Point. Deceased then woke up and got off the boat for the purpose of getting a pint of beer. He came back again and did not have any more drink until Frodsham Bridge was reached. Here witness left him saying that he was going home to Runcorn. Before going witness had completely extinguished the fire in the boat. The pipe (produced) he believed to be the property of deceased, who was a smoker.</p>
        <p>P.O al.. gave evidence. The lull, recurded a verdict of " Found burnt to death."</p>
        <p>It might be stated that the damage to the " Minerva" amounted to £BO, and she is the property of Mr.Williain Burton, Sankey Hall, Warrington, and was uninsured. The damage to the cargo of horns is estimated at £2O. These were insured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>11AYDOCK.</head>
        <p>11AYDOCK.</p>
        <p>COLLIERY ACCIDENT.—John Leach, of Rigby-street,St. Helens, was on Tuesday seriously injured in the South- port Pit, belonging to Messrs. R. Evans and Co. While at work a large stone fell upon him and injured him internally. He lies in the flaydock Cottage Hospital a precarious condition.</p>
        <p>_.AmormisdlMMEL</p>
      </div>
