      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>VALUABLE DISCOVERY.</head>
        <p>A VALUABLE DISCOVERY.</p>
        <p>--- Most readers have had some experience of sick rooms, either as patients or attendants. How many such are there who have not felt the weakness or d-htlity which follows the sickness, more discouraiing than even the disease itself ? Though the disease may have given way before the timely application of proper remedies, and though, in conequettce, no immediate danger is to he apprehended. the slow progress towards convalescence is very disheartening to Patient and attendant alike.</p>
        <p>How often do we hear people say :—" Yes. The fever has left him. He is free from an: immediate danger. But he is so weak. If he could only regain strength?" This debilitating effect of fevers and other diseases is indeed a great source of anxiety to medical men as well as to their patients ; and anything which would tend directly to shorten thc period between the departure of the fever and the complete recovery of strength would be naturally welcomed by both doctors and patients.</p>
        <p>This is one of the reascns why Gwylym Evans' Quinine Bitters are so popular,why doctors proscribe it so largely, why chemists sell it so generally, why patients have recourse to it so readilF, and why everybody has a good word to say for it. Not only does it attack the disease in its stronghold, not only does it drive the evil from the system, but it gives ba, k directly the strength of which the system has been deprived by sickness ; it infuses new life into the blood, and the recovery of health and strength, instead of being, as it otherwise would be, discouragingly slow, intermittent, and uncer- tain, becomes, when this excellent medicine is resorted to, encouragingly rapid, regular, and certain. It is, therefore, confidently recommended as an unrivalled tonic medicine,and may be safely used by all who suffer from any form of weakness or debility from whatever cause it may arise.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Yung„ of 107, Laxton-st., New Brompton, Chatham, writing to a friend, says</p>
        <p>"I thank you very much for SPndinc, me the ',Gwitsm EVANS" BrrrEas ' handbill. I have taken two bottles, a 2s. 9d. and a 4.5. 6,1. It has done me more good than anything I had ever tried before, and I am so pleased with it that I shall recommend it to every one who comes to see me."</p>
        <p>GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. PRAISED BY EVERYBODY. AGM'S' EVERY- WHERE. Sold in Battles at 1/14, 2/9, and 4/8 each. Beware of Imitations! See the Name of "Gwrrxm Eva.Ns" on Label Stamp and Bottle. SOLE PROPRIETORS : QUININE BITTERS MAN UFACTIi RING CO., LTD., LLANELLY, SOU fir WALES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BEFORE THE MIGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MIGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>' AN IMPURE WELL CLOSED. John Smith, Legh-street, was charged at the Warring- ton Borough Court, on Friday, that being the owner of premises na 9, 11. 13, 15, and 17 Orchard-street, and one house in the rear, he had a well, the water of which was unfit for domestic purposes. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) who prosecuted said that the well in question was used for drinking purposes by the above mentioned houses. The water was analysed by Dr. Gornall a,nrl found to be unfit for drinking purposes. Dr. Gornall then gave the result of his analysis, and said that the substances he complained of in the water were chlorine, albumenised ammonia, and solid matter. He gave a certificate to that effect to the Corporation. Inspector Norman also gave evidence to obtsining the water.— Mr. Hatton who defended, said that the well had been there for many years, and an analyst had declared it to be good water.—A tenant of one of the houses men- tioned named T. Backhouse was csl'ed and said that he bad had water from the well for 40 s ears,and had never found anything wrong with it.—Themagistrates ordered the well to be closed wii lain a month. THE ALLEGED ASSAULT UPON A WIFE. At the Warrington Borough Police Court,on Saturday, before Mr. Burgess (in the chair) and Mr. Chandley, Joseph Marratt, a respectable-looking middle-aged man, was charged on remand with having on Wednesday committed an aggravated assault upon his wife Martha. —Prisoner pleaded not guilty.—The complainant said that on Wednesday evening, about a quarter to seven o'clock, she and her husband were sitting at the table. The prisoner was cutting up some tobacco,and they were having a few words. She stooped down and the knife cut her head, but her husband did not do it wilfully. The Chief Constable explained that on the evening in question the prisoner spoke disrespectfully of the com- plainant's father, who is dead. Mrs. Marratt made a reply, and he (Mr. Hunt) was led to believe that the prisoner threw a table knife at her, cutting her on the right temple. The prisoner called in a neighbour, and subsequently a doctor was sent for. Information was given to Detective-Sergeant Monaghan and P.C. Hardy, the former of. whom thought it his duty to arrest Marratt. In, reply to the charge the prisoner said "That's right." Thedoctor considered the w.iund rather dangerous, and the maeistrates remanded the case.— The prisoner : I feel very sorry for what I have done. It was done accideet•lly.-1 he Chairman : Well with the evidence before us this morning there is nothing for the Bench to do but to dismiss the case. THEFT BY A SERVANT GIRL. At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednes- day, Sarah Ellen Taylor pleaded guilty to stealing from the dwelling-house of William Hall, some time between the 9th and 12th ult., a pur-e containing £2 ss. and three watch guards, value ss,—Mrs. Hall stated that she was the wife of Wm. Hall, of Radley Farm, Houghton, and she engaged the prisoner as servant at her house, from the Industrial Schools, Padgate, on the 9:h August. On the following Sunday she went into the prisoner's bedroom, and on noticing something hanging out of a pocket in a dress of the prisoner's she felt in it, and found the three watch guards pr deced. They belonged to witness's husband and were work ss.—Ellen Hall stated that the purse proiuced was hers and she had in it about £2 55., on the 9th ult. She kept it in a small .box in the front kitchen. On looking in the box on the 11th ult. she found her purse missing. The prisoner -afterwards adn.itred taking the purse. She had no right to go to the box.—John Hall stated that he speke to the prisoner about the missing purse on the 11th ult., but ,she said ". I knew nothing about it:' He threatened to fetch a policeman to her, whereupon she Pulled thepurse out at her pocket. She said she had given her father and uncle 2s. 6d. each. She also spent several coppers in sweets for herself and distributed lls, amongst the children at the Padgate, Industrial Schools. Mr. Mines, the superintendent, was told of the matter and he returned lie. id. which the children had received from the prisoner. She was sent back to the Industria schools.—Sergeant Barnes said from information he received from the Union schools he went to the schools and took the girl into custody. He charged her with the theft and she replied " I have given them all the things back." The girl had been. to service at three places. She was returned from the first two as being insolent and idle, and the third place was the complain- ant's. The magistrates dealt with her under the first offenders act, and bound her over to come up when called upon. SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST WARRINGTON BOYS. At the Warrineton Borough Court, on Friday, John Whittaker, 61, Nicholson-st., Orlando Jones, 41, Wakel field-se, and Griffith Roberts,loB, Pierpoint-st., aged 12, and 10 years, respectively, were charged with felon- iously, and maliciously, moving and displacing certain brakes, and also_ with uncoupling certain wagons in Whitecross branch of the Cheshire Lines Committee, on August 16. Mr. Percy Davies, instructed by Detective- ' Inspector Simpson, appeared for the Cheshire Lines • Committee, and in opening the case said that the charge against the boys was of a most serious character, as in- calculable damage might have been done. The boy Whit- taker had already been charged with an exactly similar offence two years ago, which was committed in the same place. On that occasion Whittaker had taken the brakes*off some wagons which were on an incline in Whitecross siding.and the wagons went at a tremendous pace into Whitecross works, but fortunately no one was in the way, or loss of life would have ensued. For that Whittaker was fined 65., and received 12 strokes of the birch rod. In the present case.on August 16, there were 17 empty wagons !eft on the Whitecross branch. They were all coupled together. and secured with brakes,each wagon having the brake applied. At 8.15 p.m., Mr. James, the foreman porter for the Cheshire Lines Com- mittee at Warrington, noticed that twelve wagons had disappeared entirely. Hewent to the siding, and there saw the three defendants, and a small boy named Ellis, and another, Isaac Lowe, who would be called as a wit- ness, in one of the remaining wagons. When they saw James, they ran away, but James caught Ellis and ob- tained the names of the others. He then went to look for the other wagons, and found Ithem a long distance down the incline, in Whitecross Works. Fortunately no one was working at the time, or some loss of life would no doubt have ensued. The boys must have had a great deal of trouble to uncouple the wagons and loose the brakes, for each wagon had gone down the incline separately. The company pressed the case against Whit- taker especially,as he had done the same thing before.— Evidence for the prosecution was given by Hy. James, Isaac Lowe, and Alfred Reece Winter, and the Bench, after considering the matter, decided to send Whittaker and Roberts to a reformatory for three years, as they had both been summoned for other offences previously. Jones, who made his first appearance, was ordered to receive six strokes of the birch rod.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>MINOEt CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOEt CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison (chairman), Alder- man Wright, Councillor Roberts, Mr. S. Wright, and</p>
        <p>Dr. Gornall. ABSENTING APPELENTICES.—For absenting herself from</p>
        <p>SEALSKIN JACKETS AND FURS very description CLEANED or RE-DYED and 11—'sof e ALTERED. FUR SKINS dressed and made up. into rugs, COOK.E. Manufacturing Furrier, 11, Upper Newing- :ton, and 21, • Ren.shaw-street, Liverpool. Established 20 years.</p>
        <p>-111 IRDS, ANIMALS AND FISH STUFFED, and 1-1 mounted in Glass Cases of any design. HEADS and HORNS mounted also for Sale. Birds made into Hand Screens or Fire Screens. Healer in British and Foreign Birds and Animals' Skins, Glass Shades. Stags' Heads. Cabinets, Store Boxes, Nets. &amp;c., for Butterfly and Egg Collectors: —B. COOKE, Naturalist and Furrier. 21, Renshaw-street, Liverpool</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>'-iiirimiNlMMllilim•mimm.....-_</head>
        <p>'-iiirimiNlMMllilim•mimm.....-_</p>
        <p>the service of Messrs.J. and J. Wilkinson,fustian cutters, on August 21, Elizabeth Ann Smith, an apprentice, of 17, Pierpoint-street, was ordered to go back to her work. Lucy Deakin, 23. Beswick-ttreet, was also ordered to go hack for absenting herself. 'THROWING STONES AT TECEGRAPH WlRES.—Thomas Pendlebury, 41, Dial-street, and Joseph Berry, 21, Dial- street, were each fined ss. including costs, for throwing stones at telegraph wires,belonging to the C.L.O Railway Committee on August 11. Mr. Percy Davies prosecuted.' P.C. Meacock gave evidence to seeing two boys throwing the stones from Lythgoes'-aane. TRESPASSING ON RAILWAY LAND —Alfred Ford, 1, Napier-street, Harry Forster, 31, Orford-street, ano Peter Tomlin, were charged with damaging clover in a field off Marsh House-lane on August 11th. Mr.P. Davies prosecuted. P.C. Berry stated that he saw the defend- ants gathering clover in the field, and when asked why they were doing so, they said that they were getting it for rabbits.—The defendants said that they did not know as there was a footpath. across the field. The Bench fined Foster and Tomlinss. each, and is. for damsge, and discharged Ford. CRUELTY TO HORSES.—Patrick Conroy, Darbyshire- street, Wigan, was fined in the costs for working a horse while in an unfit state on August 15 in Market Piece. Inspector Luff said that there were three old wounds under the saddle when he examined the horse.—Thomas Birchall jun., was charged with the same offence on August 16, and Thomas Birchall sen., was charged with , causing it to he worked, in Sankey-street. There was no evidence to show that the senior defendant owned the horse and he was discharged. P.C. Bell and Inspector Luff each gave evidence tor the prosecution, and Bir- (+all jun., was fitted in the costs.—George Wicics, 7 Navigation-street was charged with working a horse, and James Lashford, Orford-lane, was charged with causing it to be worked while in unfit state on August 17th Inspector Luff and P.C. Rylatt said that the horse was walking very lame up Horsemarket-street, and was evidently suffering pain—Robert Dobson, butcher, was called and said that the horse was sound and was not suffering pain. The magistrates dismissed the ease.—Richard Gore, Commercial Hotel, was also charged with working a horse while in an 'unfit state. P.S. Taylor said that the defendant was driving a horse and waggonette up Horsemarket-street, on Aug. 23. The horse appeared to be in pain., and witness found, upon examining it,, a wound upen its shoulder. Inspector Luff and P.C. Rylatt corroborated, and defen- dant was fined ss. and costs.—Arthur Rogers, and George Whittamore were charged, the former with working, and the latter with causing to be worked, a , horse while in an unfit state on July 26. Inspector Luff proved the cese and the defendants were fined 2s. 61 each.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY .—Before Mr. Burgess (in the chair) and Mr. Chandley. DRUNICENIES3.—Sarah Dillon was charged with hav- ing been drunk and disorderly in irlam-st.., on Friday night. P.C. Millington proved the case, and the prisoner was sent to gaol for one month.</p>
        <p>MONDLY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman) Alderman Harrison, 'Councillor H. Roberts, Messrs. S. Wright, E. J. Guest, J. W. Hayward and W. Pierpoint. TEMPORARY AUTHORITY —Wm. Allc(.,ck was granted temporary authority to sell off the premises at 1, Allen- street. _ _</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—For this offence the follow- ing were dealt with :—Timothy Shaw, labourer, 11, Dial-street, 55.; Harriet Greenwood, last from Oldham was discharged; John Bagulev,43th appearance,hawker, 4, Mill-street, ss. and costs ; 'John Kelly. collier, Burton- wood, ss. and costs ; Patrick Thompson, labourer, 4 Back Baydock-st., ss. and costs. A DESERTER.;—Richard Barks was chart.,ed with being a deserter from Her Majesty's Ist Yorkshire and Lanca- shire regiment stationed at Pontrefract. The case was proved by P.C. Bell. Prisoner was detained to await an escort. P.C. Bell was recommended for the usual reward.</p>
        <p>FURIOUS DRIVING .—Wm. Houghton, butcher's assist- ant, 39, Leicester-st.,was charged with furiously driving a horse and trap to the danger of passengers on the 24,h August. P.C. Hollingsworth deposed that on the date named he was in Horsemarket-street in cAmpany with P.C. Barlow when he saw the defendant standing in a butcher's,cart, driving at a very smart trot. Witness put his hand up for the defendant to spot, but he re- fused to do so. The magistrates discharged the-defend- ant on payment of costs, 63. DOG IA ITHOUT LICENCE. —Thoma.% Barlow, Buckley's- yard, was charged with keeping a dog without a licence on the 16th inst. Inspector Bowen gave evidence as to visiting the defendant's hause, and finding a fox terrier bitch there. The defendant's wife told witness that they were without a licence for it. Defendant now rt tted that the dog did not belong to him, and he was unahl. to get rid of it. On promising to bring it to the police station if it visited his house again the magistrates dis- charged him. TURSDLL—Before Mr. H. Garnett (chairman) and Mr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>T. Birchall.</head>
        <p>T. Birchall.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —Patrick Fov, Platt-street, for the above named offence in Cockhecige-lane the Previous evening was fined ss. and costs. P.C. Corbett proved the case. TIIURSDAY.—Before. Mr. S. Char.dley (chairman) and</p>
        <p>Alderman Sutton.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.--FOr this offence. COM- mitted in Golden Grove-st. on Wedne=dav night, Robert Porter was fined 53. or seven days. P.C. Evans proved the case. TEMPORARY AUTHORlTY.—Fairfield Turner was granted temporary authority to sell in the Pelkan Inn, Butter- market-street. in the place of John Allen, Mr. Hatton represented Mr. Turner. SCHOOL CASES.—TiIe following were summoned for neglecAng to send their children regularly to school : Thomas Pierpoint, architect, Bewsey-road, adjourned for two weeks. Miss Pierpoint saying that they were leaving town ; Fred. Roberts, Bewsev-road. fined 53.; Thomas Chrimes, Winwick-road, 55.; Thomas Kilcourse, Town Hill, 3s. 6d.; James King, Hamilton-so eet, 54.; Thomas Robinson. Latchlotd, 55 ; .Tho Mas Ta vlor, Latch- ford, 53. ; James Travis, Silver Square, ss. Mr. G. Riley prosecuted. . .</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS. WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. Ziba Armitage (chairman), Messrs. G. Comber, and William Mortimer. • DRUNK Asp DISORDERLY. —The following were dealt with for this offence.:—John Peak, at Croft, on the 21st ult., ss. and costs ; Patrick Rooney. at Croft, on the 27th ult., 25.. 61 and costs ; John Booth, at Poulton, 24. 61 and costs ; Peter Kelly, at Sankey Bridges on the 25th ult., 2s. 61 and costs; Thomas Brown, at Sankey Bridges on the 13th ult., 53. and costs. UNMUZZLED DOGS. —For allowing ,their dogs to be at large uninuzzled the following were summoned :—Walter Cooper, Sankey Bridges, is. and costs ; James Forster, Sankey Bridges, find the costs ; William Stanley, Pen- ,' keth, fined the costs. Doa Wrritour A LICENCE.—WiIIiam Scott, Penketh, was fined is. and costs for having on the 28th July kept a doe' without a licence. The defendant stated that he had had the dog in his possession only one day when the officer called at bis house. .</p>
        <p>I A SLEEPY Dim-mt.—John Dawson pleaded .guilty to having been asleep whilst in charge of a horse and lurry at Sankey Bridges on the 25th ult. P.C. Dugdale proved the case.—A fine of is. and costs was imposed. ASSAULT.—TharIes Matthews was charged . with as- saulting and using threats to Mr. Gray at Great Saner I on the 25th ult. The complainant stated that he was leaving Sankey Station to go to Penketh on the date named when, on turning into the high road the defend- ant met him, and struck him on the right- arm. The I defendant afterwards got hold of his hands and thrust , his nails in them, causing one hand to bleed. He also I used most threatening language.—James Peers, brick.l setter, deposed that he was working at some buildings near Sankey Station on the 25th ult. when he saw the defendant bar Mr. Gray's passago,and use most threaten- ing language to him. He did not see the assault.—The defendant denied the assault but admitted stopping the complainant and asking him for an apology. It was simply a " shop " case. Witness had been under the employ of the complainant, and there was some ill-feel- ing between them. The magistrates bound the defend- ant over to keep the peace for six months in tww sureties of £5 each. DRUNK IN CHARGE OF A nOßSE.—Thomas Fildes WEE fined 2s. 6d. and costs for being drunk whilst in charge of a horse and cart at Penketh, on the 21st ult. P.C. Thorley proved the case.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETIY SESSIONS. MONDAY—Before Messrs. F. J. Norman (in the chair),and S. Taylor. Doc OWNERS IN TROUBLE.—Thomas Dobson, butcher, of Church-street, was fined 73. 6d. and Bs. 6d. costs for keeping a dog without a license on the 22nd August.— Thomas Bourne, 1, Bvron-street, was fined 6d. and 9s. 6d. costs for allowing his dog to wander at large minus a collar bearing his proper name and address on the 28th August. CRUELTY TO A HORSE.—Thomas Powell, carter, 61, Heath-road, was summoned for cruelly ill-treating a horse by working it when unfit.—P.C. Richardson de- posed to seeing defendant at 9.30 a.m. on the 27th of August in High-street when he was driving a black horse attached to a lurry, loaded with flag-stones. He stopped the horse and upon examination found a wound underneath the collar about the size of a five-shilling piece. Blood and other discharge was issuing from the wound, which was in a very bad state through being irritated by the collar. Witness quostioned defendant with regard to the animal's condition, and he replied that the horse had had a boil and it had broken that morning. In witness's opinion the wound was an old one, and caused the horse much pain.—Sergeant Woolley bore out this statement.—Defendant stated that the animal suffered from "heat lumps,' and on that morn- ing referred to one of the lumps having burst owing to its being rubbed by the collar.—Fined ss. and 10s. costs</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 8, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 8, 1894.</p>
        <p>or fourteen days in gaol with bard labour. Ins OBSTRUCTION NUISANCE.—SiX boys named Ernest Woodward (12), Thomas Jones (12), John Price (15), Michael Moores (16), Robert Robinson (16), and Harry Harrop (15), all of Runcorn, were summoned fur having obstructed a footpath in Lowlands-road, on the evening of the 22nd August. Joaes, Moores, and Robinson pleaded guilty,and the remaining defendants denied the charge. The case having been proved against Jones, Moores, and Robinson, they were each fined 6.1. and 4s. 6J. costs. Woodward, Price, and Harrop were dis- missed.</p>
        <p>DESTRUCTIVII Boys.—Thnmas Knowles (10), Ralph (11),John Bowden (13),ancl 'fhornas Henry Green (10) were charged with doing damage to the extent of . to a haystack belonging to Mr_ Thomas Clough, at Halton,on th• 26th August. Evidence was given to the rtfect that the boys were seen in the field where the stac.k was, and ran away when a servant of the prosecu- tor's apprcached them. The thatch of the stack had been partially destroyed, and the hay had thereby become wet. Each of the defendants was tined 23. 6d. and 9s. 61. costs.</p>
        <p>TRANSFER.—The license of Mr. John Higham, beer- seller, Stanley-st., Runcorn, was transferred to George Stephens.</p>
        <p>Recs. forwards were now playing well together. From I a throw-in Ellison received, and worked well down, the visitors' back finding touch from a scrimmage in the home 25. Hulmos got over, but Fester failed at an easy place. Half-titne now arrived with the visitors leading by a try to nil. Final result ; Warrington "A," 2 tries, 2 minors ; St. Helens Recs. " A," nil. WIDNES v. RUNCORN. _</p>
        <p>These old opponents" opened their season on Saturday afternoon by a tussle for supremacy before 4,000 spectators. The teams were :—Widnes Full back, Case: three-quarters,Rispan, Clarke, Alforde, and Peters; half backs, Drummond and Birber ; forwards, Hogan, Whelan, King, Fraser, Derbyshire, Grace, Johnson, and Emery. Runcorn: Houghton,' full back; T. Richard- son, T, Warder, J. Butterworth, and H. Myers, three- quhrter baeks ; J. Faulkner and G.Robinson, half backs; Langley, W. Faulkner, Taylor, Williams, Davies, W. Gayter, Lightfoot, and H. Farmer, forwards. The venue was the Lowerhouse enclosure, Whines, which was in capital condition.. It need hardly be said that the keenest interest was centred in the event, equally by Runcornians and Widnesians, and this was fully demon- strated by the large number of the former who crossed Runcorn •Budge in the afternoon to watch the fortunes of their pet footballeis. Neither of the teams was fully represented: Widnes were short of Brennan,Dunbavin, and Forster, whilst Runcorn lacked the presence of IMorric, and Reece. As will be found above, however, their places were filled with good substitutes. The toss was iost by Runcorn, who were compelled to play with the sun in their faces. Operations in the first half were quite unproductive, though some very fine tactics were observed on both sides. The onslaughts of the homesters were repelled in admirable form by the visitors. Runcorn on several occasions looked extremely dangerous, but the Widnesians were well on the alert and effectually hampered them. Before the call of half-time Barber dribbled nicely and attempted to secure a try, but the Runcornians suc- ceeded in grounding the leather before it came off, and it was accordingly disallowed by the referee. Nothing had therefore been scored in the first half, and on the resumption of hostilities the same even play was wit- nessed. The visitors made several determined efforts, but nothing resulted from them. Shortly before the close of the game Rrincorn had to play a defensive game chiefly and Widnes taxed themselves to the utmost to gain some material advlantage. A few minutes before the call of full time this advantage came in the form of la try secured by Rispan. Drummond tried to convert it into a goal but failed. The game continued in the same even manner and ended :—Widnes, one try; _Runcorn, nil. _ _</p>
        <p>RUNCORN Y. WIDNEB, " A "TEAMS.—Whilst the elders were battling vigorously for the supremacy across the water, the younger members of the above clubs were showing what they were made of on the Irwell-lane enclosure, before a moderate number of witnesses. The weather was fine and the venue in good state The teams were as fellows :—Runcorn : Basnett, fullback; W. Gayter, Lightfoot, J. Nickson, T. Antrobus, three- quarter backs; E. Woodward, W. Pendlebury, half backs; J. Stubbs, A. Clarke, J. Moores, S. Johnson, C. Pendlebury, J. Pendleburg, Simpson and Derbyshire, forwards. Widnes: Toohey, full back; West, Owen, Nevitt and Percival, three-quarter backs ; Highfield and Murphy, half backs; Malone, E. Williams, T. Williams, A. Peters, McLaughlin, Hughes, Handley and Roston, forwards. It will be seen that the team was slightly different in composition from what it was last season. Widnes kicked off. but the leather was well received by the homesters wha began to press. Things then took a turn, and Widnes, securing the oval ran dangercusly I chase to the home citadel, and had not W. Pendlebury challenged, a disagreeable ending to the onslaught might have occurred. However, the ball was landed mid-field where a nice little bit of give and take play was seen. The passing was very creditable to both combinations ; the forwards showing some good metal. Again the visi- tors ground was besieged, and the lines brought into peril, but they were at last relieved with more help. Shortly after this the visitors secured the leather, and Flighfield seizing an opportunity delivered the ball near to Basnett and before that player could well nply, Murphy scored a try for the Widnesians. The attempt to make a goal of it failed. Better form was now displayed by the homesters who soon placed their oppocents on the defensive,and after szme clever passing they managed to equalize the score, the shot at goal not proving good. Half-time now arrived and the players changed ends. On the continuation of play Runcorn again pressed and were within an ace of scoring but Toohey effectually cleared. Operations now became even and for some time neither team showed up the best. Towards the close more spirit seemed to be thrown into the game, but notwithstanding the efforts of both teams neither could gain the mastery. Final score, Runcorn, one try, Wianes one try. •</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION. WATERTREE V. RUNCORN.—The Runcorn players of the Ass€ciation code opened their season on-Saturday by contesting with Wavertree, a club upon which many laurels are resting. It was only too evident at the be- ginning of hostilities that Runcorn had no.; availed them- selves of sufficient time in which to practice for the meet- ing of such a superior team, and deeply did many of the visiting players deplore this lack of precaution on their part. Tne match was played at Woodcroft, fine weather prevailing. There Was a good gate," and much interest was displayed in the movements of the respective teams. Wavertree opened the game and at once ran down to the visitors' citadel where, after a neat bit of passing, they scored. They then secured two corners but no goal resulted from either. Almost immediately, however, Griffiths possessed himself of the leather and centred, from which Gidman shot through the sticks. Scoring went on pretty merrily until half-time, when Wavertree had five goals and Runcorn none. The defence of the visitors had been. well-maintained under the circumstances, and when operations were resumed they showed better form. Wavertree took up the press- ing and addded four goals to their already high score, al- the visiting goal-keeper was very skilful in replacing many shots. The full and half backs of the Runcorn team also did good service.—Final score :—Wavertree, 5. ne goals; Runcorn, nil. EARLESTOWN V. LIVERPOOL SOUTH END.—The Earles- town club opened the football season on Saturday with a match against Liverpool South End, at Liverpool. Earlestown had not their best team in the field,but South End had got together a fairly strong combination, and they fully expected winning in an easy, manner, in this, hawever, they were disappointed- The home team won the toss, and Earlestown had to face the sun and wind. Topping set the ball rolling, and the visitors rushed to the South End goal, but were driven back, and the home forwards took up the running and commenced to bombard the Earlestown goal, but could not break down the defence of Lawrence and his supporters Peat and Wilkinson, who played in their usual good form. V 4 hen the game had been in progress about ten minutes,Sidde• ley received a severe kick on the shin,which placed him hors de combat for some time, and, in fact, interfered with his play for the remainder of the game. The visi- tors commenced to press the home backs, and after ex- periencing hard lines, C. Lee scored for Earlestown with a splendid shot. South End shortly afterwards equalised, the game at half time standing one goat each. On turn- ing round Earlestown had the sun and wind in their favour, and at once got to business, and gave the home backs no rest. 13ailey, however, twisted his knee severely, which gave South End somewhat of an advan- tage. Shortly afterwards South End broke away and scored a second goal,but Earlestown retaliated and equa- lised. The game became of an exciting character until the close, each goal la!ing severely attacked in turn, the game eventually terminating in a draw of three goals each. GARSTON COPPER WORKS V. WIDNES PARISH CHURCH.— Played at Garston, on Saturday. before a large attend- ance of spectators. Widnes kicked off. A bit of play was shown by both sides and Tench scored the first goal for the Copper works, which was soon followed by one from the Widnes back. Even play followed for ric..me time, when F. Smith took an advantage and scored a third goal, Price followinEr, suit with a splendid shot. The game ended—Garston Copper Works, six goals ; Parish Church, one goal. BARNTON Aerator; v. PENKETII.—At Penketh, on Satur- day, before a small gate. Bowyer kicked off for the homesters, but the Barnton forwards gained possession, and with a dash drew in front of Jackson, who was beaten by Acton in less than a minute from the com- mencement. Before half-time, three more had been registered by Acton, S. Hatton and A. Rose. After the cross-Over the game became of a rough and tumble char- acter, each side scoring once. Barnton thus won their first League engagement by five goals to one.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT -F-ROll A RUNCORN HOTEL.</p>
        <p>ACCUSED ARRESTED IN CHESTER. Ellen Turner, an elderly and respectably dressed woman, was charged before Mr. S. Taylor at the Runcorn Police Court on Wednesday morning with stealing a pair of blankets value 135., on the 4th September the property of James Lea, proprietor of the Temperance Hotel, Runcorn. Inspector Hicks,iaformed Mr. Taylor that the prison,r was charged with stealing the blankets from the Tem- perance Hotel on Tuesday morning. She had gone there for a bed on the previous night and the following morn- ing went away, taking the blankets of her bed with her. From inquiries the police had discovered that she had gone to Chester: As a result prisoner was arrested the same night in Chester,and subsequently the blankets were recovered from the pawnshop of Mr. Burton, East- gate-street,Chester. He (the Inspector) desired a remand for about seven days in order that the police investiga- tions might he completed. Margaret Sweetman,waitress at the Temperance Hotel deposed to seeing the blankets (produced) safe in the Hotel on Monday morning. On Monday night the prisoner called and ordered a bed which was accordingly prepared for her. The next morning she went away, and at about nine o'clock the blankets were missed from her bed. P.C. Richardson proved going to the pawnshop of Mr. Burton, of Eistgate-street, Chester, that (Wednesday) morning and reclvering the blankets. He brought the prisoner from Chester to Runcorn. Prisoner was remanded for a week pending further inquiries.</p>
