        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>GEO. G. BUSSEY &amp; CO. are the leading malters of requisites for Football, Hockey. Golf, Basebadl, Gymnasia, and all Sports and Games. 'illustrated catalogues may be obtained foam the local deakra, or the Sports Manufactory, Peckham, London. Trade</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. ST. RELENS RECREATION.</p>
        <p>The Warrington club opened the present footbal season on Saturday, when they had for their opponents, the well-known St. Helens Recreation team. The weather was more favourable for a cricket match than a football match, and during the match the players per- spired pretty freely. This fine weather of course was the means of attracting a large "gate," and there would be about 4,000 people present when the game began. The referee was Mr. H. Williamson, of Salford, and the players under his charge were ..:—Warrington, fleesom, full back ; Carey, Potts. Evana, and Webster, three quarter backs sJ. T. Bate, and Clarke, half backs; Fair Turner, Jolley, Deakin, Burrows, Houghton,Taylor, Perry, and Buckley, forwards. Et. Helens Recreation, Crossley, full back; Ellison, Seddon, Traynor, and fluehes, three quarter backs ; Orford, and Molyneux, half backs; Fildes. Dolan, Potts, Chapman, Reynolds, Briers, Sidler, and Mosedale, forwards. The, gentlemen in red won the toss, and set their opponents to defend the railway end. The ball was set in motion by 'lurner whose kick was returned by Molyneux. Play for a time settled down at the Rees' quarter flag, and in spite of several breaks away, they were penned in their quarters. Warrington still gained ground,and Potts by a grand run through his opponents took play to the line. Scrimmage after scrimmage ensued en the lines and Seddisn trying te. break away, was finely tackled by Jolley. The Warrington forwards again took the ball to the fine, but just failed to get over. The Rees at length got relief by a tremendous punt from Molyneux, and they by this means crossed the half way line for the first time. They now became very dangerous, and a most dangerous rush was stopped by Webster. Play was now in the Warrington 25, and Warrington here had a great fright,for a kick by Efeesom was charged down by Sidler who now had a clear course. before him. Webster and Evans, however just caught him in time, and with the help of Carey they repelled the invaders. The Rees at this portion of the match played grandly, and it took ffeesom all his time to keep his charge intact. The prolonged struggle on the Warrington lines ended in a drop at goal by Seddon, which went just outside the posts, and the first minor was thus scored. After the kick out a scramble all over the field ended in no favourable result to either side, but at length a free kick was conceded to Warrington. Webster attempted the place but was unsuccessful, Orford warding off the danger. An un- fair tackle by John Bate gave a free kick to the Recs., which took them half way. Free kicks from marks became very frequent, which ended in Jolley secnring' and by a judicious kick he placed the St. Helens line in I danger. One tar two of the St. Helens players appeared to have become too excited in consequence, and one of them by a foolish pass to Webster almost let Warrington in. Jolley, Carey,end Potts were successively held near the line, and at length a fruitless minor was conceded. After the kick out, A good run by Carey was spoiled by a wild pass. A spell of mouotonons forward play en- sued, until Hughes got away, but was tackled by by Carey at a critical moment. Half time came at length v‘ith each team having scored a useless minor. Seddon re-started the game after the interval, and Web- ster returned the kick. Evans tried to break away, but found Crossley in the way, and Briers in clothe the same for the Recs.,also came across Webster. Houghton now obtained the ball from a scrimmage and made the best dribble of the afternoon, but his career was cut short by , Crossley. The next ten minutes' play was of a very un- interesting character, but the monotony was broken at length by Jolley having a race for the line, but he found the way to it too much blocked. TheWarrington team, however, attacked strongly, and a St. Helens player endeavoured to obtain relief by kicking over the line. A scrimmage was consequently ordered,and Carey olozain- taining the ball ran well to Crossley. He now kicked over that player's head, but the' kick was a trifle too strong, and the ball had become dead before he could reach it again. The kick out gave but slight relief to the Itecselor Jolley made one of his characteristic rushes Which was spoiled by Mosedale. The Recs. in their turn attacked, and Houghton's free kick was charged down, hut the Warrington defenders stepped in and cleared the danger. An offside tackle by Chapman was the means of giving great relief to Si arrington, and a long spell of forward play was broken by some fine passing between Clarke. Jolley, Evans, Potts, and Webster, the latter of whom was tackled in the nick of time. In spite of all their exertions, neither side could score anything substantial, and a not very interesting game ended with the score of Warrington two minors, St.Helens Recs. one minor. 'WARRINGTON y. BROUGHTON RANGERS. A friendly match took place on Monday night on the Rangers ground, before a good number of spectators. ''he home team was without the services .of Hacking and Squires (who were badly injured on Saturday), hut they had the assistance of Pearson, of Swinton, whilst Warrington were well represented. Fair Turner kicked off, and the forwards at once rushed the ball close to the-home line, where several tight scrimmages took place. At length the home team were penalsed, and Plumpton attempted at goal, but only a minor re- sulted. Resuming, the Rangers were quickly inside their opponents quarters, and from some neat passing between Hosker, Garrity. and Whitehead, Fleet was well tackled close to the visitors' line. For some time Warrington were forced to act on the defensive, but Potts at length removed the pressure with a good run to the home quarter, where the visiting forwards dribbled the hall over the Rangers line,but only a minor resulted. .Another minor quickly followed. Resuming Garrity made a capital dodgy run, but Jolley headed a loose dribble by the visitors, and carried play back to the home quarter. Half-time arrived without any score. Ends were quickly charred, and Whitehead kicked off, when the Ringers forwards carried play to the visitors' line. Potte relieved slightly, but Whitehead got posses- sion and dropped at goal, but only a minor resulted. Resuming, even play followed at centre, until Plumpton :put in a high kick, and following well up tackled Tweedale close to his own line. The Rangers were severely pressed for some time, and several hard fought scrimmages followed on their line. Ultimately a com- bined rush by the Rangers forced Warrington back to midfield, where Pearson got hold and ran strongly, but he was well tackled by Heesom lust in time to prevent a score. The Rangers then pressed and Warrington were penalised, but Whitehead made an unsuccessful attempt at goal. A last and evenly contested game ended: in a draw, no major point sccred. The followipg were the teams :—Broughton Rangers: F. TWeedale, full back; W. Pearson, R. Whitehead, W. Fleet, and G. Berry,three- quarter backs ; J. Garrity, and R. Hosker, half backs; J. Worthington, G. Skidmore, J. Shepherd, W. Camp- bell, J. Fitton, J. Clapham, R. Medley, and J. Sander- .son, forwards. Warrington : F. Heesom' full back ;H. Webster, J. Evans, F. Plumpton, and C. Potts, three- quarter backs; John Bate and P. Carey, half backs; Fair Turner, J. Jolley, J. W. Taylor, E. Bailey, W. Bur-, ' rows, E. Berry J. Holmes, and W. Houghton,forwards. Touch judges: Mr. T. Barnes, Warrington ; and Mr. R. Davies, Broughton Rangers. Referee: Mr. Crossley. RUNCORN RECREATION V. WIDNES . Sr. MARY'S.—These clubs opened the season on Saturday, on the Recreation ground, opposite Wigg's Works. Both sides had selected very good teams in the week, but when the time came to commence &amp; good number of the originally selected men failed to put in an appearance. The house club was heavily handicapped in this respect. However.the full complement turned out, and at four o'c,jock O'Connor kicked off down hill, for St. Mary's. The opening play was very even, and neither side claimed any advantage. A pass was nicely taken by Hughes, who easily evaded Wheelan and scored the first try of the season for the Recreation, which was not converted. H ilf-time arrived with the score one try to nil in favour of the Runcorn men. On resuming, after a lengthy interval, the match became very interesting, and both sides played a good open game. A good dribble was made by the Recs. and Brennan scored. The home men had all the best of the latter stages of the game and registered another try by Brennan, who beat Wheelan in getting possession, the kick at goal in each instance failing. Runcorn Recreation won the first match of the season by three tries and three minors to Widnes St. Mary's nil. Sr. HELENS 'RECS.. V. WARRINGTON (A TEA3l9).—Played at St. Helens before a fair crovri. The Recs. won the toss, and Warrington kicked off. Even play followed until the home back got a free kick and reached their opponents' territory. Scrimmages were now the order, when the visitors were awarded a free kick, which, how- ever, brought no relief. The visitors by strong forward play secured the first minor. The drop out was well replied to by the Warrington centre. Archer returned with interest frem a scrimmage, and then made a splen- did effort, being held dangerously near the line. The</p>
      </div>
