      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>WHY PEOPLE ARE HOPEFUL.</head>
        <p>WHY PEOPLE ARE HOPEFUL.</p>
        <p>The opinion of the poet is that "lope springs eternal in the human breast." It is certainly true that even in the most adverse circumstances people cling to hope— for the reason, probably, that, however gloomy prospects may appear, a turn c omes even to the most hopeless. An interesting story of hope found in a hopeless case is told us by a '6 Dewsbury Chronicle." reporter, in that important newspaper. The other day, he writes. I visited Mrs. Benjmnin Smith. She lives in Back Han- over-street, Boothroyd-lane, Dewsbury. I had heard that she had been a hopeless sufferer, but had at last found relief from pain. Was that so?</p>
        <p>Mrs. Smith's was a grateful affirmative answer. Ten years ago, she stated—she is fifty now—" change of life "left her with chronic rheumatism in the head and , eyes. Her last illness commenced fifteen months ago. The pain in her head was too acute for words. She could not describe it. She got no sleep either night or day. Day followed—day and night followed—it was the same; for her there was neither rest, nor peace, nor sleep. Nor could the most skilful medical attention give her relief. She bad a naturally sluggish liver and a weak heart,and her illness had so preyed upon her eyesight that in her own graphic words she " could only see the shadows" of objects before her. But, in this as in many another case, hope was not futile. "I saw an account in the paper," said Mrs. Smith to the reporter, " of the marvellous cures effected where all ordinary medicine had failed, by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and my husband got me a hox. I had only taken half when the pain left me just like magic, and it's to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills alone that I'm indebted. I've had only two boxes. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills had indeed, as all the neigh. bourhood was declaring, wrought a modern miracle in this case, and they give hope even where all hope seems lost, especially to women, • whose sufferings in late life so often appear incapable of relief. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills are an unfailing cure for rheumatism, sciatica, neuralgia, partial paralysis, loco- motor ataxy, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, pros- tration, and all cases in which the system needs to be strengthened and built up. They are a specific for the troubles of the female sex, and in men effect a radical cure in cases arising from mental worry, overwork, or excesses of any kind. They are sold by chemists,or may be had direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn-viaduct. London, at 2s. 9d. a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d. Dr. Williams' jPink Pills are never sold loose, and the wooden box must b 0 in a pink wrapper, bearing XI name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pala People. Not a purgative.</p>
        <p>Photographs have been taken 500 feet under water. The attempt to establish a lloard of Couci.liation iu the . Durham coal trade has failed.</p>
