      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DRO WNING FATALITIES AT SANKEY BRIDGES AND PEN KETII.</head>
        <p>DRO WNING FATALITIES AT SANKEY BRIDGES AND PEN KETII.</p>
        <p>Mr.L. Brighouse held an inquest at the Black Horse Inn, Sankey Bridges, on Tuesday morning,on the body of a man unknown, which was found in the St. Helens and Sankey Canal, at Penketh, on Friday. August 31. —Jacob Roe said that he lived at Old Lock, Penketh. On Friday he was going along the canal bank when he noticed a body floating in the water. He at once went to Penketh and informed P.C. Thorley.—P.C. Thorley said that from information he received from the last witness,he want to the canal and pulled the body out of the water. Reexamined the body and found no marks of violence. It appeared to be the body of a man about 35 years of age. He had dark brown hair and was dressed in moleskin trousers. The body appeared to have been in the water for some time. No one had been to identify the body. The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned." Mr. Brighouse also held an inquest upon the body of George Blaze. aged 14 years, son of Wm. Blaze, 27, Wright-st., Sankey Bridges, who was drowned in the , river Mersey on August 26.—Wm. Blaze said that he ' last saw his son alive op August 22, when he went out of the house saying that he was going to have a walk. Sometime the same evening the deceased's clothes were , brought to him by a boy named Webb,who said that his' son had been drowned in the weir. Deceased could not swim.—Frank Miller said that he met deceased and two or three other boys on August 26. Deceased asked them to go into the river near Monks' forge, but they would not. Witness then went to meet another boy,and when he returned theysaw the deceased's headabove the water and then saw it sink. He never came to the surface again. There was nobody with the deceased when he went into the water.—Wm. Warburton. Quay Fold,gave evidence to pulling the body out of the river, near to Fairclouth's mills, Bank Quay, on Saturday, September Ist. The jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
        <p>HOLY TRINITY S.S • V. ST. ANN'S. —This match was played on the ground of the latter, but before the game could be finished, the Homesters not being able to agree with the umpire's decision Holy Trinity were obliged to abandon the field in disgust. The scores were Holy Trinity S.S., 58; St. Anne, 20 for five wickets.</p>
