      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>BOROUGH TREASURER'S REPORT CRITICISED.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH TREASURER'S REPORT CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>AN INCREASED DEBT OF £5,000.</p>
        <p>The municipal elections are now within measur- able distance, and the ratepayers of Warrington will then have an opportunity of obtaining pledges from the would-be councillors for their good behaviour during the next three years. Many questions arise on these occasioas, but, considering the high state of the rates of the borough,it is rather singular that the question of finance should he so often left in the back ground. With a view to bringing this import- ant question prominently before the public, an "Examiner" reporter waited upon the gentleman who a short time ago contributed the articles upon finance, in the "Examiner," in order to obtain his views upon the subject. "What do you think about the borough trea- surer's report recently issued ?" asked the reporter. " Well," said the gentleman, "a comparison of the report for the year ending March, 1894, with that for the year ending March, 1893, shows the following results :—The borough indebtedness has increased from £669,873 to £684,873, an increase of £15,000. The amount repaid un account of Cs e debt was £10,032, leaving an indebtedness of £4,968 more than in the previous year. The ratepayers might very well ask what have we to show for this increased indebtednes3 ? ' If the corporation finances are administered in like ratio. in 50 or 60 years, which is the time calculated on the most. lib- eral allowance for the extinguishment of the debt, the ratepayers ot Warrington would be face to face with an increased debt of £300,000. Why cannot the ratepayers be placed before them, in the borough treasurer's report, a clear statement show- ing the actual amount of the debt, the amount paid in interest each year and the amount paid in the reduction of the principal, together with the total list of assets? That is to say, the current market value of the water works, the gas .works, the Longford manure works, the cemetery, the museum and school of art, the town hall build- ings, the fire engine station, the baths, and the markets and open spaces,together with the estimated value of the roads, sewers, docks, quays and water frontages on the river, and the houses and real estate. The borough rate is G. 6c. in the .E.and the assessable value of the Corporation rate is £154,000, which gives a yearly income of £50,000 to which must be added the income from the gas undertak- ing, the water works and ether receipts. The total income which need .not be itemized, should. be set forth with the expenditure. on account of the vari- ous departments,and it would form a balance sheet much simpler than the one issued. The Mayor, aldermen, councillors, and burgesses, would then have before them a clear and intelligible statement of accounts, which would enable the officials and those I.olding authority to effect economies in the way ot general expenditure where necessary which at present seems utterly impossible. In the face of the increased rates, and the improvements that are so urgently required, some such statement as this would be of immense benefit to the town." "And what is your opinion of the retiring coun- cillors ?" "The retiring councillors this year for the vari- ous wards are, Town Hall, Councillor Smethurst; IVhitecross, Councillor Plumpton ; Bewsey, Coun- cillor Flood; Orford, Councillor Baird; St. John's, Councillor Garner; Fairfield, Councillor Woods; Howley, Councillor Evans; St. Austin's, Councillor J. Fairclough ; and Latchford, Councillor Tinsley. As the elections take place on November Ist, the ratepayers will naturally expect some account from these gentlemen of their stewardship during their term of office. They have had opportunities without number of moving a resolution in the Council for a return such as the one proposed, but no such resolution has been proposed. This return is not an unreasonable one. In fact there are many corporations that are distinguished on account of their low rates, and the advantages which exist in the towns they represent, such as electric lighting, tramways, winter gardens, boule- vards, fountains, monuments, covered ways, etc., all of which tend to improve the town, attract the population,and increase the prosperity and influence of the community." "What do you think of the various benefactions to the town during the past year ?" "I think that great credit is due to Mr. J. Charleton Parr for his gift to the town of the public hall, to Mr. F. Monks for his gift of the park gates, and also to those gentlemen by whose influence the improvements on the Central Station have been brought about. But these efforts on the part of a few should be backed up by the members of the Cor- poration, and year by year a marked improvement, in the town should be seen. Everything depends upon whether the Corporation properly admin- ister their finances. With this right, other things become easy, and the improvement grows apace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>SHOP MANAGER CHARGED WITH</head>
        <p>A SHOP MANAGER CHARGED WITH</p>
        <p>ASSAULT.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATES DISAGREE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Court onFriday,Frank Garrett, manager for Mr. Harrop, furniture dealer, Bridgesstreet,was charged with assaulting and beat- ing Harriet Foster, Scott-street, on August 27. Mr. Percy DAviPs prosecuted and said that in 1892, his client purchased from Mr. Garrett a quantity of furniture to the amount of £2l 24. 10d. and entered into an agree- ment to pay so much per week. Mr. Garrett promised her that if the money were paid at once, he would knock £5 off the bill. OH August 27, she determined to pay the balance 10s. 10d. off, and she went to the shop in Briige-street. She asked what he would knock off that. He replied that he would knock 10d. off the bill. Some words ensued between the two parties when Garrett suddenly got into a temper ani ran at her and gave her a violent blow in the face saying he would kill her. Garrett's finger nail scratched tier face the whole length, and complainant would have fallen to the ground if a woman passing had not caught her in time.—The complainant gave corroborative evidence.and also Annie Leigh.—Mr. Browne, who defended, said that his nt denied absolutely striking the complainant, but said that she came into the shop and wanted the whole 101. and 10d. to be knocked off. When he refused, the woman became excited and Mr. Garrett had to put her .4ut.—An assistant named Griffiths was called, and said that Mr. Garnett simply hustled the complainant mkt of the shop.—After a long consultation, Alderman Harrison presiding magistrate, said that the bench was equally divided about the cace,there being two for con- viction and two for discharging. Fle himself was satis- fied that an assault had been cotnmit,,,ed, but as the bench was equally divided the case would be dismissed. Dr. Gornall said that'he considered the evidence of the complainant and h-r witness had been given in a satis- factory manner, while Councillor Roberts and Mr. S. Wright on the other hand said that they were of opinion that the complainant had gone to the shop in a furious manner, and that Garnett had not used any more violence than was necessary to put her out of the shop.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 25th August, 1894. During the week, which was the 34th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington. 26 8 Latchford 4</p>
        <p>Total 3O</p>
        <p>The excess of births over deaths was 22. The death- rate from all causes was equal to 7 8 per 1,000, against 14.8 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 301. the corresponding week of last sear. The death- rate from the seven chief zyrnotic diseases was equal to nil per 1,000, against 2.7 the average rate of the four previous weeks,and against 12 2 the corresponding week ot 1893. The ages of mortality were- 0-1. 1-5 5-15 15-25, 25-60 60 up. 2 2 1 1 2 8</p>
        <p>- -</p>
        <p>The deaths included-2 from tubercular disease, 1 from old age, 1 from heart disease, 2 from lung disease, 1 from drowning, 1 from debility. The following cases of infectious disease have heen reported during Om week :—Scarlet fever 5 cases, enteric fever 1 case. METl3oltoLnl3lf.—The mean temperature was 55-5 Fdh, and therefore 4 deg. colder than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 66 F.. was rec., d-d on the 23rd inst., and the minimum temperature 44 F., on the, 22nd inst. The.rainfall measured 1.06 inch. The general direction wa4 N.' ,the reading of the Anemometer denoting 1,070 miles. _ _</p>
        <p>JOLIN H. GORNALL, Mediccal Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>WORTH KNOWING.—WOODEND'S FAMOUS NINE OILS, is boon to sufferers and I cts like magic in all cases of RHEUMATISM, SPRAINS, BRUISES, and STIFF JOINTS. Pri,e Is. 4d, tier bottle, carriage paid.—J.WooDEND, chemist. 22. Pitt-street. Liverg2.3l. —Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>TECHNICAL EDUCATION AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>TECHNICAL EDUCATION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Museum and Technical Instruction Com- mittee have provided an excellent programme tor the .winter session of the Municipal Technical Institute. The School of Art, which commences on Tuesday, will again be under the supervision of Mr. ' Aylward, as headmaster, and he will be assisted by a competent staff of assistants. There are all the usual subjects far instruction, and the headmaster wishes especially to call the attention of advanced students ta the circumstance, that under a new regulation of the Science and Art Department, work car- ried out in various materials, such as earthen- ware, porcelain, glass, metal work, wood carv- textiles, or needlework, Isisy now be sent up to Kensington forthe National Competition, along with the designs for the same ; also that the County Council of Lancashire offers annually to art students a number of Exhibitions, value £lO each, w hich are awarded upon the result of a competitive examination in outline drawing of ornament from cast, shading ornament from cast. The examination is held at Warrington School of Art, and other places, in June, and residents in Warrington are leligible to compete, without limit of age. A special class is held for the study oUstill-life painting in oil or water- colour. The monthlY'skstching competition, which was cominesced last session, will be continued, the subjects being set and the work criticised by the headmaster as before. The continuation classes will commence on Monday, Septem%ier 17th, and embrace a large number of subjects. Those who have just left school will find these classes of great assistance. Prizes will be given for regular atten- dance. The Committeis would press upon beginners the importance of laying a sufficient groundwork of elemen- tary general knowledge, such as is provided in the pre- paratory classes of the continuation school, before taking up the serious study of those subjects more particularly related to their daily occupation. With the view of encouraging this action on the part of students, the Com- mittee has placed 32 free admissions to the clntinuation school at the disposal of the head teachers of elementary schools, and' has awardedl3 scholarships, tenable partly in the higher classes, to the most successful students of last session in that department. On the same date (September 17th). the School cf Science will commence its session. There is a large and varied list of subjects for the students of Warrington to choose from, and it is to be hoped that all the classes will be successful. They include.scientific, technical.and commercial subjects,hesides music and manual training. It will he noticed on the syllabus that an honours , stage of Iron and Steel has been added, and two short courses of lectures on chemical analysis, for the benefit of students of practical chemistry will be given by Mr. Ruddock. An advanced class in music is also provided. Physiography is omitted, a class in this Subject having been undertaken by the pupil teachers' classes commit- tee. The attention of intending students is called to the various scholarships which are to he competed for by the members of the classes. The Museum and Technical Instruction Committee offer a scholarship (tenable at the Institets next session), to each student who complies with the rules of the school, and takes a fint class in the Spring Examinations. The value of a Scholarship is ss. for the elementary stage, and 10s. for the advanced stage. The County Council of Lancashire offer annually scholarships (value £6O yearly for three years), and exhibitions (value £10) for proficiency in science or commercial subjects. The next examina- tions will be held probably in June. 1895. The subjects of examinations are Science Exhibitions : inoreanic chemistry, physics (heat, electricity, and magnetism), mechanics, mathematics. Candidates must take three out of the four subjects. Comi..ercial Exhibitions : arithmetic, English grammar and compssition, book- keeping, and either French or German. It will be noticed that the classes cover this examination. 'the Union of Lancashire and Cheshire Institutes also awards Prizes (1) to successful candidates in each sub- ject in which examinations are held by the Union, (2) on the result of certain of the science examinations, (3) for sets of Mechanical and Architectural Drawings. The Union also has the disposal of valuable Scholarships to successful science students. All the awards are limited to students of Institutes in union. In addition to the above c mipetitions Queen's prizes of books, Szcs, are given to a small number oil hose who, having previously. passed in the elementary stage, do best in the advanced stage of each ' subject. Prizes are also given for sets of inked-in drawings sent no by stu- dents in machine or building construction. R iyal ex- hibitions of £5O a year, national scholarships valus 304. a week, and tree studentships at the ltsyal College of Science, and Whitworth scholarships are aw on the result of the May cssaminations. For conditions of these scholarships, the students are rsquested to see the direc- tory of the department, at the Museum. Full syllabuses of the classes can be obtained sn application at the Museum or School of Art.</p>
        <p>" CADBURY'S COCOi has in a remarkable degree those natural elements of sustenance which give the system endurance ansi hardihood, building up muscle and HAHN' vigour with a steady r,etion that renders it a most reliab.c awl Accertable bevelage."-lical4.</p>
