      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>THERE ARE PLASTERS AND PLASTERS.</head>
        <p>THERE ARE PLASTERS AND PLASTERS.</p>
        <p>• DR. r YNAWS AMERICAN PINE PLASTERS. ARE MARVELLOUS AND RELIABLE. If you are troubled with Indigestion. Wind, or Sick- ness. ON'r TAKE MEDICINE. THY PYNAh'S PINE PLASTER, the mos Wonderful Cure for Back Ache, Side Ache, hheurnatism Lumbago. Sciatica; in fact, the very worst Aches or Pains. THEY ..kCT LIKE MAGIC. Are Grat ful, Comforting. Stimulating, AND BRACE UP THE WHOLE SYSTEM. Astonishin:g Testimonials from all Classes. SUFFERERS FROM ASTHMA SHOULD TRY THESE WONDERFUL PLASTERS. DON'T HESIT LTE. TRY TUIS WONDERFUL EMEDY AT ONCE. If your Chemist has not got it, sent Post Free for 91d. by the Proprietors. LAWRENCE, AND CO., CHEMISTS, RHYL. AGENTS : RUNCORN—Mr. J. H. Weston, Chemist. WIDNES—Mr. J. Bennett, Chemist, 32. Victoria Road. ZARLESTOWN—Mr. A. Peake, Chemist. WARRINGTON—Mr. J. R. Young, Chemist. 81. HELENS—Mr. G. Stringfellow, Central Stores. NORTHW ICH—Mr. C. M. Blades Chemist.</p>
        <p>THINGS ILEARD AND SEEN AT WARRINGTON. About two years ago there was some talk of the Corporation providing an open space for Latchford. The site selected was the old burial place of St. James' in St. James'-street, which is hidden from public view by a high stone wall. The idea was to take the wall down, and build it along side the river which runs cn one side of the burial ground. It was not, however, intended to make it a public playground but a miniature park, with beds., walks and seats. I have often wondered what has be- come of this scheme. That stone wall still faces the public in St. James'-street and the conspicuous inscription attracts much attention. There was no impediment in the way, I understand, of the trans- ference of the ground to the Corporation, as the authorities had given permission for the place to be used as suggested. It was for the Corporation to take the matter in hand. Can it be that the Sankey- street improvement has been carried out instead? If so, I should think that Latchford people have just a little cause for grumbling.</p>
        <p>Not many months ago, Mr. Asquith, the Home Secretary, was the butt of ridicule, owing to a slip of the tongue at a speech at Cambridge. In thit speech he alluded to the Newcastle programme, and said that the Liberals were not going to drop "one tot or one jittle " of it, meaning, of course, "one jot or one tittle." On Saturday week a magis- trates' clerk at a court near Warrington, committed a:similar ludicrous mistake. A man was brought up on the charge of cruelty to a horse, and the clerk charged him "with cruelly illtreating a horse, to wit, an animal, by travelling it shi;e in an unfit state." He found his mistake of transposing the words" horse" and " animal " after he had said it, and read the charge out again amid the laughter a the court. In these democratic days, dukes, marquises and the like are apt to become less awe-inspiring to the public generally, and are looked upon for the most part as fortunate individuals who have come into their positions through the accident of birth. Con- sequently a duke is more an object of curiosity than anything else to most people. A member of the Warrington cycling club, however, has not yet overcome a feeling of embarrassment when coming into the presence of these august personages, and last week he had a rather awkward accident in con- sequence. He was riding in Eaton Park, in com- pany with several companions, when he saw the Duke of Westminster driving in a carriage towards him. In trying to get out of the way of his Icrd- ship, he was so confused, that his bicycle and the duke's horse came into collision, and he was thrown to the ground, but fortunately was not hurt. The duke did not show any signs of anger at the unto- ward circumstance, but had a hearty laugh at the cyclist's expense. Alderman Harrison is noted for the interest he takes in the cases which come before him, and for the lectures be usually gives the defendants. These lectures are not generally appreciated, but on Friday at the Police Court. the worthy alderman was asked by Mr. Solicitor Knowles to give his client a fatherly lecture for doing what he ought not to have done. Alderman Wright complained that there was rather too much business for lectures,but the lecture came nevertheless, much to the amuse- ment of the court. Mr. Knowles' client was fined 40s. and costs in addition, but Mr. Knowles con- soled himself by saying, "Never mind, we have had the lecture." • .</p>
        <p>Swimming galas are becoming very popular in Warrington, and it is almost safe to say that water polo matches are the most popular games at present in the town. The large number pregent on Friday at the baths to witness the two polo matches and the other swimming events is a proof ot this. In fact so eagerly were the tickets sought after that by on Friday afternoon everyone was sold. Warring.: ton has been exceedingly fortunate since the new baths were opened in obtaining the best fixtures that could be obtained in water polo, but none have been equal in importance to the 'North v.South match. In this match the very best players in England were in full array against each other, and it Nina from the teams that the All England team was chosen. The Baths Committee scored heavily indeed when they erpbarlred in the projeot of building a new bath. MERLIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EXTRAORDINARY DEATH AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY DEATH AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>TEN ItIB6 BROKEN. At the St. Helens Town Hall on Tuesday after- noon, Mr.Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest on the body of John Manchester, aged sixty-four years, a waggon greaser employed at the Peasley Cross Colliery, who was found dead on the railway siding on Saturday morning. Mrs. Esther Hender- son, of 33. Peter-st., daughter of deceased, gave evidence of identification. James Fishwick, employed at M'Kechnie's chemical works, adj)ining the colliery, said he saw deceased at half-past nine about forty yards from the place where he was found dead. John Thomas Marsh. shunter, had seen deceased at nine o'clock, and found laim dead at ten o'clock alongside the colliery line. He was on his knees face downwards, with one hand on the grease box. Witness did not think any of the waggons had been moved, and Man- chester had apparently fallen on the line. He did not think he had been run over. Dr. James Wallace said he was called to deceased on Saturday morning and found him dead. From a super- ficial examination he found two ribs broken. He, had made a pod mortar' examination. Externally he found, a slight mark over the chest, more especially on the right side. That was caused by effusion of blood over the fifth rib on the right side. The ribs were extremely brittle. On the right side the fifth, sixth, seventh and eighth ribs were fractured, and on the left side six ribs were fractured. They were so brittle that he could break them as easily as a piece of wood of the same thickness. As an instance, while he was separating the lung from the chest wall another rib broke. The Coroner: Do you think the ribs could be broken by the man fallinß ? Witness: I think the fifth rib on the right side was broken by a fall, and the others probably with moving the body after death. I don't think he received any violence by being run over or knocked down. There were no signs of injury. Death was due to hemorrhage, caused by the fifth rib, which was fractured, lacerating the lung and piercing one of the arteries of the heart. Witness (continuing) did not think the man had been seized with a fent or fit, but was of opinion that he had caught his foot against the rail and fallen. The Foreman said he could scarcely get at why a man should have four or six ribs broken with a fall, except he had peen crushed or knocked down. The Coroner said he found it so in a great many cases of deaths of old folks where they had got out of bed and fallen on the floor, and there been found with ribs broken. Re thought the medical profession agreed that a person falling on the floor might break a number of</p>
        <p>Dr. Wallace: Yes: considering the peculiar condition of the ribs. The Foreman said the man had been lost sight of for half an hour, and they did not know what had become of him. Be was seen forty yards from where he was found, and they did not know what had transpired. . The Coroner saki that was the view he would take of' it. There was practically- nothing proved. It was not a death frem natural causes. It was a death from injuries, and the jury had to consider how the injuries were inflicted. The jury returned a verdict that deceased met his death while follovrtog his employment, but in what &lt;lefty it bad come about there was no evidence to show.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. I have placed this subject for first consideration this week, because for the next eight months, cricket will have to take a back seat, and football will rule supreme. For nearly the whole of the summer the cricketers have been sighing for good weather,but their sighs have passed unheeded. On Satur- day, however, when the whiter pastime commenced its ' innings, the weather was simply charming, and just the kind for cricket. This is, of course, one of the many freaks of the English weather. This is jut the kind of weather for the spectators, but what about the players. It is rather hard work to be running all over the tield after a ball, with the hot sun ablaze overhead: It is an excellent method of reducing the rotundity of *ne's figure.</p>
        <p>The "gates" all over the country were of a monstre character, and the treasurers had a good time of it. The I Warrington club had arranged a very attractive fixtute ' to commence the season with, in the shape of the St. Helens Recreation team. This team arzways draws, and although the football fever has not yet got to its height, there were about 4,CCO' spectators to see the first kick of the season which was performed by Pair Turner. The Rees were not so strong last year as in previous years, and as a result Warrington were able to wipe out the arrears of defeats. Previous to Saturday's match, the two teams had met eleven times, and each had won four, the other three having been drawn. Warrington had, however, made the most of their opportunities in scor- ing, for whereas they scored five goals, ten tries, the Recs scored four goals, nine tries. On Saturday the relations of the two teams remained unaltered, for try as hard as they could, neither side could elude the vigil- eine of the other.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team have changed but slightly from last year. With the exception of Webster, all the players took part in matches last season. " Smack " Bate had been announced to play, but was anable to do so, but an able substitute was found in Claike. Heesom at his old post as full back was as agile in fielding the ball as he was last season. Of the four three-quarters, Carey did the best work, althlugh Evans was not far behind. Potts was not quite ready enough in parting with the ball, whilst Webster was very erratic. He was wateised pretty closely by the other side. John Bate and Clarke did not do anything sensational, but hell their own against the other side. The most conspicuous players among the forwards were Jolley and Houghton, the former of whom , ought to be in the county team again this season, and achieve even still higher honours.</p>
        <p>The match on Saturday was not a very good one from a spectator's point of view, but with the sun blazing overhead it was not to be expected that the players would be as energetic as in colder weather. The War- rington team had slightly the best of the game, but taken as a whole the score Of two minors to one minor about represents the abilities of the two teams on Saturday.</p>
        <p>After their pointless game on Saturday., the Warring- ton team journeyed to Broughton on Monday to meet the Rangers of that district, and another pointless game was the result. As this was not a league match, the Warrington team have still a chance of beating the "Griffins." In this match, Carey appeared in the character of half back in place of Clarke. The match is described by those who witness-A it as an excellent exhibition of Rugby football. Plumpton Aide his re-appearance in this match, and gave every promise or being a decided acquisition. The result of the match on Monday was better to Warrington than the initial meeting of the two teams last year, for on that occasion, Warrington unexpectedly suffered a severe defeat, the first and only one they have ever re- ceived at the hands of the Broughton Rangers.</p>
        <p>To-day the Warrington team are enjoying them- selves, as a preliminary to their struugle for supremacy in the League. They are on a visit to Manxland, and are showing the natives how football ought to be played. It is to be hoped that they Will return like 'giants re- freshed, and that they will then be able to bowl over the other members of the league, one by one, until they capture the cup. However, we had better get out of the wood before we shout.</p>
        <p>The players for the match with Mona to-day are full- back, Heesorn ; three-quarter backs, Carey, Plumpton, Evans and Potts: half-hacks, James T. Bate and John T. Bate ; ' forwards. Fair Turner. Jolley, Buckley, Thornily, Berry. Deakin, Taylor, and Burrows.</p>
        <p>The A team opened their season away from home, and met the " Rees " Aat St. Helens. Notwithstanding that fact, they came out of the ordeal successfullv, and have a win and two tries to be going on with. They have begun very strongly, and a most successful season is looked forward to. I hope to see them again earn the title of "Champion A team of Lancashire." Caicicirr. • Although tbe big soft ball is brought into requisition, the little hard ball is still required, but there is now very little interest taken in it. It is getting time it was laid by in Warrington, when our local. team go to Preston, and after scoring 86.0 f which the two professionals Bates. and Clark claimed 37 between them, they allow the Prestonians to run up a score of 214. for the loss of only seven wickets. All the Prestonians got into double figures except Leigh, and he very probably would have done had he had time. There were no *fewer than five "ducks "scored by the Warringtonlans. Elowever, the less said the better.</p>
        <p>After scoring seven idackh' and no extras,t he Cairo-st. Unity were equal to the task of beating the Y.M.C.A. on the latter's ground. Monks was the saviour of his side as he scored an invaluable 20. and Cook and he between them just equalled the Y.M.C.A. score. rowna.ll also took six wickets for seven runs for the Unity.</p>
        <p>The Christian Endeavour -.had a bad time of it on Saturday when they met the Penketh Wesleyans. To begin with Appleton and Wilkinson were in good form, and each 3f them took five wickets for eight runs. These 16 runs with three extras were .the snm total of the Christian Endeavour's effort's. E. Wilkinson then showed his appreciation of the Endeavour's bowling by knocking up a capital 22, and the Weslevans scored altogether 77. Other good scorers besides Wilkinson were Hooper 17, Dunabin 11, and W, T, Wilkinson 10_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>• THE SANITARY AtTIIORITY AND THE PAROCHIAL COM- MITTEE.-At the fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authnrityheld on Tuesday afternoon at Dutton Workhouse, Mr. John Mclntyre presiding, the minutes of the last meeting (August 31st) of the. Appl- eton Parochial Committee were submitted for confirma- tion. These showed that Mr. Newton had acceded to the offer of £l2O per annum for emptying ashpits in the districts of Stockton Heath and Wilderspool. The Clerk reported having received the draft of an Order which the County Council proposed issuing dealing with the division of the township of Appleton into wards, such wards to be called by the reonective names, to comprise the respective boundries and to have distinct for each the number of Guardians .or district councillors and parish councillors as follows :-,-Stockton Heath ward: All that part of the township of Appleton with Hull north of the centre lino of toe Bridgewater Canal, with one Guardian, and seven parish Councillors. Appleton ward All that part of the township of Appleton with Hull south nf the centre line of the Bridgewater Canal, and not included in the Stockton Heath ward; with one Guardian or district councillor ; and seven parish min- cillors.—On the motion of Mr. E. J. Hall, seconded by Mr. Davenport, it was resolved "That in tire opinion of this Committee inasmuch as the growina tieeds of the union demand constant sanitary inspection, it is desir- able that the Rural Sanitary Authority appoint a proper- ly qualified surveyor for the purpose of examining,and reporting on plans for the laying out of the streets,lbs erection of buildings, and drainage works required, and In course of construction in the I) nion."—Mr. Farrineton (nuisance inspector) reported that notices had been served upon Messrs.G. and E.Wallingtnn, of Warrington, to extend the sewer to their property situated in Gas- kell-street, Stockton Heath. These notices had been complied with and the cesspools to such property had been abolished. The inspector submitted plans of an outbuilding 'erected and of five other similar buildings proposed to be erected by Mr. Thomas Cowdell - on land situated behind the" Mulberry Tree " Inn, at Stockton Heath. The Committee approved the plans.—Mr. Bran- dreth, referring to the minute with regard to the pro- posed appointment of a surveyor, asked if the township would pay the salary of such an officer.—The Clerk re- plied that it would be paid by the Union. The appoint- Ment could not, he thought, be made, however, before the consent of the Local Government Board had been obtained.—After some discussion the minute's were confirmed with the exception of one having reference to the surveyor: which question, it was 'considered, bad better be left in abeyance until the Pariah Councils came into existence when probably it could be finally dealt with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
