      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 8, 1894.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY SEPTEMBER 8, 1894.</p>
        <p>running them hard in the iron trade, but from the amount of application given to the land and to agecul- tural products it was the same in that respect. What they had to do, therefore, was to put themselves in form like thorough John Bull, and win hack that prosperity and that success of which they all stood so much in need. And he believed that their preside.nt,whose health he now submitted, could help them in that direction.</p>
        <p>(Cheers.) The toast wa3 most heartily received, cheers being given. and " For he'3 a jolly good fellow" sung. Sir Gilbert Greonall reznarked that he had already said he did not intend to make a speech. He thanked them for the ir.ind manner in which they had received the toast proposed by Mr. Monks.—Mr. they Davies proposed the health of the Judges,observing that he believed they had on this occasion the very best judges in England (Hear,</p>
        <p>• hear). The Rev. Cecil Legard, in. reply, said he could assure</p>
        <p>_ them that it gave him and his colleagues .Treat pleasure to see a show of that, kind with such an excellent lot of</p>
        <p>young animals. Nothing couli be better than the show of foals and the lighter horses. They had all to thank Sir Gilbert Greenall, for the fostering help he had given to the show. It must be very gratifying to him to see his efforts so thoroughly appreciated by such a gather- ing as that. It must also be a pleasure to him to see so many of his hunting friends around him. (applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MARE AND FOAL SHOW AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MARE AND FOAL SHOW AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Beautifully fine weather favoured the firth annual show of the Warrington and district Mare and Foal Society, held at Wilderpool, on Saturday. The number of entries was of a most encouraging char- acter, there being an increase of no fewer than 100 an the previous year. The attendance throughout the day was large, and the show may be said to have been the most successful the society has held.</p>
        <p>The officials were as follows :—President, Sir Gilbert Sireenall. Bart. ; vice president, Mr. Monks ; committee Messrs. Richard Ainscough, William Bradford, H. Broad- bent, A. Broome, T. Ball, T. Charnock, William Darbi- shire, J. E. Davies, William Fairhurst, Cyril Greenall, H. Greenough, J. Halsall, Walter Jones, William Long, John Marsh, C. Marson, Henry Mawdsley, John Mainwaring, Henry Milling, W. Richardson, S. Reynolds, Major Ridg- way, Mr. Joseph Shakeshaft, Major W. 11. Walker, Messrs. Jamas Warburton, William Whitlow, J. White ; secretary: Mr. Colin Maclver ; treasurer : Mr. Joseph Knowles; assistant secretary.: Mr. H. 11. Melville. Judges —Shire horses: Mr. Edward Green, The Moors, Welsh- , pool -; Mr. W. H. Potter, .Ullesthorpe, Lutterworth. Light horses and turnouts: The Rev. Cecil Legard, Cot- tesbrooke Rectory, Northampton:; Mr. Alfred Ashworth, Tables. Consulting veterinary surgeons .: Mr. Joseph Welstiy, F.R.C.V.S"., West Derby; Mr. Hugh Fer- guson, F.R.C.V.S., Warrington. Stewards Shire classes : Messrs. John Weir,John Whitlow, James Gould, Joseph Shakeshaft, James Warburton. Light classes and turnouts: Messrs. Cyril Greenall, Sylvanus Rey- nolds, Henry Whitlow, James Tinsley, jun., W. D. Bul- locks William A. Dewhurst • THE EXHIBIT&amp; The committee had happily selected judges of well- known experience, and these gentlemen, after their hard work was over, expressed the great pleasure they had had in assisting at such a good all-round show. The veterinary surgeons, reported that their services were very little required by the judges, and this must be con- sidered a satisfactory feature. The stewards had a hard time of it getting the numerous classes in and out of the rings, but good humour prevailed. The Shire judges, started with the special prizes offered by the Warring- ton Shire Horse Society, the competition for which is confined to members of that society or others who have used their Shire horse Carbon. The foals by this horse were a fairly good lot, and the Society certainly owns a very valuable sire. In colt foals, Mr. Joseph Shakeshaft, of Lymm, appeared to be an easy winneisalthough the one bred and exhibited by Mr. J. Weir, of Walton, would have run him hard but for being lame. The filly foals gave the judges a little more trouble, but a very good foal of Mr. Worthington's, of Rixton, taptc first place with three or four good fol- lowers. Mr. Joseph Shakeshaft scored again with his mare " Ercall Diamond," with foal by Carbon, Mr. John Smith's mare coming a good second. For ,mares under Le hands the members of the ShiresElorse Society would be delighted at seeing the Ist and 2nd prizes carried off by two four year old mares by Lancashire Lad U, the horse the Society hired froin Lord Ellesmere for three seasons; the one placed first, bred and exhibited by Mr. John Weir, was a very useful, nice little mare. ;Ihere were eleven entries in the class for foals by Mr. Char- nock's horses, and he no doubt would be gratified to see such a good show doing credit to his horses, three or four of them being capital foals: the one bred and ex- hibited ley Mr. J. Gerrard, of Chester, a wonderfully good foal, was at once picked out by the judges to top the class. The open district classes of course proved the most interesting, and the Ist class for mares with foal at foot by any registered Shiro horse,had some well-known show mares in. Mr. S. B. Chadwick's Royal Lancashire Winner" Derby" was beaten by a lovely mare "Jenny" exhibited by Mr. Wm. Blackburne, of Rostiserne, Mr. Charnock'a "0 ik Apple" coming third. In the next class mares without foal, Mr: S. B. Chadwick gained first with the well-known "Moulton Lassie" and Mr. Edward Rowes, of Crowley, came a close second with a very useful looking brown mare, and the third the big powerful looking mare of Mr. F. Worthington's Rixton Princess. The class for district foals by any registered shire horse, had as many as twenty-two entries, and took some time in selecting for the honours. Mr. S. B. Chadssick'a Lancashire Lad foal which had been shown at so many shows and always gained first prize, was in this class. He looks a grand young colt and many people were surprised when he only got placed second to Mr. Blackburne's filly foal by Lord Garston's "Royal William," but the latter named feat was in the opinion of the judges the better of the two. The yearling fillies were topped by Mere Jewell 11. by R.R., bred and ex- hibited by Mr. Thomas Hardy. of Mere, and she is a beautiful little filly. The two year old filly class pro- duced seven really good animals, and outside opinion was again upset. Mr. Chadwick's " Crofton Daisy" and Mr. Frank Worthington's "Rixton Rosebud "being at the bottotn end and only one of the two Lancashire Lad fillies shown by Mr. J. E. Davies getting a card— the third place—being beaten by Messrs. Ainscough's black " Vulcan "filly, and Mr. James Mercer's brown filly by "Jupiter," Mr. john Marsh's" Littler Queen" getting fourth place. As a class this last named was perhaps the best lot of shires shown. Mr. Reingill, of Hale. Altrincham, was first in three year o'd fillies with " Wes'ern Queen "heating Mr. Hardy's " Esperance " the two being very fine fillies. The competition for the champion prizes resulted as most of the on -lookers ex- pected. Mr. B'arkhurn's mare " Jenny" was awarded the spscial prize for the best shire mire ; Mr. Chadwick's " Moulton Lassie " reserved. Mr. Hardy's yearling filly gained special prize for the best shire filly. Mr. Gerrard's coal foal by " Calwick Combilation " was declared the champion foal, and then the judge; had to chaose the best shire exhibit in the show, and they unhesitatingly award(' I Sir Gilbert Clreenall'a Challenge Cup to Mr. Blackburne's " Jenny," thus making her the champien of this year's show, and the reserve card was given to Mr. Har,3y's yearly filly. The exhibits in lighter horses were of a very high class 'character, in fact, one of the judges in responding to a toast at the luncheon held near the Saracens's Head Hotel, said that nothing could be better than the yning animals displayed. The hunter classes, perticula.rly,the two-year olds, were very fine. Great interest was taken by the spectators in ths traeos7len'l3 tusnouts, and the judges had some difficulty in awaiding the !Drive. Two of the exhibits. Mr. Thomas Hatton's grey mare, Polls-, and Mr. J. M. Porter's turnout were so nearly equal in merit that the judges were unable to say which should he first. Even after Mr. Wynne-Jones had given them a Apia round the ring, the judges appeared no nearer coming to a decision. Eventually it was decided to equally divide the first and second prizes between the two compstitors. The Walton Brass Band was in:attendance during the day, and played selections of music. THE LUNCHEON. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., the president of the seciety entertained a large number of friends and supporters to luncheon in the Aesembly Room vcently erected by Mr. T. Speakman, of the Saracen's Head. Mr. Speakman catered, and all were highly pleased with the liberal manner in which they were entertained. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart.. presidsd, and amongst those present were Mr. F. Monks, Mr. E lward Green. Mr. W. H. Potter, Rev. Cecil Leger&amp; Mr Alfred Ashworth. the Ron. John Cross. Captain Park Yates. Mr. Walter :trines, Mr. Cyril Greenall. Mr. HornhY Lewis, Mr. John White, Mr. S. Reynolds. Mr. H. Brnadbent, Mr. Wm. Lone, Mr. Harvey Talbot. Captain Birkett. Mr. Wm. Birkett, Mr. Leigh Townsend. Mr. Alfred Ashton, Mr. John St. V. Fox, Mr. Grimke-Draytnn, Mr. Oliver Ashwerth, Mr. G. Littleton Dewhersa, Mr. W. A. Dewhurst, Mr. T. P. Norris, Mr. James Tinsley. jun., Mr. John Down. Mr. R. J. Wynne-Jones, Mr. Colin MacPser. Mr H. B. Melville, Mr. Henry Mihlinr, Mr. W. D. Bullock, Mr. I.E. Seeker, Mr. Joseph Knowles, Mr. J. E. Daviee, Mr. Wm. Fair. .hurst. Major Ilideway, Mr. Richard Ainscnugh, Mr. T. S. Burgess, Mr. Jame. Warhur on. Mr. A. Brooms, Mr. John Weir. Mr. Henry Whitlow, Mr. Wm. Whitlow, Mr. John Hals.all, Mr. John Marsh, Mr. Henry Mawdsley. kr. Sir GILBERT GREET:ALT., Bart., on rising to propose the first toast met with a nine,: enthusiastie. welcome, He remarked that they would bo glad to hear they were not to have any speeches. There was, however, one toast that they ennta not very well ninit, and that was the , health Of her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen. (Aso I plaice.) Mr. F. MONKS, the vier-president,Pproposed the health , of tha President, which he remarked was a toast that would commend itself to their hearty acceptance.. (An- Platten) Circumetencest to which they need net refer very much had to some extent altered the programme of the society, and they would no loiiht make all sorts of allowanees ; hut in the changee which it had neceseita,ted he entild only say on behalf of Kr Gilbert's neighbours, his tenants, and his friends that they wished him a long and honourable life and success in anything he took in hind. (Anplatise.) They knew that he exhibited the beitt he had' got, and they all shared with him the Wise faction and the pleasure which it no doubt .WAS to him to win prizes: het he (Mr. Monks) would on behalf of those present. go one step further. Sir Gilbert had got such a enlendid pedigree stock that if they could only induce him tri let them have the use of it on satisfactory terms he wreild re-infer a tonnn not only upon his tenantry, neighbours arid friendssbut it wrield give him an untold riteaenre, in nit-lire years. (Apnlatiae.) He knew that there were difficulties in the way, hut at the proper time he hoped to have an onnneenity of making a sug- gestion teshew how those diffiesrltiee could to be over- ennui. He did hope that the time was not far distant when they would all have the benefit of the hest Sir Gil- bert had got, and at prices which would at any rate do something to make farming remenerative. (flear.hear.) Thry heari a great deal of talk about the various in- &amp;strips, hut if they enlv hooked at the figures of what • the avrieultural industry was they would find that the iron. -coal, en'ttnn . and woollen Widest, and almost every other hrarch of industry, did not total en to anything like that of the agricultural industry. He Was afraid thirthent were ton apt to overlook that hese industry ; it was by far the largest industry of the country, and it was also an ineltietry mein whlch every other to a large extent depended. He had only returned a few days sinwe f•om a trip amongst their comoetitnre. move particularly in the iron trade. Hitherto they had an idea that Englishmen were the hest men on the field, but he ventured to say that other peolile Wert not only</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>SHIRR MARES AND FOALS.-For mares with foal at foot, by any registered shire horse. Prize given by th Vice-President : 1, William Blackburne, Rostherne ; 2, S. B. Chadwick, Crofton Lodge, Runcorn ; Thomas Charnock, Hale, Liverpool ; 11 c, John Smith, Sudlow Farm, Knutstord ; c, Edward Gibbon, Lymm. Mares without foal, stinted to a registered shire horse 1, S. B. Chadwick, Crofton Lodge, Runcorn ; 2, Edward Ravres, Crowley Ball, Northwich ; 3, Frank Worthiageon, Rix- ton ; h c, John Smith, Sudlow Farm, Knutsford. Foals : 1, William Blackburne, Rostherne, Cheshire ; 2, Frank Worthington, Rixton Hall; 3, S. B. Chad-wick, Crofton Lodge, Runcorn ; h c, Edward Gibbon, Lymm, and Joseph Shakeshaft, Lymna. Yearling fillies: I. Thomas Hardy, Mere Hall Farm, Knots- ford ; 2, S. B. Chadwick, Crofton Lodge, Runcorn ;3, John Longshaw, Grappenhall ; Ii c. Thomas Pereival, Moore. Two-year old fillies: 1, H. and R. Ainscough, Risley.; 2, James Mercer, Hale Bank, Wid- nes; 3, J. E. Davies, The 'Old Warps, Warrington ; hc, John Marsh, Grappenhall, Warrington. Three-year-old fillies : 1, Sun S. Raingill,Hale, Altrincham ; 2, Thomas Hardy, of Mere Hall Farm, Knutsford ; 3, Joseph Whit- low, Preston Brook • he, Joseph Shakeshaft, Lymm, Special prizes offered by the Warrington Shiro Horse Society (not limited to the Show Society's District): Mares over 16 hands with foal at foot by Car- bon or having produced a fuel this year by Carbon : 1, Joseph Shakeshaft, Lymm, Warringtm ; 2, John Smith, :sudlow Farm, Knutsford; 3. Edward Gibbon Lymm. Mares 16 hands and under with foal at foot by Carbon or b•aving produced a foal this year by Carbon: 1. John Weir, Lower Walton, Warrington ; 2, Joseph Whitlow. Preston Brook; 3, Joseph Knowles, Appleton, Cheshire, Colt foals by Carbon: 1, Joseph Shakeshaft, Lymm Warrington; .2, John Whitlow, Broomfields, Appleton; Cheshire ; 3, John Longshaw, Grappenhall. reserve, Alfred Broome, Keckwick. Filly foals by Carbon: 1 Frank Worthington, Rixton Hall, Warrington ; 2, Smith Brothers, Bold, Widnes; 3, Joseph Knowles: Appleton, Cheshire ; h c, John R. Whalley, Mill Green, Bold, Widnes; c, Edward Gibbon, Lymm. Yearling fillies by the society's 1892 horses, Franklin and Vandal: 1, John Longshaw, Grappenhall, Warrington ; 2, Frank Worth- ington, Rixton Hall, Warrington ; 3. Joseph Knowles, Appleton, Cheshire; h c, Alfred Ashworth, Tabley Grange, Knutsford. Special prizes offered by Mr. T. Charnock, Hale (not limited to Show Society's District: -Foals by any of his registered shire horses: 1, John Gerrard, Stoke, Chester; 2,-Evans, Belgrave, Chester; c, Isaac Dilworth,. fialebank, Liverpool ; and Joseph Beecroft, Duckington Grange, Malpas. DRAUGHT HORSES. -First prize and teamsman's pre- mium given by John. White, Maclver and Co. Pair of draught horses to be shown in gears, horse only to be judged 1, S. B. Chadwick, Crofton Lodge, Runcorn ; 2, Edward Rawes, Crowley Hall, Northwich ; 3, Joseph Shakeshaft, Lymm, Warrington ; h c, Thomas Percival, Moore, Warrington. Single draught horse in cart or hurry, the whole turnout to be judged : 1, The Warring- ton Slate Company; 2. Thomas Percival, Moore, War- rington ; h c, Thomas Halton, The George Hotel, War- rington. HUNTER CLASSES. -Prizes given by Mr. Sylvanus Reynolds, Haddon Court, Warrington.-Mares with foal at foot,or stinted to a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire : 1, W. Long, Thelwall Heyes ; 2, George Priestner, Whitley Superior, Northwich ; 3, Walter Jones, The Elms, Moore; Ii c, Colin Maclver, Statham. Prizes given by Mr. Walter Jones, The Elms, Warrington.- Mares with foal at foot, or stinted to a thoroughbred. or registered hunter sire (for tenant farmers only) : 1, John Ravenscroft, Moss Farm, Holford, Knutsford ; 2, Sam S. Raingill, Hale, Altrincham ; 3, John Newton, Denfield Hall; r. Mary Whitlow, High Legh, Knutsford. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., gives a premium (£3) in this class for the best exhibit, being the properly of a tenant farmer.-Foals, by a thoroughbred or regis- tered hunter sire: 1, Thomas Hewitt, Lower Whitley, Northwich; 2, George Priestner, Whitley Superior,North- wich ; 3, John E. Newton, Denfield Hall, Knutsford ; r, Hannah Holliday, Dutton, Warrington ; h c, Wm. Lone, Thelwall Reyes. Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., gives a pre- mium (£3) in this class for the best exhibit, the property of a tenant farmer-Yearlings by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire: 1, George Priestner, Whitley Superior, Northwich ; 2. James Brookes. Thelwall ; r , Lt.-Colonel Cornwall Legh, High Legh, Knutsford ; h c and c, Mary Whitlow, High Legh, Knutsford. Si r Gilbert Greenall, Bort., gives a premium (£3) in thi s class for the best exhibit, being the property of a tenant fartnor-Two-year-olds, by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire: 1, Thomas Wright, High Legh; 2, James Tinsley, Daresbury ;'r, Wm. Long, Thelwall Hayes, War- rington ,• h c, Colin Maclver, Statham, Warrington, and Walter Jones, Moore, Warrington. Sir Gilbeit Greenall Bart., gives a premium (£3) in this class for the best exiaibit, being the property of a tenant farmer-Three-year-olds (to be shown in hand), by a thoroughbred or registered hunter sire; 1, Thomas Hewitt, Lower Whitley, Northwich; 2, William Long, Thelwall Reyes, Warrington; r, Walter Jones, Moore, Warrington; h c. C. Ravenscroft, Plumbley, Knutsford. .ROADSTRRS. -Foals, likely to make a roadster ; 1, Samuel S. Raingill, Hale, Altrincham ; 2. Joseph Hall, Birchdale Farm, Frodsham ; hc, John Clare Ridgway, Grappenhall Lodge, Warrington ; Thomas Percival, Moore. Warrington ; c, Peter Leather, Stretton, War- rington.. Special prizes given by Mr. Alfred Goodman, Cheadle, for foals by his horse, Prince of Denmark (registered hackney)-not confined to the Show Society's District; 1, Joseph Hall, Birchdale Farm, Frodsham ; 2, John Clare Ridgway, Grappenhall Lodge, Warrington ; r Thomas Darlington, Daresbury, Warrington ; h c, ' lames Ashley, Latchford. TURNOUTS. -Mare or gelding, driven in harness. 15 hands and over; 1, Richard Clarke, Warburton, War- rington ; 2, Thomas Hatton, Seven Stars. Warrington ; 3, T. J. Down, Lower Walton; he, A. Hernby Lewis, MarbUry Hall, Northwich. Mare or gelding, driven in harness, above 14 hands, not exceeding 15 hands ; 1, George Rodger, Newton Bank, Preston Brook; 2. Harry Reynolds, Sandiway Northwich; Ii c, John Blackshaw, Hatton, Warrington. Mare or gelding, driven in harness, not exceeding 14 hands; /, Thomas Percival, Moss Side, Moore ; 2, John Clare Bldg-. way, Grappenhall Lodge, Warrington; h c. Joseph Knowles, Appleton Thorn, and H. Ferguson,Warring,ton. Best and neatest tradesman's turnout.horse,harness, and conveyance, used for delivering goods. Prize of whip given by Mr. Isaac Wilkinson, saddler. Warrington, to the driver of first prize : 1 and 2 divided,Thomas Hatton, Seven Stars,Warrington, and J.M. Porter, Horsemarket- st.. Warrington ; 3, Robert Taylor, Farnworth, Widnes ; hc, Frederick R. Pierpoint, Bridge-st., Warrington ; c, Margaret Massey, Acton Grange. SPECIAL PRIZES. -Prize, value £s,for the best shire mare in Classes 1,2, 7. B.given by Mr. Fred .Stapleton-Brether- ton, The Hall. Rainhill : Wm. Blackhurne, Rostherne (Jenny). Special prize, value £5, for the best filly in Classes 3,4, 5,6, 10 11, 12, given by Sir Gilbert Green- all, Bart., Walton Flail: T. Hardy, Mere Hall Farm, Knutsford (Mere Jewel). Challenge Cup, value £2o,for the best shire exhibit in Classes 1 to 12 inclusive, pre- sented by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., to the society : Wm. Blackburne. Rostherne (Jenny). Cup, value .£5, given by Sir G. Greenall, Bad., for mare with foal at foot, or stinted to a thoroughbred or registered hun- ter sire : open to tenant farmers only, residing within seven miles of Warrington, over whose holding the Cheshire Mounds hunt: Mary Whitlow, nigh Legh, Knutsford (Dido).</p>
