      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>LYlnki</head>
        <p>LYlnki</p>
        <p>Tn TEMNICAL CLASSRS.--It has not yet been decided whetticr the Technical Classes will be tontinutel during the coming winter. The attendance and results of the last session were not all that could be desired, and it is regarded as probable that the classes will be closed. The sick-nursing lectures which were held in connection 'with the institute seem to have been very inaccesltil in the past, and it is hoped the committee will see their way to continue them. Tits SMOOT. Boann.—On Tuesday evening a meeting of the above authority was held in the Roard-room.when there were present—Mr.Greeson (chairman),and Messrs. J. flolt, W. Bratendale, R. Whitelegge, A. Mclntyre, J. Perrin, E. bolt. Ridgway (law clerk), Mullard Mort (surveyor), and Severs (gas manager).—Mr. Mort submitted hit report, which contained no item of !public interest. —Mr. Severs reported that the production 'Of gas during the-past month had been 821,000 cubic fe, compared with 777.00.0 cubic feet far the Fame trlnnal of the preceding year, being an increase of 44,1900 Ctibic feet —An invitation from the Lantashire and Cheshire Union to attend their annual meeting was acknowledged. —There was no other business of public interest.</p>
        <p>(eet,</p>
        <p>The most economical Watch is the new quick-wind, stem- set Waterbury; seldom requires reNtirs, and then at little cost. Made in all steel and styles, from geld to nickel. ' From 17/6 to 130/- Illustrated catalogue post free on application to Waterbury. 7, Snow Hill, London. Sold by all Watchmakers. —Adet. (5)</p>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERN 3IENT IN CMIRY AT F RODS HAM. --- APPLICATION FOR BORROWING POWERS: THE WATER SUPPLY. Mr. Thomas Codrington, M. mat, C.E., conducted a Local Government Board inquiry on Friday at the Town Hall, Frodsham, into an application by the Frodsham, and Frodsham Lordship Parochial Com- mittees for sanction to borrow £6OO for works of water supply ; and also for the determination of the reasonable cost at which the supply may be required to be furnished to houses in the townships of Prod- sham and Frodsham Lordship. There were present: Mr. Ashton (clerk to the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Autho- rity), Mr. George Ashton (deputy clerk) Mr. S. Davies (clerk of the Water Works), Mr. J. Farrington (nuisance inspector), Messrs. A. Thomas, J.P., J. M. Williams, H. E. Heywood, T. Lewis (Bradley), Thomas Riley, Henry Tiley, W. Shore, James Nicholls, Thomas Earlam, James Hillidge, C. D. Parkinson, James Edwards, John Ockle- ston, H. G. Linaker, and W. Youd. Mr. Ashton stated that the Parochial Committee of the I Frodsham townships had found that they had not suffi- cient money to carry out the works in order to adequ- atelv supply the district with water. They had received sinction for £3,800, of which they had euended £3,772 2s. 101 They now found it was necessary to extend the water mains to various streets, to provide fences at the reservoir and pumping station, stores, lee., and add- inc for contingencies the expenditure came to a total of £602, thus making the costs of the works £4,375. Since the estimate had been made out a petition had been re- ceived from certain residents in Frodsham Lordship, and presented to the Parochial Committee at their meeting I on the previous night, desiring to be supplied with water rfor at present they had no spring water, and in dry weather were compelled td carry fr3m a considerable I distance. The memorial was sigaed by five inhabitants I whose houses were rated at £l5O 4s. The committee con- sidered the matter, and passed a; resolution to the effect that subject to the sanction of the Local Government Board, and the parties signing the petition consenting to pay a rate equal to 12/ per cent. on the rateable value of the premises, that the necessary works be carried out and that it be requested that the sum now to be bor- rowed should be altered from £6OO to £7OO. Although the petition was signed by only five persons there was a rateable value of £368 on the line of pipes proposed. Mr. Edwards, who opposed the borrowing of the extra £lOO for Frodsham Lordship, contended that one township had .no power to trade in any commodity with another township. There would sure to be a deficiency account on the revenue derived from the water in Prod- sham township, which the ratepayers would have to make good. He asked whether at the time tlaey got per- mission from the Frodsham Gas and Waterworks Com- pany to supply the township they also obtained sanction for the Lordship. Mr. Ashton said the matter had been settled by arbitra- tion. It simply had reference to the township. The Gas Company did not intend to exercise their powers and he thought that a letter giving the necessary per- mission would be procured very easily. The Inspector thought this should be done and,in re- ferring to the objection of one township trading with another, he said that such would not be the case, the two townships being supplied by the Runcorn Rural Sanitary Authority. No opposition was made to the proposal to:borrow the extra £7OO. Mr. Ashton, in referring to the charges, said the Com- mittee wished to make the scheme self-supporting as far as possible. They did not wish to make it profitable to them, neither did they desire to have any deficiency which would be included among the special expenses for that part of the Union. After careful consideration they had ascertained that of the 506 houses in the township, 229 were tinder £7 rateable value, and on these it was proposed that the charge should be 2d. per week, which was thought to be fair. The rateable value of the other houses, in the aggregate, amounted to £4,771, and the charge to be made upon them was 71 per cent., without any graduated scale, for the reason that they would have no extra charge for baths or water closets, and the increased percentage would make up for these con- veniences. The income from the houses under £7 was estimated at £99 4s. 81. From those above that rateable value, £357 163.61 ; and from outbuildings,£l7 185.6 d.; or a total income of £474 19s. Bd. The expenditure for providing the water for the houses would be £9B 8,1.61; oil, stores, and repair., £5O; wages of pipe fitter, £7B : repayment of loan, £l5B ; which with interest for the first year would make the total expenditure £487 1.85.6 d. or £lO more than the income. The additional loan from Frodsham Lordship would be about £lB, and the esti- mated income £2O. The Inspector intimated that the Authority already had power to charge the 2d. per week on houses rmA., exceeding a rateable value of £7 per annum. Forsimilar houses in Frodsham Lordship the charge would be 31. per week. He asked the Clerk to make a scale for the other houses in accordance with the proposed charge of 7/ per cent, for premises in Frodshatia township, and 121 per cent. for those in Frodsham Lordship. The scale for works, gardens, &amp;c.,would be a matter of agreement between the Authority and the consumers. Mr. Ashton asked if it would be possible to get the Local Government Board to dgfer the first payment of principal for five years. He drew attention to the sec- tion of the Public Health Act empowering the Treasury to postpone for periods not exceeding five years the payment of the instalments of principal and interest due on leans from the Public Works Loan Commissioners ; but no such power existed in respect of loans from other bodies or private individuals. The Inspector thought that his Board had already replied to the Authority to the effect that they had no such power. He considered the question to be a matter for the Public Works Loan Commissioners. The Inspec- tor at the conclusion of the inquiry visited the water works.</p>
      </div>
