      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WIPEMRAWAL OF THE GENERAL STEAM NAVIGATION COMPANY.</head>
        <p>THE WIPEMRAWAL OF THE GENERAL STEAM NAVIGATION COMPANY.</p>
        <p>"MANCHESTER PEOPLE ASLEEP."</p>
        <p>The "Manchester Evening News" says :—Canal., shareholders and others interested in the great waterway have heard with regret that the General Steam Naviga- tion Company intend to abandon their service between Manchester and the West Coast of Africa, and to load , only at Liverpool. This will come as a surprise to most people, especially after a somewhat favourable reference to this particular.trade by Sir Stuart Elegg at the half- yearly meeting of the company held .recently, but it ia nevertheless a fact. One of our representatives called on Mr. C. H. Glyn, the secretary of the company, to make inquiries on the subject. In reply to questions Mr. Glyn said it was perfectly true that his company had decided to abandon the canal and load only at Liverpool. Asked as to the reason for this decision, Mr. Glyn, with some warmth, said "It's no use our going to Manchester. Manchester people will not give us any support. They have All gone to sleep, and are simply playing the fool with us. There is not the slightest inducement for us to go to Manchester, and it is simply a waste of money our continuing to do so. Unless some inducement be forth- coming to persuade us to alter our decision, the trade from Manchester will soon be at an end." Mr. Glyn added that this enly referred to the trade with the West Coast of Africa, and not to any of their other services. Our reporter next called on Mr. William B. Mears. the Landon representative of the Manchester Ship Canal Companf, at the London Shipping Exchange, who, on being told of the determination of the General Steam Navigation Company, expressed some surprise, and ?teemed inclined to doubt the accuracy of the statement. He said it seemfd very strange that the company should have decided upon this step after the remarks of Sir Stuart Hogg, and especially after the success which had attended the voyages of some of their vessels, notably that of the Amara, though this was only chartered by the company. Mr. Mears went on to say that it was all humbug to talk about the olistacles and other defects of the canal. It was sheer prejudice, principally on the part of Liverpool and her representatives in London. "'The canal," he said, with emphasis, " must assert itself sooner or later, and within a reasonable time there would certainly be a consilerable increase in the traffic." "You will see," he added, "some of these days such a rush of traffic as will surprise you. The feeling here amongst shipowners, captains, and others interested in the shipping industry is that the canal will, in the course of time, overcome all opposition, and Manchester must hold its own against all rival ports," Reverting to the decision of the General Seam Company, Mr. Mears said the company would have to take their vessels to what- ever port they were chartered for, and if the chaL wished his goods to be taken by the canal, the company would have to take them by that route. Mr. Mears also informed our renortor that he had had a conversation with some of the representatives of the largest South Australian nom trading firms, all of whom were fully cognisant TIT the facilities of the canal for the discharging of grain cargoes, and of the speedy and cheap means of distributing the grain to the consumers. Mr. Mears is at present working up this trade, and hopes to induce sortie of the Australian corn merchants to send their cargoes 'tip the canal to Manchester. "But.," he added, the success of the canal depends largely—almost solely --uivn the Manchester merchants themselves; and if they won't support it and find cargoes for vessels they can hardly expect outsiders to do so." Since the above was put in type it has been officially denied that the Steam Navigation Company intend to diacentinue sending steamers to Manchester.</p>
        <p>I•terer</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>BOLD FORGERY,</head>
        <p>A BOLD FORGERY,</p>
        <p>A etranger entered the bank of Messrs. Glyn,Mills,ami Co., Lombard-street, London, on Tuesday, presented a draft on the'Credit 'Lyonnais for £4.800, and was paid. He afterwards wedt to the Bank of England sind gat the notes he received changed for notes of smaller amount. It is said that the arait was a forged one, cleverly molelled on a drift for £4B of which the London arentO of the CreditilLyormais had been advised by their Paris 1-scaiss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>W RRINGTON BEERSELLER'S LICENSE ENDORSED. A MIDNIGELT CAROUSAL.</head>
        <p>A W RRINGTON BEERSELLER'S LICENSE ENDORSED. A MIDNIGELT CAROUSAL.</p>
        <p>John Armstrong, holding an off-licence at 1, Hoyle-street, Warrington, was charged at the Borough Court on Monday morning, with having on the 27th August, sold intoxicating liquor during prohibited hours. Samuel and Richard Armstrong, brothers, were charged with aiding and abetting. Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, represented the defendants. Mr. Hunt (chief constable) in his statement to the magistrates deposed that Constables Dean and Meacock were on duty in plain clothes at the end of Bewsey-road at 1.30 the previous Monday morning, for a special pur- pose. Whilst standing there they heard a number of men singing. They listened for some time, and went in the direction of the sound. They found that the sing- ing was going on in a hut in a garden on some waste land abutting the Cheshire Lines Railway near Folly- lane. Henry Whitehouse, the former licensee, erected the hut, but it was not in the possession of the present holder of the licence. The officers made their way to the back of the hut and heard everything that was said. About two o'clock a.m. they saw four men leave the hut! The two brothers, Samuel and Richard, carrying a large large bottle proceeJed to the back door of the licensed premises of John Armstrong. The other two men went In another directiou. After waiting a short time, the officers saw the two brothers return with the bottle. They stopped them and took from one of them the bottle containing beer, produced. P.C. Dean stated that about 1.30 a.m. on Monday morning, the 27th August, lie was in Bewsey-road near to Lodge-lane end in company with P.C. Meacock. They heard singing in the gardens adjoinin. the Cheshire Lines Railway. They proceeded through' the garden which recently belonged to Mr. Fairhurst and got to s hut where several songs weie sung. They got behind the hut and heard everything that was said. Such songs were sung as" Strolling round the town," and " If you want to know the time ask a policeman." One person shouted " Call on the -- butler and let him ' tot that ale round." Another one said" You will get no-- ale here until Mr. Williams has sung." After he had sung, another one said "Now 'tot' that ale round." Some- one then said "There is no beer." Witness then heard somebody else rejoin "I will give my 6d. towards the cost of a gallon of beer." Money was then collected and they immediately afterwards left the hut and proceeded to Hoyle-street. Witness and P.C. Meacock followed and saw two of the men enter Mr. Armstrong's premises. About five minutes to two o'clock the two men came out again by the back door one of them carrying a gal- lon bottle. Witness accosted the man with the bottle whose name was Samuel Armstrong brother to the defendant beerseller, and said, " I will thank you for that beer," adding that he was surprisedto see him with it at that hour. The other brother, Richard, was in his company. The man with the bottle stated that Mr. Whitehouse had got married, and they were having a. little jollification. They again visited the hut, and wit- ness counted eleven men inside. The table was still wet with beer. Witness afterwards accompanied Inspec- tor Cox to Mr. John Armstrong's house. He was in bed at the time, and said he did not know anything about the matter. His brother Must have supplied it while he was in bed. He hoped they would make it as light as they could. P.C. Meacock corroborated. Inspector Cox gave evidence as to receiving the bottle produced centaining a gallon of beer. He went with one of the previous witnesses to the house of the licensee. The defendant told them that so far as he was concerned he knew nothing about it. He promised Whitehouse a gallon of beer when he was married a fort- night ago. In cross-examination witness said he believed Mr. Hunt did warn the defendant about per- mitting drinking in the hut. Mr. Browne, solicitor for the defence, said he was not going to waste time. He had nine witnesses but he was not going to call them. The hut in question was on private land kept lot, Mr. Frank Whitehouse who worked at Bewsey Forge. When the preceding licensee occupied the house the practice of meeting in the hut was carried on to a very large extent. When Mr. Armstrong became the licensee he was warned about the practice, and he promised Mr. Hunt that it should not be carried on. Mr. Browne contended that the defendant had carried out his promise. ,He had never sold one single pint of beer to be taken to this cabin, and had only been in it twice in his life. On the Saturday before Bank Holiday Mr. Whitehouse was married, and the defendant promised to give him a gallon of beer. Mr. Whitehouse did not want it at the time,, but on the night preceding the offence he asked Mr. Armstrong to give him the gallon of beer promised, and brought a bottle for it. It was arranged for the bottle to be filled and to be left in the next yard. This was done. At two o'clock the following morning the bottle was sent for, and they were bringing it away when the officers stopped them. If the brothers did enter the defendant's house to fill the bottle they did it contrary to the defend- ant's wish and knowledge. Mr. John Armstrong, the licensee, and Mr. Whitehouse gave evidence, corroborating Mr. Browne's statement. The latter witness denied that any person in the hut Offered to pay for any beer. The magistrates retired and on their return the Chair- man said that the Bench were satisfied that it was their duty t 4 protect publicans who were endeavouring to conduct their heuses properly. But the believed that this case had been fully proved and John Armstrong, the licensee, would be fined £5 and costs, and the other two brothers £1 each. They endorsed the licence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>TRAGIC SCENES FROM THE PHILOSOPHY OF REV. DR. SYNTAX.</head>
        <p>TRAGIC SCENES FROM THE PHILO- SOPHY OF REV. DR. SYNTAX.</p>
        <p>(ALL WIIITERS PDESEILVED). Van Deemon was a foreigner, Who on our shores once landed; Be sailed abaft a flying craft That he himself had planneded. His luggage waR a 'baccy-pouch, A pipe and two match-boxes— And these he lost when he. was tost And wrecked among the rockses. He settled in a country town, Where one small room he rented, Of which the key about lugged he, Where'er it was he wented. He wooed a blooming servant maid, Who long his suit resisted ; But sooth to say, she soon gave way When he her fat lips kisseded. And when she named the day, he bought A wedding ring brass-gilded; He'd won her when they were made *se— Their cup of joy was filieded. But time brought troubles, toils and cares, And nuptial charms soon faded ; Regrets each had now for the bad Selection they had maded. Sadly his work he goes about; She stays at home and boozes— His Sunday togs march "up the spout Kept by a pair of JeWSPEI. Once, in a rage, with water hot His precious wife he scalded, For which amour he went before The local" beak" was hauleded. Their worthy worships in their bench, With courage Dutch, he bearded—. . Told them, nor fine nor quod benign, His valiant bosom feareded. But when he heard," a month with hard--r' His heart within him freezes ; For skilly fare, and cold eells bare, • His mental eye soon seeses. When liberated from vile quod, His spouse he so upbraided, That violently the poker she Athwart his features laided. By turns they fought, by turns they bled, By turns they sulked and scolded; They scratched and swore and clawed, while o'er The chairs and stools they rolleded. And thus some forty years they lived, By love's sweet strife divided. Till, in the flow'r of youth, one hour They blinked their eyes, and dieded ! • ADAM NEWSENSE.</p>
        <p>Warrington, Aug. 1894. (ro be discontinued the coming week before last).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>MR. KEIR HARDIE AND HIS TOTE.</head>
        <p>MR. KEIR HARDIE AND HIS TOTE.</p>
        <p>Whenever the registration courts commence their operations, the outcry against the anomalies of the Registration Acts begins. This year (remarks the "Globe ") one of the first incidents is that Mr. Heir Hardie. M.P., has had his claim tos vote disallowed. It certainly seems strange that a man who is qualified to sit in Parliamedt should be deemed disqualified from voting for the election of members. That, hewever, is the law. for Mr. Heir Hardie is a lodger, and as he only pays 94. a week for his rooms this does not entitle him to a vote.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>SINGULAR INCIDENT AT ALDERSHOT.</head>
        <p>SINGULAR INCIDENT AT ALDERSHOT.</p>
        <p>The Duchess of Connaught visited Aldershot on Wed- nesday to christen anew military balloon. On the arrival of the Duchess a captive balloon, the Flo, was sent up with an empty cage. Almost immediately a Bash of.lightning struck the Flo, which exploded. At the same time three men of the Royal Engineers in charge of the winch that held the wire of the captive balloon fell to the gronnd.. They were found to have sustained a severe electrical shock, but hopes are entertained that all three will recover. The Duchess, who was much shocked by the accident, returned to Government Rouse without christening the new balloon.</p>
      </div>
