      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1894-09-08_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SALE OF WORK AT EARLESTOWN,</head>
        <p>SALE OF WORK AT EARLESTOWN,</p>
        <p>On Friday afternoon a sale of work was opened, by Mr. E. J. George, of Haydock, in. the Salvation Army barracks. The platform aud building was prettily and tastefully decorated with vegetables and fruit, and the stalls, presided over by Mrs. Cap- tain Wild, Mrs, Evans, Mrs. Howgate, Miss Nellie Roberts, and Mrs. and Miss Williams, were arranged in a very attractive manner. Miss M. E ,chus was in charge of the refreshment stall. In the evening the band of the St. Helens Corps, under the command of , Captain McKeegan, discharged the duties of conducting the open air meeting on the market ground. The meet- ing in the Barracks was led by Captain Mc,Keegan, after which the sale of work was resumed. On Sturdy afternoon at two o'clock, the sale .of work-was re-opened by Captain Wild, until 7 o'clock, when the local CorPs band summoned the call "to arms" on the market ground. Here Mr. J. E. Bradshaw, of Manchester, was the "shining star" of the evening. Marching away to the Barracks at 8 o'clock, an agreeable surprLe awaited the people, in the fact that Mr. Bradshaw delivered a lucid, amusing, and instructive lecture on Bunyan's Pil- grim's Progress. The lecturer on being introduced to the congregation, w,s received with the usual volleys of congratulation, ana tee rapt attention and frequent ap- plause of the congregation gave evident proof of the exemplary manner in which the lecturer discharged his• duties of-exhibiting the various characters portrayed in the progress of Christian to the Celestial City. The meetings all day on Sunday were under the Comtnand of Mr. Bradshaw, and the congregations at each meeting were larger and more attentive than has been the case for some time past. At the conclusion of the Sunday nighec meeting, the thanks of the corps and congregation were conveyed by Captain Wild to Mr. Bradshaw, incorporated. with a request for another early visit. In reply to this - Mr. Bradshaw consented to give a further lecture on Thursday evening on Bunvan's Pilgrim, and on Friday evening.on the" Work a.m..' Life of General Booth." Tila sale of work and fruit was further resumed on Monday' at two o'clock, and at six o'clock the bareness of Pler' barracks was evident proof that the patronage had been very satisfactory. Captain Wild in closing the sale con- gratulated and thanked all the helpers and workers for their generous assistance. The total proceeds for the festival amounted to about £l3.</p>
        <p>DISGRACEFUL CONDUCT OF A WARRINGTON MIN -A` - NORTHWICH. At the Northwich Police Court, on Monday,before. Mr. John Clough, Edward Burke, of Warrington,- „ was charged with being drunk and disorderly and, - with doing damage to the cell to the amount 01 Gd., oft Saturday night. P.C. Bourne said cora- plaints were made to the police that Burke Wag creating a great disturbance in Whalley-road. Ile went there and advised defendant to go away vrith-, out further trouble. He refused, and became se violent that witness had to take him to the Police Station, where he threatened to kill the first rasa who attempted to put him in the cell. Ile behaved like a madman, and they were obliged to handcuff him. In the cell he screamed and raved and dam- aged the door, &amp;c. Prisoner, against whom there .• was a long list of convictions, was fined 10s. and costs for each offence (.Cl' 143. 2d. in all), or 111.- default a month's imprisonment. "SHEPHERDS PICNIC. A portion of the members who comprise the' Court 240 of the Ancient Order of Shepherds io neyed to New Brighton, on Saturday, that place-. having been selected for the annual picnic. The day was spent in an enjoyable manner, all the :4 places of interest in the locality receiving attention. 4, Tea was partaken of, and the comfort of the portY was in the capable hands of Mr. Llewellyn Davies, The return journey was made soon after ten o'cloct and thanks were tendered to Mr. Davies for the labour he had bestowed in making the outing e. successful. AIARKEI'67 WIDNES, SA.TCRDA.T.-Fresh butter, Is 45 and Os Od r n er ' lb.; fresh eggs,l2 for Is ; fowls, 3s Oct to 03 per couple ; ducks' Os Od to Os per coupfe ; geese, , d per lb. ' • beef. 714 to 95 Per' lb. ; mutton 4d to 65 Per lb. ; pork, 9d to Od per lb. ;_..stea Od to '-/d per lb. ; lamb. Od per lb. ; potatoes per load. 5s 604.... RUNCORN, SATURDAY. --Fresh butter is 3d and Os Od Pc": • lb.; fresh eggs,ll for Is ; fowl, 4s Od to Os Od per couple; duck' s 6s Od per 