        <p>RUNCORN UNION. CONTRACTS FOR PROVISIONS AND SUPPLIES FOR THE HALF-YEAR ENDING MARCH 30Tit. 1895. rrHE GUARDIANS OF Ti-tb POOR OF THE RUNCORN 1. UNION are desirous of receiving TENDERS from par- ties willingto CONTRACT for the supply of FLOUR, OAT- MEAL, GROCERIES, PEAS, CHEESE, SALT BUTTER; cob LIVER and PARAFFIN OIL; QUININE; GOOD BEEF, to consist of vein and brisket pieces, the price to in- clude about 501bs. per week if the Best Butcher's Meat for the Officers. and Sides of MUTTON; BREAD; HOME CURED BACON; POTATOES; WHEAT STRAW; DRAPERY; and CLOTHING; Men's. Women's, Boys' and Girls' SHOES, of various sizes, as per $ .mples to be seen at the Workhouse, Dutton, and Shoe Mending; HOUSE COAL (Colliery to be camel); GOOD DEAL COFFINS, specifying size, and in- cluding plate with name age, and date of oeath, to be de- livered in the Union when and where required; COKE; CARTING at per mile, Funerals at par Funeral. Parties Tendering for the supply of the above-mentioned Goods, willbe required to deliver the same at the Workhouse free of cost to the Guardians, how and when required. 'enders to be accompanied with samples (except Drapery Goods) as far as practicable. The Guardians do not bind themselves to accept the Lowest or any Tender. Persons desirous of tendering, must apply by letter to my I office, in Runcorn, for a printed Form of Tender (and none other can be entertained), where a Schedule of the various articles required and further particulars may be obtained. Sealed Tenders addressed to the Guardians.to be forwarded to the Clerk's Office, Runcorn, on or before Monday, the 17th day of September, 1894, endorsed "Tender for The Tenders will be opened and considered at a Meeting of the Guardians to be held in the Boardroom, Dutton, on the 18th dayof September, 1894, at Two p.m. Parties whose Tenders are accepted must enter into a written Contract, and be prepared to enter into a Bond with two sureties, if requiredby the Guardians. By Order of the Board, JOHN ASHTON, Clink to the Guardians. Runoorn, 4th September, 1894.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON UNlON.—Persons willing to CON- TRACT for the supply of any of the undermentioned articles from Michaelmas to Lady Day next, except where stated for a year. to be delivered free of expense at the Warrington Union Workhouse, or at the Industrial Schools. Pedgete, at such times and in such quantities as the Board of Guardians shall direct, are requested to send in TENDERS. properly sealed, endorsed, and free of expense, addressed to the Clerk to the Guardians, before four o'clock p.m. on Tuesday, the 18th day of September inst. The following are the articles for which tenders are re- quired : Groceries and Provisions Shoes, Clogs and Slippers Beef and Mutton Glazing „ Joints for Officers Brushes Milk for a year Coffins Flour and Irish pin head Meal Beat Oakum Rope for picking Loaves of Bread Best Penmaenmawr Stone Beer and Porter for breaking purposes Straw and Potatoes Leather Drapery, !ie. Eat thenware Cord and Twill Coal, Slack, and Gas Coke Samples of any of the above may be seen on application to the Master of the Workhouse. the Coal and Sleek supplied must be to the satisfaction of the House and Schools Commit- tees. The Pit and quality of Coal must be named in the ' Tender. Forms of tender and full particulars as to the prob- able quantities of each article required may be obtained at the Clerk's Office. The Guardians also i3vlte TENDERS for PRINTING and STATIONERY, from Michaelmas, 1894 to Michaelmas. 1895. All goods under this head must be equal to samples to be seen at the Clerk's Office, where also forms of Tenders, Ac., may be obtained. Goods required from Poor Law Publishers will be obtained through the Stationery contractor at a commission en the nett cost thereof to be named in the Tender. Each tender must be made upon the prescribed forns and properly enclosed and endorsed with the name of the article tendered for. Each article tendered for must be of the size, material, and quality shown at the Workhouse or Schools. No Tenders for other articles will be accepted. Each contractor must be prepared (if required) to enter into security for the due performance of his contract. The Guardians do not bind themselves to accept the lowest or any tender. All contract accounts will be paid quarterly.—By order. JAMES CLOWES SUTTON, Clerk to the Guardians. Bewsey Chambers, Warrington, September 4th, 1894.</p>
