        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 8, 1894.</p>
        <p>STRANGE DROWNING FATALITY ON THE SHIP CANAL. An inquest, was held on Wednesday at the Dog and Dart, Grappenhall, by Mr. Blunt. deputy coroner for Mid-Cheshire, on the body of Mrs. nnie Hanna Atha,a Leeds woman,whose body was found in the Ship Canal early on Monday morn- ing. Mrs Emily Pugh, residing in one of the Ship Canal huts, Brick-yard, Martinscroft, said she was well ac- quainted with the deceased. She last saw her alive on Sunday night, about 10 p.m., when she (witness), her husband, deceased, and the man Preece, with whom de- ceased was living, came out of the Horse and Jockey, Martinscroft. Preece and deceased seemed always to be on good terms, and witness had never heard them having any quarrel. She had never heard deceased threaten to make away with herself. The relatives of the decased asked, through the Coroner, if witness had known of any quarrel between Preece and the deceased, ' and witness admitted that once deceased told her that that Preece had not turned up with his money, and that she could not put up with it and would have to leave him. Joseph Marshall, lockmaster on the Ship Canal at Latclifdrd. said Preece came at twenty minutes to twelve o'clock on Sunday night, and told him his wife had fallen into the canal by the pontoon at Thelwall Ferry. Immediately witness ordered the electric light on, and diligent search was made. After an hour's search no trace was found of the body, and witness sent a lockgateman home with Preece to see if there was any mistake, and if the woman had gone home by another road. On their return the search was resumed, and witness found the body about ten yards from the spot where Preece informed him that he had heard her call out " Joe." The body was about six feet from the bank and in four feet of water. Preece seemed very agitated and put about when he came to witness. Although he had had liquor he was not incapable in any way. Preece told him whilst searching for the body that he had walked ahead at her request, and had only pro- ceeded a short way slowly when he heard her call "Joe." After hearing her call he heard a splash in the water, and immediately went down the bank, and saw a ripple on the water,but no sign of her. After he searched a little he went to the locks. By the Foreman.—Deceased's footprints could be traced down the bank,and looked as if she had run down to the set off. There were no signs of a struggle what- ever. Deceased's hat was found half-way up the em- bankment. Police constable Millington said he found a gold watch and brass chain and a purse with ss. Md. in money on the body. The watch had stopped at twenty minutes past eleven. It appeared to witness that deceased had been walking on the outside of the metals nearest the canal, and in stepping from one to the other she had only got half •a footing on the sleeper, and had slipped and gone straight down the bank. Joseph Preece, after been cautioned by the coroner, elected to give evidence. He was a watchman on the Ship Canal. Deceased had lived with him close on two years. Last Sunday on the. way home, they walked along the canal bank on the cinder footpath, going side by side to the ferryboat. At that point he stepped aside, and deceased walked on in front. He overtook her on the fourfoot on the embankment. Deceased was very fond of walking on the sleepers between the rails. She asked him to step on in front of her, and when he had got about 20 yards he heard her shout out "Joe." He turned round and said, "Come on, Annie." He got no answer, although he called several times, and then he went back. When he heard her shout he did not think there was anything the matter. He heard what sounded like a splash in the water, and went down the bank ; but could not see anything but a ripple. She had not been depressed. They had lived together on the best of terms. They both had had drink at the Horse and Jockey, but neither were the worse of it. In answer to the deceased 's relatives, he said he could not have saved her life ; he would only have been too glad to have done SO. The jury returned a verdict of " Found drowned." RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD. On Tuesday evening the monthly meeting of the Runcorn School. Board was held in the Town Hall, Runcorn under the presidency of Mr. J. Pritchard. There were also present the Revs. Canon A. Mait- land Wood, and Dr. Gastaldi, Dr. McDougall, and Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., and George Moss, clerk. SCHOOL MANAGEMENT COMMITThE. The Clerk read the minutes of the above committee's meeting held on the 28th August. These showed that it had been decided to recommend the purchase of a gas metre for the Mill Brow School at a cost of £2 75.; and that the circular from Lord Templetown respecting thei teaching of temperance in Board schools be allowed to lie on the table. The Inspector's reports on the schools, were also read. They stated that the boys' school was " an excellent school," and earned the highest possible grant in every subject amounting to 203.6 d. per scholar. The drawing awards from the Science and Art Depart- ment Was also " excellent " and earned a grant of 225. 6d. per scholar in average attendance: The girls' schools were said to be in" very good order and does well in many respects." The amount of grant earned was 195.6 d. per scholar. The Mill Brow Infants' School was described in the report as "an excellent school."— The total amount of the grants was £568 19i. 6d. Dr. McDougall moved and Mr. Taylor seconded the adoption of the minutes. and they were accordingly confirmed. Dr. Gastaldi remarked that considering the amount of money they spent on the schools, they ought to have perfect excellence everywhere. There should be effi- ciency both for the Inspector and those who supported the schools. This was not the case, he regretted to say, with the girls' school. Dr. McDougall thought that as the minutes had been passed by the Board the opportunity for discussion had been lost by anyone wishing to distinguish themselves. Members should attend the committee meetings and they would have the necessary opportunities of discus- sing such matters. Canon Wood expressed the opinion that the reports on the whole were very satisfactory. They ought not to expect too much from the schools. Dr. McDougall thought that Dr. Gastaldi could not _Support with facts the contention that many girls schoels got " excellent." Canon Wood concurred with Dr. Gastaldi in saying that the Board ought to have the very best returns, but too much should not be said in a public meeting, as the remarks were likely to be taken as reflections upon the teaching staff. This, he was sure, Dr. Gastaldi had not intended. Dr. Gastaldi replied that he had no such intention. • Canon Wood, continuing, thought that the schools, on the whole, had done extremely well. The boys' and intants' schools had both secured the highest grants ; and with the exception of two subjects this was the case with the girls' school. Dr. Gastaldi said he wished to see the Board's schools at the top of the tree. Canon Wood remarked that it was very creditable to find that the pupil teachers had passed so well. The Chairman said that the Board ought to aim at the attainment of the highest returns. Probably the atten- tion of the teachers would be drawn to this, in order that they might try their 'ut most to achieve this object. STATISTICS. The visitors ieported that the certified accommodation was 3,349. The number on the rolls 3,468. comiSa red with 3,622 in the corresponding period of the previous year, showing 152 decrease. The total average attend- ance was 2,803, against 2;939 for last year. The per- centage of attendance was 81, the same as twelve months ago. This terminated the proceedings.</p>
